window.B79_BANK1X = [
{"id":"d1I55x6","tp":"line","d":["2023-05"],"q":"The graph below shows pollution levels in London between 1600 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Pollution levels in London, 1600–2000 (micrograms per cubic metre)","unit":"µg/m³","x":["1600","1650","1700","1750","1800","1850","1900","1950","2000"],"series":[{"n":"Sulphur dioxide","v":[200,400,680,650,780,900,800,350,20]},{"n":"Smoke","v":[60,100,165,180,240,360,420,250,10]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường tại các mốc 50 năm nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the levels of two pollutants, sulphur dioxide and smoke, in London's air between 1600 and 2000, measured in micrograms per cubic metre.\n\nOverall, both pollutants rose for well over two centuries, peaked in the second half of the nineteenth century and then fell steeply to negligible levels. Sulphur dioxide concentrations were at least twice as high as smoke concentrations for most of the period.\n\nIn 1600, sulphur dioxide stood at about 200 micrograms per cubic metre. It more than tripled to 680 by 1700 and, after a slight dip in the mid-eighteenth century, climbed to a peak of around 900 in 1850. Smoke levels, by contrast, increased far more gradually, from approximately 60 micrograms in 1600 to 180 in 1750, before accelerating to a high of 420 by 1900.\n\nFrom the late nineteenth century onwards, both figures declined rapidly, fluctuating sharply during the first half of the twentieth century. Sulphur dioxide fell to 350 micrograms by 1950, while smoke dropped to 250. By 2000, each had fallen to 20 micrograms or less, lower than at any earlier point on the graph.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1c3wU","tp":"line","d":["2023-05"],"q":"The graph below shows the number of overseas visitors who came to the UK for three different purposes between 1989 and 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Overseas visitors to the UK by purpose of visit, 1989–2011 (millions)","unit":"Millions","x":["1989","1991","1994","1996","1999","2001","2004","2006","2009","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Business","v":[6.5,6.5,8,9,8,7,6.5,7,8,9]},{"n":"Holiday","v":[3.5,4,4.3,4.5,5,5.5,6,6.5,7,6]},{"n":"Meeting friends and relatives","v":[5,4,4.5,6,6.5,6.5,7,7.5,8,7]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; tên nước được ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu và mốc năm là xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many overseas visitors came to the UK for business, for holidays and to meet friends and relatives between 1989 and 2011.\n\nOverall, visitor numbers in all three categories were higher at the end of the period than at the beginning. Business was the most common reason for visiting in almost every year, while holidays were generally the least common, although the gap between the three narrowed.\n\nIn 1989, 6.5 million people visited on business. This figure rose to a peak of 9 million in 1996, after which it fell back to 6.5 million in 2004 and then climbed again, returning to 9 million by 2011. These fluctuations meant that business travel was briefly overtaken in 2004 by visits to friends and relatives.\n\nThe other two categories followed a steadier upward path. Visits to friends and relatives dipped from 5 million to 4 million in 1991 but then doubled to 8 million in 2009, whereas holiday visits grew continuously from 3.5 million to 7 million over the same twenty years. Both figures then fell by 1 million in the final two years.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d187EB2","tp":"line","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The graph shows the number of visitors to four international museums between 1980 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Visitors to four international museums, 1980–2015 (millions)","unit":"Millions","x":["1980","1985","1990","1995","2000","2005","2010","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Louvre","v":[7.8,8,8.8,9,8.4,8.6,8.9,9.5]},{"n":"Vatican Museums","v":[4.8,5.1,5.8,6.8,7.4,8,8.7,9.2]},{"n":"London Science Museum","v":[4.1,4.1,3.2,3,2.9,2.6,2.5,2.2]},{"n":"Shenzhen Museum","v":[3.8,3.6,3.3,3.2,3,3.4,3.6,3.8]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many people visited four museums, the Louvre, the Vatican Museums, the London Science Museum and the Shenzhen Museum, between 1980 and 2015.\n\nOverall, the Louvre attracted the most visitors throughout, although the Vatican Museums grew far faster and had almost caught up by 2015. The other two museums received far fewer visitors, and the London Science Museum was the only one whose attendance fell.\n\nIn 1980, the Louvre welcomed 7.8 million visitors, compared with 4.8 million at the Vatican Museums. The Louvre's figure rose to 9 million in 1995, dipped slightly and then recovered, reaching 9.5 million in 2015. Attendance at the Vatican Museums, by contrast, increased in every five-year period and almost doubled to 9.2 million.\n\nThe London Science Museum and the Shenzhen Museum both started at about 4 million. Visits to the former declined steadily to 2.2 million, roughly half the original figure, while the latter slipped to 3 million in 2000 before returning to 3.8 million, exactly where it had begun.","lv":"ex","pn":["Louvre","Vatican","Shenzhen"]},
{"id":"d19hhrT","tp":"line","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The graph below shows relative price changes for fresh fruits and vegetables, sugars and sweets, and carbonated drinks between 1978 and 2009. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Relative price changes, 1979–2009 (index)","unit":"Index","x":["1979","1981","1983","1985","1987","1989","1991","1993","1995","1997","1999","2001","2003","2005","2007","2009"],"series":[{"n":"Fresh fruits and vegetables","v":[65,75,100,107,117,143,175,180,212,215,247,250,268,288,315,328]},{"n":"Carbonated drinks","v":[65,80,95,105,110,122,135,143,150,160,164,175,182,190,203,211]},{"n":"Sugar and sweets","v":[65,85,97,105,110,113,128,133,135,147,153,157,160,165,176,197]},{"n":"Consumer-price index","v":[70,82,98,105,108,108,114,116,118,121,118,126,127,130,138,155]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường (theo hình trong bộ đề) nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows relative changes in the prices of fresh fruit and vegetables, sugar and sweets, and carbonated drinks between 1979 and 2009, alongside the general consumer-price index.\n\nOverall, all four measures rose over the thirty years, although the price of fresh fruit and vegetables increased far more than the others. The prices of the two sweetened products also outpaced the consumer-price index, which showed the smallest rise.\n\nIn 1979, all four indices stood at a similar level, between 65 and 70, and they rose together to just over 100 by 1985. From that point they diverged. The index for fresh fruit and vegetables climbed steeply, with minor fluctuations, to 175 in 1991 and 250 in 2001, before ending at 328 in 2009, roughly five times its starting value.\n\nThe other three lines rose much more gently. Carbonated drinks increased steadily to 211 in 2009 and sugar and sweets to 197, each roughly tripling over the period. The consumer-price index, by contrast, remained between 105 and 130 from 1985 until 2005 and finished at only 155, less than half the figure for fresh produce.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1o2PRP","tp":"line","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The chart below gives information about how many European citizens of various age groups frequently went to the gym from 1990 to 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Europeans who frequently went to the gym, by age group, 1990–2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1990","1994","1998","2002","2006","2010"],"series":[{"n":"Aged 44–53","v":[16.5,23,20,22,23.5,25.5]},{"n":"Aged 34–43","v":[15.5,18.5,16,20,25,17.5]},{"n":"Aged 24–33","v":[7,7,8.7,9,9.5,10]},{"n":"Aged 18–23","v":[5,5,6.8,6.5,6.3,6.2]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph illustrates the proportion of Europeans in four age groups who went to the gym regularly between 1990 and 2010.\n\nOverall, gym attendance was considerably more common among the two older groups than among the two younger ones, and the oldest group finished the period with the highest figure. Attendance rose in every age group, although the increase among people in their late teens and early twenties was marginal.\n\nIn 1990, roughly 16% of the oldest group, aged 44 to 53, were regular gym users, slightly more than among those aged 34 to 43. The older group's figure jumped to 23% in 1994, dipped four years later and then climbed steadily, reaching just over 25% in 2010. The second-oldest group followed a similar pattern until 2006, when it peaked at 25% and briefly took the lead, before falling sharply to 17.5%.\n\nBy contrast, the figures for the two youngest groups never exceeded 10%. Attendance among people aged 24 to 33 grew gradually from 7% to that level over the two decades, while the share of 18-to-23-year-olds was the lowest throughout, edging up from 5% to just over 6%.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1yLRh5","tp":"line","d":["2023-02"],"q":"The graph below shows the number of enquiries received by the Tourist Information Office in one city over a six-month period in 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Enquiries received by a Tourist Information Office, January–June 2011","x":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun"],"series":[{"n":"In person","v":[450,600,800,1220,1550,1900]},{"n":"By letter / email","v":[750,700,700,560,350,350]},{"n":"By telephone","v":[900,800,1000,1000,1400,1600]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many enquiries a city's Tourist Information Office received in person, by telephone and by letter or email during the first six months of 2011.\n\nOverall, enquiries made in person and by telephone rose considerably, whereas written enquiries declined. Face-to-face enquiries grew the fastest, moving from the least to the most common method.\n\nIn January, the telephone was the most popular channel, with about 900 enquiries, followed by letters and emails (750) and personal visits (450). In-person enquiries then increased every month, overtaking written enquiries in February and telephone calls in March, and reached 1,900 in June, more than four times the January figure.\n\nTelephone enquiries dipped to 800 in February and stood at around 1,000 in March and April, before climbing to 1,600 by June. By contrast, the number of letters and emails stayed close to 700 until March and then halved, levelling off at 350 in May and June, less than a fifth of the figure for personal visits.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1PN2oh","tp":"line","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of workers from five different European countries that were absent for a day or more due to illness from 1991 to 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Workers absent through illness in five European countries, 1991–2001 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1991","1992","1993","1994","1995","1996","1997","1998","1999","2000","2001"],"series":[{"n":"Netherlands","v":[4.9,5.6,5.3,5.0,4.8,4.0,4.4,4.7,5.0,5.2,5.4]},{"n":"France","v":[5.0,5.0,4.3,3.9,3.5,3.1,3.0,3.0,2.9,2.9,2.9]},{"n":"Sweden","v":[3.0,3.1,3.0,3.1,3.0,3.1,3.5,4.0,4.3,4.4,4.6]},{"n":"UK","v":[2.5,2.4,2.5,2.4,2.5,2.4,2.6,2.4,2.5,2.4,2.6]},{"n":"Germany","v":[1.4,1.6,1.4,1.6,1.5,1.7,1.6,1.8,1.6,1.8,1.5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph compares the proportion of employees in five European countries who took at least one day off work through illness each year from 1991 to 2001.\n\nOverall, absence rates were highest in the Netherlands for almost the entire period and lowest in Germany. While the figures for the UK and Germany were stable, France saw a marked decline and Sweden a marked rise in the second half of the decade.\n\nIn 1991, about 5% of workers in both the Netherlands and France were absent through illness. The Dutch figure peaked at 5.6% the following year, fell to a low of 4% in 1996 and then recovered steadily to 5.4% in 2001. The French rate, by contrast, dropped continuously to 3% by 1997 and stayed just under that level for the rest of the period.\n\nSweden's rate hovered around 3% until 1996, after which it climbed every year to reach 4.6%, overtaking France and finishing second only to the Netherlands. The UK and Germany recorded the lowest figures throughout, fluctuating narrowly around 2.5% and 1.5% respectively.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1oChaG","tp":"line","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The graph below shows the number of overseas visitors to three different areas in a European country between 1987 and 2007. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Overseas visitors to three areas in a European country, 1987–2007 (thousands)","unit":"Thousands","x":["1987","1992","1997","2002","2007"],"series":[{"n":"The coast","v":[40,35,48,62,75]},{"n":"The mountains","v":[20,25,30,31,34]},{"n":"The lakes","v":[10,25,40,75,50]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many overseas tourists visited three areas of one European country, the coast, the mountains and the lakes, between 1987 and 2007.\n\nOverall, all three areas attracted more foreign visitors at the end of the period than at the beginning. The coast was the most popular destination in most years, whereas the lakes saw by far the greatest growth, despite a sharp fall after 2002.\n\nIn 1987, the coast received 40,000 overseas visitors, twice as many as the mountains and four times as many as the lakes. After dipping slightly to 35,000 in 1992, the coastal figure climbed steadily, reaching 75,000 in 2007. Visitor numbers in the mountains also rose, although far more slowly, from 20,000 to just under 35,000.\n\nThe lakes followed a very different pattern. Having started at only 10,000, the number of visitors there matched the mountain figure in 1992 and then surged to a peak of 75,000 in 2002, briefly overtaking the coast. It subsequently fell by a third to 50,000, which was still five times the original figure.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1ON2EF","tp":"line","d":["2022-12"],"q":"The graph below shows US government spending on research in five fields (health, space, energy, general science and other) between 1980 and 2008. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"US government spending on research, 1980–2008 ($ billions)","unit":"$ billion","x":["1980","1984","1988","1992","1996","2000","2004","2008"],"series":[{"n":"Health","v":[8.7,8,10.5,13.2,15.2,16.8,22,18]},{"n":"Space","v":[6.2,5.1,5.2,5.5,8,8.7,8.9,9.6]},{"n":"Energy","v":[5,5.1,5,5.2,8.5,5.1,5.1,7.8]},{"n":"General science","v":[2.5,2.7,4,3.3,3.9,5,5,5]},{"n":"Other","v":[7.9,5,5.3,6.5,8,8.7,8,5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how much the US government spent on research in five areas, namely health, space, energy, general science and other fields, between 1980 and 2008.\n\nOverall, health received by far the most funding throughout the period and was the only area in which spending grew substantially. Expenditure on the remaining four fields stayed within a much narrower range, although space and general science both finished higher than they had started.\n\nHealth research cost approximately $8.7 billion in 1980. After a slight dip in the early 1980s, the figure climbed steadily to almost $17 billion in 2000 and then surged to a peak of $22 billion in 2004, before falling back to $18 billion, still roughly twice the amount spent on any other field.\n\nSpending on space and on other research followed a similar path until 2000, rising from around $5 billion each in 1984 to nearly $9 billion. Thereafter, space edged up to $9.6 billion, whereas the other category dropped back to $5 billion. Energy stayed at about $5 billion in most years, apart from brief spikes to $8.5 billion in 1996 and almost $8 billion in 2008. General science, the least funded area, doubled from $2.5 billion to $5 billion by 2000 and then levelled off.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d15Yrtp","tp":"line","d":["2022-09"],"q":"The chart below shows the changes that took place in three different areas of crime in Newport city centre from 2003 to 2012. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Number of incidents of three types of crime in Newport city centre, 2003–2012","x":["2003","2004","2005","2006","2007","2008","2009","2010","2011","2012"],"series":[{"n":"Burglary","v":[3400,3700,3100,2600,1600,1200,1400,1300,1500,1300]},{"n":"Car theft","v":[2750,2800,2750,2100,2250,2100,2300,2600,2500,2300]},{"n":"Robbery (theft from the person)","v":[700,800,850,600,550,500,650,700,600,650]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how the number of burglaries, car thefts and robberies in Newport city centre changed between 2003 and 2012.\n\nOverall, burglary fell substantially, while car theft and robbery ended the period only slightly below their starting levels. Consequently, car theft replaced burglary as the most common of the three crimes, and robbery was by far the least common throughout.\n\nIn 2003, there were about 3,400 burglaries, a figure that rose to a peak of 3,700 the following year. Burglary then dropped steeply, falling below car theft in 2007 and reaching a low of 1,200 in 2008, roughly a third of its peak. It fluctuated between 1,300 and 1,500 for the rest of the period.\n\nCar theft, by contrast, was far more stable. It stood at around 2,750 until 2005, fell to 2,100 in 2006 and then recovered to 2,600 in 2010, before easing to 2,300, which was almost twice the burglary figure. Robbery never exceeded 850 incidents a year, peaking at that level in 2005 and fluctuating between 500 and 700 thereafter.","lv":"ex","rc":1,"pn":["Newport"]},
{"id":"d1sfg8W","tp":"line","d":["2022-03","2022-09"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of people accessing news from four sources from 1995, with projections to 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People accessing news from four sources, 1995–2025 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1995","2000","2010","2020","2025"],"series":[{"n":"TV","v":[70,58,60,55,50]},{"n":"Newspaper","v":[57,50,45,32,29]},{"n":"Radio","v":[52,50,43,29,22]},{"n":"Internet","v":[0,2,29,40,56]}],"note":"Số liệu năm 2025 là dự báo. Các mốc năm trên trục ngang không cách đều nhau."},"mo":"The line graph shows the proportion of people who obtained news from television, newspapers, radio and the internet between 1995 and 2020, together with a projection for 2025.\n\nOverall, the three traditional sources all lost part of their audience, whereas the internet grew from nothing to become a major source of news and is projected to overtake television by 2025.\n\nIn 1995, television was the most popular source, used by 70% of people, followed by newspapers (57%) and radio (52%). Television's share fell to 58% in 2000 but declined only slightly afterwards, standing at 55% in 2020, and it is projected to reach 50% five years later. Newspapers and radio lost ground more quickly: both dropped to around 30% in 2020, and they are predicted to decline further, to 29% and 22% respectively.\n\nThe internet, by contrast, was not used for news at all in 1995 and by only 2% of people in 2000. Its share then rose sharply to 29% in 2010 and 40% in 2020, overtaking both newspapers and radio, and it is forecast to climb to 56%, the highest figure of any source.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1t6aNA","tp":"line","d":["2022-05"],"q":"The graph below shows the average monthly salary in Japan and the prices of black-and-white and colour televisions between 1953 and 1983. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Average monthly salary and television prices in Japan, 1953–1983 (thousand yen)","unit":"Thousand yen","x":["1953","1960","1963","1973","1983"],"series":[{"n":"Price of colour TV","v":[null,100,91,61,47]},{"n":"Price of black-and-white TV","v":[109,48,38,26,20]},{"n":"Salary","v":[18,21,22.5,29.5,66]}],"note":"Đường giá TV màu bắt đầu từ khoảng năm 1960. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ; các mốc năm không cách đều."},"mo":"The line graph shows the prices of black-and-white and colour televisions in Japan, together with the average monthly salary, between 1953 and 1983, all measured in thousands of yen.\n\nOverall, both types of television became far cheaper over the period, whereas salaries rose, most rapidly in the final decade. By 1983, the average monthly salary exceeded the price of either kind of set, having been well below both prices in earlier years.\n\nIn 1953, a black-and-white television cost 109,000 yen, roughly six times the monthly salary of 18,000 yen. Its price fell steeply to around 40,000 yen by the early 1960s and then declined more gradually, reaching 20,000 yen in 1983, less than a fifth of the original figure.\n\nColour televisions first appear on the graph in 1960 at 100,000 yen, more than twice the price of a black-and-white set at that time. Their price also fell, to 61,000 yen in 1973 and 47,000 yen a decade later. Salaries, by contrast, grew slowly to about 30,000 yen in 1973, overtaking the price of a black-and-white set, and then more than doubled to 66,000 yen, well above the price of a colour television.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1SynOg","tp":"line","d":["2022-04"],"q":"The graph below shows global demand for four types of textile fibre (polyester, cotton, cellulosic and wool) between 1980 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Global demand for textile fibres, 1980–2015 (million tonnes)","unit":"million tonnes","x":["1980","1985","1990","1995","2000","2005","2010","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Polyester","v":[5,7,11,20,32,48,58,68]},{"n":"Cotton","v":[13,18,18,20,25,26,28,32]},{"n":"Cellulosic","v":[3,3,2.5,2,3,5.5,7.5,9.5]},{"n":"Wool","v":[2,3,3.5,4.5,4,2,1.5,1.5]}],"note":"Hình đề không ghi đơn vị; đơn vị triệu tấn được ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows global demand for four types of textile fibre, polyester, cotton, cellulosic and wool, between 1980 and 2015, measured in millions of tonnes.\n\nOverall, demand for polyester grew more than tenfold and overtook cotton to become by far the most widely used fibre. Cotton consumption also rose, though far more slowly, whereas demand for cellulosic fibres and wool remained comparatively small throughout.\n\nIn 1980, cotton was the dominant fibre at 13 million tonnes, more than twice the figure for polyester (5 million). Demand for cotton climbed to 18 million tonnes by 1985, levelled off for several years and then increased gradually to 32 million in 2015. Polyester, by contrast, rose slowly at first but accelerated after 1990, drawing level with cotton at 20 million tonnes in 1995 and climbing to 68 million by the end of the period, more than double the cotton figure.\n\nDemand for the other two fibres never exceeded 10 million tonnes. Cellulosic fibres stayed at 2 to 3 million tonnes until 2000 and then tripled to 9.5 million, while wool edged up to 4.5 million in 1995 before falling back to 1.5 million.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1334c9","tp":"line","d":["2022-03"],"q":"The graph below shows the amount of money invested in four types of investment fund (property, gold, fine art and company shares) in the US between 1988 and 2014. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Investment funds in the US, 1988–2014 ($ million)","unit":"$ million","x":["1988","1990","1992","1994","1996","1998","2000","2002","2004","2006","2008","2010","2012","2014"],"series":[{"n":"Gold","v":[100,110,65,122,128,100,115,122,150,187,302,352,450,380]},{"n":"Fine art","v":[80,87,92,80,92,50,105,65,175,100,150,200,303,333]},{"n":"Company shares","v":[40,50,60,75,157,162,168,172,200,215,223,232,248,252]},{"n":"Property","v":[50,60,50,82,60,68,76,84,108,88,117,150,180,192]}],"note":"Hình đề không ghi đơn vị; đơn vị triệu đô la được ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how much money was invested in four types of fund in the United States, namely property, gold, fine art and company shares, every two years from 1988 to 2014.\n\nOverall, investment in all four categories grew considerably over the period, with the fastest growth occurring after 2006. Gold attracted the most money in the majority of years, while property was generally the least popular option.\n\nIn 1988, gold was the leading investment at $100 million, followed by fine art at $80 million, with property and company shares at $50 million or less. Gold remained between $65 million and roughly $130 million until 2002, then rose steeply to a peak of $450 million in 2012 before slipping to $380 million. Fine art was far more volatile, swinging between $50 million and $175 million up to 2006, after which it more than tripled to reach $333 million.\n\nCompany shares, by contrast, followed a much smoother path. Having more than doubled to almost $160 million in 1996, they stayed ahead of gold for a decade and then climbed gradually to just over $250 million. Property grew the most slowly, finishing at $192 million, almost four times its initial figure.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1tUpq2","tp":"line","d":["2021-11"],"q":"The graph below compares changes in the birth rates of China and the USA between 1920 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Birth rates of China and the USA, 1920–2000 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1920","1925","1930","1935","1940","1945","1950","1955","1960","1965","1970","1975","1980","1985","1990","1995","2000"],"series":[{"n":"China","v":[10.2,11.7,10,15,5,5,20,8.7,6.7,5.7,3.6,2.6,1.6,1.6,2.8,2.8,1.6]},{"n":"USA","v":[11.2,12.3,11,12.7,12,6.5,14.5,13,12.2,12.3,10.3,9.2,8.1,7,7.1,8.2,7]}],"note":"Hình đề không có chú giải; theo đề gốc, đường đạt đỉnh 20% năm 1950 là Trung Quốc. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph compares the birth rates of China and the USA over the eighty years from 1920 to 2000.\n\nOverall, both countries had considerably lower birth rates at the end of the period than at the beginning, despite sharp fluctuations before 1950. The decline was far steeper in China, whose rate was above the American one at its peak but well below it for the whole of the second half of the century.\n\nBetween 1920 and 1935, the two rates were similar, moving between 10% and 15%. Both then fell sharply: China's rate dropped to 5% in 1940 and stayed there for five years, while the American rate dropped to 6.5% in 1945. A sharp recovery followed, with China reaching a peak of 20% in 1950, the highest figure on the graph, and the USA a peak of 14.5%.\n\nAfter 1950, the trends diverged. China's birth rate collapsed to under 9% within five years and continued to fall, reaching a low of 1.6% in 1980 and finishing at the same level. The American rate, by contrast, declined far more gradually, remaining at around 12% until 1965 and ending the period at 7%, more than four times the Chinese figure.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1lOsI6","tp":"line","d":["2021-10","2018-12"],"q":"The graph below shows how people bought music from 2011 to 2018. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of total music sales by method, 2011–2018","unit":"%","x":["2011","2012","2013","2014","2015","2016","2017","2018"],"series":[{"n":"Streams","v":[5,7,10,15,20,28,35,41]},{"n":"Downloads","v":[35,38,41,43,40,37,34,30]},{"n":"CDs purchased","v":[55,49,42,36,32,28,26,25]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how the share of music sales accounted for by streaming, downloads and CDs changed between 2011 and 2018.\n\nOverall, CDs lost their position as the leading format, whereas streaming grew from a negligible share to become the most common way of buying music. Downloads rose at first but then declined, finishing slightly lower than they had started.\n\nIn 2011, CDs represented 55% of music sales, well ahead of downloads at 35%, while streaming made up only 5%. The share of CDs then fell continuously, dropping below that of downloads in 2013 and reaching 25% in 2018, less than half its original level.\n\nDownloads, by contrast, climbed to a peak of 43% in 2014, when they were the best-selling format, before slipping back to 30%. Streaming followed the opposite trajectory to CDs: its share doubled to 10% by 2013 and then accelerated, overtaking CDs in 2016 and downloads a year later. By 2018, it had reached 41%, roughly eight times the 2011 figure.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1qo6pV","tp":"line","d":["2021-07"],"q":"The graph shows information about the number of people in Australia who used Chinese, Arabic, Italian and Greek as their first language between 1986 and 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People in Australia by first language, 1986–2011","x":["1986","1991","1996","2001","2006","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Chinese","v":[110000,157000,205000,255000,302000,350000]},{"n":"Arabic","v":[50000,70000,90000,110000,130000,150000]},{"n":"Italian","v":[150000,140000,130000,118000,107000,107000]},{"n":"Greek","v":[227000,218000,208000,199000,190000,180000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many people in Australia spoke Chinese, Arabic, Italian and Greek as their first language between 1986 and 2011.\n\nOverall, the number of Chinese and Arabic speakers grew strongly, whereas the two European languages lost speakers. Consequently, Chinese rose from third place to become by far the most widely spoken of the four languages.\n\nIn 1986, Greek had the most native speakers, at roughly 227,000, followed by Italian with 150,000. Both figures then declined steadily: Greek fell to 180,000 by 2011, while Italian dropped to 107,000 in 2006 and stayed at that level.\n\nChinese, by contrast, started at 110,000 speakers and increased by about 50,000 every five years, overtaking Italian around 1990 and Greek in 1996, and reached 350,000 in 2011, more than three times its initial figure and almost twice the number of Greek speakers. Arabic grew at a slower but equally steady rate, tripling from 50,000 to 150,000 and overtaking Italian shortly after 2001.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d10ipme","tp":"line","d":["2021-06"],"q":"The graph shows information about the number of marriages in the UK from 1951 to 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Number of marriages in the UK, 1951–2001","x":["1951","1961","1971","1981","1991","2001"],"series":[{"n":"All marriages","v":[400000,395000,450000,425000,400000,375000]},{"n":"First marriages","v":[345000,350000,360000,345000,300000,250000]},{"n":"Remarriages","v":[150000,145000,145000,150000,160000,200000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường (theo hình trong bộ đề) nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the number of marriages in the UK between 1951 and 2001, divided into first marriages and remarriages.\n\nOverall, the total number of marriages was slightly lower at the end of the period than at the beginning, having peaked in 1971. First marriages declined substantially after that year, whereas remarriages became more common.\n\nIn 1951, there were 400,000 marriages, of which roughly 345,000 were first marriages. The total was stable for a decade, rose to a peak of 450,000 in 1971 and then fell steadily, reaching 375,000 in 2001. First marriages followed a similar pattern at a lower level, edging up to 360,000 in 1971 before dropping by almost a third to 250,000 over the next thirty years.\n\nRemarriages, by contrast, stayed at about 150,000 a year until 1981. They then increased, slowly at first and more quickly in the 1990s, to reach 200,000 in 2001. Consequently, the gap between the two types of marriage narrowed from nearly 200,000 to just 50,000.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1mqvQr","tp":"line","d":["2021-04"],"q":"The graph below shows the proportion of total expenditure on five categories in a certain European country between 1960 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Share of total expenditure in a European country, 1960–2000 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1960","1970","1980","1990","2000"],"series":[{"n":"Food","v":[34,30,24,19.5,14]},{"n":"Leisure","v":[19,17,16,13,11]},{"n":"Clothing","v":[10,9,7,6,5]},{"n":"Transport","v":[9,10,12,14,15]},{"n":"Fuel / energy","v":[6,5,4.5,3.8,3]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows what proportion of total spending in one European country went on five categories, food, leisure, clothing, transport and fuel or energy, between 1960 and 2000.\n\nOverall, the share of spending devoted to four of the five categories declined, with food showing by far the steepest fall. Transport was the only category whose share grew, and by the end of the period it had become the largest item.\n\nIn 1960, food made up 34% of expenditure, almost twice the figure for leisure (19%). Both shares then fell steadily: food dropped by roughly five percentage points per decade to 14% in 2000, while leisure declined more gently to 11%. Clothing and fuel followed the same downward path at a lower level, halving from 10% to 5% and from 6% to 3% respectively.\n\nTransport, by contrast, rose in every decade. Starting at 9%, slightly below clothing, it climbed to 12% in 1980 and overtook leisure in the late 1980s, before reaching 15% in 2000, marginally above food.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1G7Ka","tp":"line","d":["2021-03"],"q":"The graph below gives information about the percentage of people living in Australia who were born in Asia, the UK, and other regions. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People living in Australia who were born overseas, 1976–2011 (% of population)","unit":"%","x":["1976","1981","1986","1991","1996","2001","2006","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Total born overseas","v":[23,22.5,22,21.5,21,20.5,22.5,25]},{"n":"Born in the UK","v":[14,12,10,9,8,7,6,5]},{"n":"Born in other regions","v":[11,9,8,8.5,10,12,14,13]},{"n":"Born in Asia","v":[3,4,5,6,7,9,11.5,15]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows what percentage of Australia's population was born overseas, in Asia, the UK and other regions, between 1976 and 2011.\n\nOverall, the share of residents born in the UK fell steadily, whereas the share born in Asia rose throughout the period and eventually became the largest of the three. The total proportion of overseas-born residents declined slightly for twenty-five years before climbing to its highest level at the end.\n\nIn 1976, 14% of people living in Australia had been born in the UK, compared with only 3% in Asia. The two figures then moved in opposite directions: the UK-born share dropped in every five-year period, reaching 5% in 2011, while the Asian-born share grew gradually to 7% in 1996, overtook the UK figure shortly afterwards and accelerated to 15%.\n\nThe proportion born in other regions dipped from 11% to 8% in 1986, recovered to a peak of 14% in 2006 and ended at 13%. Taken together, these changes meant that the overall figure slipped from 23% to just over 20% in 2001, after which it rose sharply to 25%, or one in four residents.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1NzpKU","tp":"line","d":["2020-04"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of people of different age groups who went to the cinema once a month or more in one European country from 2000 to 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People who went to the cinema once a month or more, by age group, 2000–2011 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2000","2002","2004","2006","2008","2010","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Aged 15–24","v":[15,25,33,34,37,52,52]},{"n":"Aged 7–14","v":[10,12,17,20,24,38,30]},{"n":"Aged 25–35","v":[3,7,11,17,18,32,30]},{"n":"Aged 35 and over","v":[1,1,3,4,6,10,13]}],"note":"Đường gốc dao động theo từng năm; bảng chỉ ghi các mốc chính. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of people in four age groups who went to the cinema at least once a month in one European country between 2000 and 2011.\n\nOverall, regular cinema attendance rose in every age group over the period. Young adults were the most frequent cinema-goers throughout, while the oldest group was by far the least likely to attend.\n\nIn 2000, 15% of people aged 15 to 24 went to the cinema monthly. Despite sharp fluctuations in the middle of the period, this figure more than tripled, standing at 52% in 2011. Children aged 7 to 14 formed the second most active group, with attendance rising from 10% to a peak of 38% in 2010, before slipping to 30%.\n\nThe 25–35 group started from a far lower base of 3%, but its figure grew faster than any other, reaching the same level as the children's group by the end of the period. By contrast, attendance among the oldest group stayed below 10% until 2010 and finished at just 13%, a quarter of the figure for young adults.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d15FYhB","tp":"line","d":["2020-03"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of people in different age groups in one city who attended music concerts between 2010 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"People who attended music concerts in one city, by age group, 2010–2015 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2010","2011","2012","2013","2014","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Aged 16–24","v":[40,55,59,60,63,70]},{"n":"Aged 25–44","v":[52,40,50,52,52,53]},{"n":"Aged 45–54","v":[40,32,30,32,42,48]},{"n":"Aged 55–64","v":[24,28,28,28,23,22]},{"n":"Aged 75+","v":[10,21,12,11,13,21]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of people in five age groups in one city who attended music concerts between 2010 and 2015.\n\nOverall, concert attendance was far more common among younger and middle-aged residents than among older ones. The youngest group recorded the strongest growth and became the most frequent concert-goers, whereas the figures for most other groups ended close to where they had started.\n\nIn 2010, people aged 25 to 44 had the highest attendance at 52%, while the youngest group (16 to 24) and the middle-aged group (45 to 54) both stood at 40%. The youngest group's figure then jumped sharply in 2011 and continued to rise, reaching 70% in 2015. Attendance in the second-youngest group dipped at first but recovered to its original level, and the middle-aged group fell to 30% before climbing to 48%.\n\nThe two oldest groups went to concerts far less often. Roughly a quarter of people aged 55 to 64 attended each year, with little variation, while the figure for those aged 75 and over fluctuated between 10% and 21%, finishing level with the group below it.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1sU1fD","tp":"line","d":["2019-07"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of Australian exports to four countries from 1990 to 2012. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of Australian exports to four countries, 1990–2012","unit":"%","x":["1990","1995","2000","2005","2010","2012"],"series":[{"n":"Japan","v":[26,20,18,18,17,17]},{"n":"US","v":[11,8,11,8,5,7]},{"n":"China","v":[2,3,5,11,25,28]},{"n":"India","v":[1,1,1,5,7,5]}],"note":"Các mốc năm trên trục ngang không cách đều nhau."},"mo":"The line graph shows what percentage of Australia's exports went to four countries, Japan, the US, China and India, between 1990 and 2012.\n\nOverall, China's share of Australian exports grew more than tenfold, making it the largest market by the end of the period, whereas the shares going to Japan and the US declined. India's share also rose but stayed relatively small.\n\nIn 1990, Japan was by far the leading destination, taking 26% of exports, more than twice the figure for the US (11%). Japan's share fell to 18% by 2000 and then levelled off at about 17%. The proportion sent to the US fluctuated between 8% and 11% until 2005, dropped to a low of 5% in 2010 and recovered slightly to 7%.\n\nChina, by contrast, received only 2% of Australian exports in 1990. Its share rose slowly to 5% in 2000, doubled over the next five years and then surged to 25% in 2010, overtaking Japan, before reaching 28% in 2012. India accounted for just 1% until 2000, after which its share climbed to a peak of 7% in 2010 and ended at 5%.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1ELVQa","tp":"line","d":["2019-05"],"q":"The graph below gives information about car ownership in the UK from 1975 to 2005. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Households with regular use of a car, Great Britain, 1975–2005 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1975","1985","1995","2005"],"series":[{"n":"1 car","v":[42,38,39.5,41]},{"n":"0 car","v":[30,23,19,18]},{"n":"2 cars","v":[7.5,12.5,15,17]},{"n":"3 or more cars","v":[2.5,4,6,7.5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of households in Great Britain with no car, one car, two cars and three or more cars between 1975 and 2005.\n\nOverall, one-car households formed the largest group throughout, and their share changed little. The proportion of households without a car fell markedly, whereas multiple-car ownership became steadily more common.\n\nIn 1975, 42% of households had regular use of one car. This figure dipped to 38% in 1985 and then recovered gradually, ending the period at 41%. Households with no car made up 30% of the total at the start, but their share dropped to 23% within a decade and continued to decline more slowly, reaching 18% in 2005.\n\nBy contrast, the figures for households owning more than one car rose in every decade. The share with two cars more than doubled from 7.5% to 17%, almost matching the no-car figure by 2005, while households with three or more cars tripled from 2.5% to 7.5%, although they were still by far the smallest group.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1kDyka","tp":"line","d":["2018-11"],"q":"The graph below shows UK acid rain emissions, measured in millions of tonnes, from four different sectors between 1990 and 2007. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"UK acid rain emissions by sector, 1990–2007 (million tonnes)","unit":"Million tonnes","x":["1990","1993","1996","1999","2002","2005","2007"],"series":[{"n":"Electricity, gas and water supply","v":[3.3,2.5,1.7,1.0,0.9,0.7,0.5]},{"n":"Other industries","v":[2.1,2.1,1.7,1.5,1.3,1.2,1.2]},{"n":"Transport and communication","v":[0.7,0.8,0.8,0.7,0.9,1.0,0.7]},{"n":"Domestic","v":[0.6,0.6,0.5,0.4,0.3,0.2,0.2]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề gốc. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ; các mốc năm không cách đều."},"mo":"The line graph shows how much acid rain gas was emitted by four sectors in the UK between 1990 and 2007, measured in millions of tonnes.\n\nOverall, total emissions fell considerably over the period. The decline was steepest in the electricity, gas and water supply sector, which went from being by far the largest source to one of the smallest, whereas emissions from transport and communication changed very little.\n\nIn 1990, electricity, gas and water supply produced 3.3 million tonnes, compared with 2.1 million from other industries. Emissions from the utilities sector then dropped rapidly, falling level with those of other industries in 1996 and reaching 1 million tonnes in 1999, before declining more slowly to 0.5 million in 2007, less than a sixth of the initial figure. Other industries also reduced their output, although more gradually, to 1.2 million tonnes.\n\nTransport and communication, by contrast, fluctuated between 0.7 and 1 million tonnes throughout and finished at its starting level. Domestic emissions were the lowest in every year, shrinking from 0.6 to 0.2 million tonnes.","lv":"pre","rc":1},
{"id":"d1g6K","tp":"line","d":["2018-01"],"q":"The line graph below shows the percentage of tourists to Scotland who visited four different attractions between 1980 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of tourists to Scotland who visited different attractions, 1980–2010","unit":"%","x":["1980","1985","1990","1995","2000","2005","2010"],"series":[{"n":"Aquarium","v":[20,35,20,23,20,15,9]},{"n":"Castle","v":[23,27,40,45,35,34,33]},{"n":"Zoo","v":[10,15,9,12,10,15,20]},{"n":"Festival","v":[30,27,25,24,23,20,18]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of tourists to Scotland who visited four attractions, the aquarium, the castle, the zoo and the festival, between 1980 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the castle became the most visited attraction and the zoo also gained popularity, whereas the aquarium and the festival attracted a smaller share of tourists at the end of the period than at the beginning.\n\nIn 1980, the festival was the leading attraction, drawing 30% of tourists, followed by the castle (23%) and the aquarium (20%). The castle's share then rose sharply to a peak of 45% in 1995, before settling at around a third of visitors from 2000 onwards. The festival, by contrast, declined gradually in every five-year period and finished at 18%.\n\nThe aquarium was the most volatile attraction. Its share jumped to 35% in 1985, making it briefly the most popular site, but then fell back and eventually dropped to just 9%, the lowest figure on the graph. The zoo, which had started at 10%, fluctuated until 2000 and then doubled to 20%, overtaking both the aquarium and the festival.","lv":"pre","rc":1},
{"id":"d1KvGNw","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below shows average carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions per person in the United Kingdom, Sweden, Italy and Portugal between 1967 and 2007. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Average carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions per person, 1967–2007 (metric tonnes)","unit":"tonnes","x":["1967","1977","1987","1997","2007"],"series":[{"n":"United Kingdom","v":[10.8,10.7,10.0,9.5,8.8]},{"n":"Sweden","v":[8.7,10.3,7.1,6.2,5.5]},{"n":"Italy","v":[4.3,6.3,6.7,7.7,7.7]},{"n":"Portugal","v":[1.2,2.3,3.3,5.3,5.4]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph compares average carbon dioxide emissions per person in four European countries, the United Kingdom, Sweden, Italy and Portugal, at ten-year intervals between 1967 and 2007.\n\nOverall, emissions per person fell in the UK and Sweden, whereas they rose in Italy and Portugal, so that the gap between the four countries narrowed considerably. Despite its decline, the UK remained the highest emitter throughout the period.\n\nIn 1967, the average Briton produced almost 11 tonnes of carbon dioxide, a figure that decreased gradually to 8.8 tonnes by 2007. Sweden's emissions initially climbed from 8.7 tonnes to a peak of 10.3 tonnes in 1977, almost matching the UK, but then dropped sharply to about 7 tonnes a decade later and continued to fall, reaching 5.5 tonnes in 2007.\n\nThe two southern European countries started from far lower levels. Italy's figure rose from 4.3 tonnes to 7.7 tonnes in 1997, overtaking Sweden's in the late 1980s, and then remained unchanged. Portugal recorded the lowest emissions in every year, although its figure increased more than fourfold, from just over 1 tonne in 1967 to 5.4 tonnes in 2007, by which time it was virtually level with Sweden's.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 11 · Test 3"},
{"id":"d1xFWiX","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below shows the number of tourists visiting a particular Caribbean island between 2010 and 2017. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Number of tourists visiting a Caribbean island, 2010–2017 (millions of visitors)","unit":"Millions","x":["2010","2011","2012","2013","2014","2015","2016","2017"],"series":[{"n":"Visitors staying on cruise ships","v":[0.25,0.5,0.25,0.5,1,1.25,1.5,2]},{"n":"Visitors staying on island","v":[0.75,0.75,1.25,1.5,1.5,1.5,1.25,1.5]},{"n":"Total","v":[1,1.25,1.5,2,2.5,2.75,2.75,3.5]}]},"mo":"The line graph shows how many tourists visited a particular Caribbean island each year from 2010 to 2017, distinguishing between those who stayed on the island and those who stayed on cruise ships.\n\nOverall, the total number of visitors rose more than threefold over the period. Most of this growth came from cruise passengers, who went from being a small minority to outnumbering visitors staying on the island.\n\nIn 2010, the island received 1 million tourists, three-quarters of whom stayed on the island itself. The number of island-based visitors doubled to 1.5 million by 2013 and then changed little, apart from a slight dip to 1.25 million in 2016.\n\nCruise-ship visitors, by contrast, fluctuated between 0.25 and 0.5 million until 2013, after which their number rose every year, reaching 2 million in 2017, eight times the initial figure. Having overtaken island stays in 2016, they made up well over half of all tourists in the final year. Consequently, the total climbed steadily to 2.75 million in 2015, levelled off for a year and then jumped to 3.5 million.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 15 · Test 2","pn":["Caribbean"]},
{"id":"d1vl3IV","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below shows the number of shops that closed and the number of new shops that opened in one country between 2011 and 2018. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Number of shop closures and openings, 2011–2018","x":["2011","2012","2013","2014","2015","2016","2017","2018"],"series":[{"n":"Closures","v":[6400,5900,7200,6500,600,5200,5000,5200]},{"n":"Openings","v":[8500,3900,5000,6200,4000,4000,4200,3000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows how many shops closed and how many new shops opened each year in one country from 2011 to 2018.\n\nOverall, both closures and openings were lower at the end of the period than at the beginning, although neither followed a steady trend. Closures outnumbered openings in every year except 2011 and 2015.\n\nIn 2011, 8,500 new shops opened, well above the 6,400 that closed. Openings then more than halved to 3,900 in 2012, whereas closures fell only slightly before rising to a peak of 7,200 in 2013. By 2014 the two figures had almost converged, at 6,500 closures and 6,200 openings.\n\nThe most striking change came in 2015, when closures collapsed to just 600, while openings dropped to 4,000. This fall proved temporary: closures rebounded to 5,200 in 2016 and stayed at around 5,000 for the remaining years. Openings, by contrast, held steady at roughly 4,000 until 2017 and then declined to 3,000 in 2018, the lowest figure recorded and less than half the number seen at the start of the period.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 17 · Test 4"},
{"id":"d19TmDG","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below gives information about the percentage of the population in four Asian countries living in cities from 1970 to 2020, with predictions for 2030 and 2040. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of the population living in cities, 1970–2040","unit":"%","x":["1970","1980","1990","2000","2010","2020","2030","2040"],"series":[{"n":"Philippines","v":[32,34,48,45,42,45,50,56]},{"n":"Malaysia","v":[30,40,45,60,70,75,80,83]},{"n":"Thailand","v":[18,23,30,30,32,33,40,50]},{"n":"Indonesia","v":[14,17,25,30,43,52,60,64]}],"note":"Số liệu năm 2030 và 2040 là dự báo. Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ đường nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the percentage of people living in cities in four Asian countries, the Philippines, Malaysia, Thailand and Indonesia, from 1970 to 2020, with predictions for 2030 and 2040.\n\nOverall, the urban share of the population grew in all four countries and is projected to keep growing. Malaysia became the most urbanised country, whereas Indonesia saw the fastest growth, rising from last place to second.\n\nIn 1970, about 30% of people in Malaysia and the Philippines lived in cities, roughly twice the proportion in Thailand (18%) and Indonesia (14%). Malaysia's figure then climbed rapidly to 75% in 2020 and is forecast to exceed 80% by 2040. The Philippines, by contrast, peaked at 48% in 1990, slipped back to 42% in 2010 and is predicted to recover to 56%.\n\nIndonesia's urban population share more than tripled to 52% in 2020, overtaking both Thailand and the Philippines, and is set to reach 64% by 2040. Thailand grew more slowly, staying at just over 30% between 1990 and 2020, although a sharper rise to 50% is anticipated in the final two decades.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 18 · Test 1"},
{"id":"d1u8Swy","tp":"line","d":[],"q":"The graph below shows the average monthly change in the prices of three metals during 2014. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"line","title":"Average monthly change in prices of copper, nickel and zinc, 2014 (% change compared with previous month)","unit":"% change","x":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"],"series":[{"n":"Copper","v":[2,1,0.5,0.5,-0.5,-0.5,1,1,1,0.5,0.5,1.5]},{"n":"Nickel","v":[6,4,1,1,0.5,-3,-1,-1,-1,-2,1,1]},{"n":"Zinc","v":[1,3,2,1.5,1,-1,-0.5,-0.5,-0.5,-1,1.5,2]}]},"mo":"The line graph shows the average monthly percentage change in the prices of three metals, copper, nickel and zinc, over the course of 2014.\n\nOverall, the prices of all three metals rose month on month at the start and end of the year, while nickel and zinc fell in price during the middle months. Nickel was by far the most volatile of the three, whereas copper showed the least variation.\n\nIn January, the price of nickel rose by 6%, three times the increase for copper (2%) and six times that for zinc (1%). The monthly rise for nickel then shrank rapidly to 1% in March, and in June its price actually fell by 3%, the largest drop on the graph. Nickel prices continued to fall by 1 to 2% a month until October, before rising by 1% in each of the last two months.\n\nZinc followed a similar but gentler pattern, with gains of up to 3% early in the year, small monthly losses from June to October and gains of 1.5 to 2% thereafter. Copper prices, by contrast, rose in ten of the twelve months, though never by more than 2%, and fell only slightly in May and June.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 18 · Test 4"},
{"id":"d1072VW","tp":"bar","d":["2023-07"],"q":"The charts below show the performance of a bus company in terms of punctuality, both actual and target, and the number of complaints made by passengers between 1999 and 2003. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Performance of a bus company, 1999–2003","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Percentage of services arriving on time (actual and target)","unit":"%","min":80,"x":["1999","2000","2001","2002","2003"],"series":[{"n":"Actual","v":[85,82,84.5,84,84.8]},{"n":"Target","v":[86,86,85,85,83.4]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Number of complaints (per thousand passenger journeys)","x":["1999","2000","2001","2002","2003"],"series":[{"n":"Complaints","v":[70,100,88,110,120]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề gốc. Trục dọc biểu đồ trên bắt đầu từ 80%. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The two bar charts show a bus company's performance between 1999 and 2003: the percentage of services that arrived on time compared with the company's target, and the number of complaints received per thousand passenger journeys.\n\nOverall, punctuality stayed below the target in every year except the last, when the target itself had been lowered. Over the same period, the number of complaints rose substantially.\n\nIn 1999, 85% of services arrived on time, just short of the 86% target. Punctuality fell to 82% the following year, four percentage points below target, which was the widest gap recorded. The target was then reduced to 85% for 2001 and 2002, when actual performance was 84.5% and 84% respectively. In 2003, the company finally exceeded its goal, although only after it had been cut to 83.4%, while actual punctuality was 84.8%.\n\nComplaints, by contrast, increased from 70 per thousand journeys in 1999 to 100 in 2000. After a brief improvement to 88 in 2001, the figure climbed again, reaching 120 in 2003, almost twice the original level.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1OhYVx","tp":"bar","d":["2023-07"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of male and female teachers in six different types of educational setting in the UK in 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Male and female teachers in six types of educational setting (%)","unit":"%","x":["Nursery / Pre-school","Primary school","Secondary school","College","Private training institute","University"],"series":[{"n":"Men","v":[5,10,44,50,57,70]},{"n":"Women","v":[95,90,56,50,43,30]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề gốc. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the proportions of male and female teachers in six types of educational institution, from nursery schools to universities, in the UK in 2010.\n\nOverall, women made up the great majority of teachers of young children, whereas men predominated at the highest levels of education. The gender balance was fairly even in secondary schools and colleges.\n\nIn nursery or pre-school settings, 95% of teachers were female and only 5% male, and the picture was similar in primary schools, where women made up 90% of staff. The gap was far narrower at secondary level, with women representing 56% of teachers and men 44%.\n\nColleges were the only institutions in which the two sexes were equally represented. Beyond that level, by contrast, men formed the majority: they accounted for 57% of teachers in private training institutes and 70% of those in universities, which was more than twice the proportion of women (30%). The share of male teachers was therefore fourteen times higher in universities than in nurseries.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1qE2zJ","tp":"bar","d":["2023-07"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of people in the UK eating more than five portions of vegetables and fish per day between 2011 and 2017. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People in the UK eating more than 5 portions of vegetables and fish per day, 2011–2017 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2011","2012","2013","2014","2015","2016","2017"],"series":[{"n":"Women","v":[20,20,20,28,33,36,30]},{"n":"Children","v":[10,14,13,14,15,14,15]},{"n":"Men","v":[17.5,18,19,22,24,23,26]}],"note":"Tiêu đề chép theo hình trong bộ đề. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of women, men and children in the UK who ate more than five portions of vegetables and fish per day in each year from 2011 to 2017.\n\nOverall, women were the most likely to eat this amount throughout, and children the least likely. The figures for both women and men rose over the period, whereas the proportion of children changed little after an initial increase.\n\nBetween 2011 and 2013, exactly a fifth of women met this level of consumption. The figure then climbed rapidly, to 28% in 2014 and a peak of 36% in 2016, before falling back to 30% in the final year. The male figure, by contrast, grew more gradually and steadily, from about 17% in 2011 to 26% in 2017, when the gap between the sexes was only four percentage points.\n\nAmong children, 10% ate more than five portions a day in 2011. This rose to 14% the following year and then stayed between 13% and 15% for the rest of the period, which was roughly half the figure for women in most years.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1MrP1c","tp":"bar","d":["2023-05"],"q":"The charts compare the number of people per household in the UK in 1981 and 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Number of people per household in the UK, 1981 and 2001 (% of households)","unit":"%","x":["1-person","2-person","3-person","4-person","5-person","6-person"],"series":[{"n":"1981","v":[17,31,20,18,8,6]},{"n":"2001","v":[26,34,17,15,6,2]}]},"mo":"The charts compare the proportions of UK households of different sizes, from one person to six people, in 1981 and 2001.\n\nOverall, households became smaller over the twenty years. One- and two-person households made up a larger share of the total in 2001, whereas every category with three or more occupants became less common.\n\nIn 1981, two-person households were the most common type, at 31%, followed by three-person (20%) and four-person households (18%). People living alone accounted for 17%, while households of five and six people represented just 8% and 6% respectively.\n\nBy 2001, the share of single-person households had risen by nine percentage points to 26%, the largest change in any category, and two-person households had edged up to 34%. Together, these two types made up 60% of all households, compared with under half two decades earlier. Three- and four-person households, by contrast, had fallen to 17% and 15%, and the largest households had become rare, with six-person homes shrinking to a third of their former share, at 2%.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d13upRt","tp":"bar","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The chart below shows the expenditure on three categories among different age groups of residents in the UK in 2004. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Expenditure on three categories by age group, UK, 2004 (% of total spending)","unit":"% of total spending","x":["Under 30","31–45","46–60","61–75","76+"],"series":[{"n":"Food and drink","v":[6,13,17,20,23]},{"n":"Restaurant and hotel","v":[14,12,12,2,7]},{"n":"Entertainment","v":[7,8,14,23,13]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the share of total spending that UK residents in five age groups devoted to food and drink, restaurants and hotels, and entertainment in 2004.\n\nOverall, the proportion of spending that went on food and drink increased steadily with age, whereas spending on restaurants and hotels was highest among the young. Entertainment took up the largest share in late middle age and early retirement.\n\nPeople under 30 spent only 6% of their budget on food and drink, less than half the figure for restaurants and hotels (14%), which was by far their largest item of the three. The share for food and drink then rose in every successive group, doubling to 13% among those aged 31 to 45 and reaching 23% among people aged 76 and over, almost four times as much as the youngest group spent.\n\nSpending on restaurants and hotels stayed at around 12% up to the age of 60 but then fell to just 2% in the 61–75 group, before recovering to 7%. Entertainment, by contrast, peaked in that same group at 23%, having climbed from 7% among the youngest adults, and dropped back to 13% among the oldest residents.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1pEP0","tp":"bar","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The chart below gives information about the income and spending (in dollars) of Americans by age range in 2013. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Average income and expenditure of Americans by age range, 2013 ($)","unit":"$","x":["Under 25","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–64","65–74","Over 75"],"series":[{"n":"Income","v":[28000,59000,78000,79000,74000,53000,34000]},{"n":"Expenditure","v":[30000,48000,58000,60000,55000,47000,34000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the average income and expenditure, in dollars, of Americans in seven age groups in 2013.\n\nOverall, both income and spending rose with age until middle age and then declined. Income exceeded expenditure in every group except the youngest and the oldest, and the surplus was largest among people between their mid-thirties and mid-sixties.\n\nAmericans under 25 earned about $28,000 on average but spent $30,000, making them the only group whose outgoings were higher than their income. Income then more than doubled to $59,000 in the next age group and peaked at just under $80,000 in the two groups covering the ages of 35 to 54. Spending also grew, although far less steeply, to a maximum of $60,000, which left a gap of roughly $20,000.\n\nAfter the age of 55, both figures fell. People aged 65 to 74 earned $53,000 and spent $47,000, so the difference had narrowed considerably, while among the over-75s income and expenditure were identical, at $34,000, only slightly above the levels recorded for the youngest group.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1aJaOm","tp":"bar","d":["2023-02"],"q":"The World Health Organization recommends that people should eat five or more portions of fruit and vegetables per day. The bar chart shows the percentage of males and females in the UK by age group who did so in 2006. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People in the UK eating five or more portions of fruit and vegetables a day, by age, 2006 (%)","unit":"%","x":["15–25","26–40","41–55","56–65","66–74","75+"],"series":[{"n":"Males","v":[15,18,18,24,21,19]},{"n":"Females","v":[17,20,20,27,22,19]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of males and females in six age groups in the UK who ate the five or more daily portions of fruit and vegetables recommended by the World Health Organization in 2006.\n\nOverall, only a minority of people in any group met the recommendation. Women were slightly more likely than men to do so at almost every age, and the proportion was highest for both sexes among people in their late fifties and early sixties.\n\nIn the youngest group, aged 15 to 25, just 15% of males and 17% of females ate enough fruit and vegetables, the lowest figures on the chart. The proportions were somewhat higher, and identical, in the next two age groups, at 18% for males and 20% for females.\n\nConsumption peaked among 56-to-65-year-olds, of whom 24% of males and 27% of females met the target, a gap of three percentage points, which was the widest in any group. The figures then declined with age, to 21% and 22% respectively among those aged 66 to 74, while among people aged 75 and over the two sexes were level at 19%.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1LIkPP","tp":"bar","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The bar chart below shows shares of expenditures for five major categories in the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom, and Japan in the year 2009. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Shares of expenditure for five major categories, 2009 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Food","Housing","Transportation","Health care","Clothing"],"series":[{"n":"United States","v":[13,26,18,8,4]},{"n":"Canada","v":[15,21,20,4,6.5]},{"n":"United Kingdom","v":[21,24,15,2,6]},{"n":"Japan","v":[23,22,10,4,4.5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the shares of spending that went on five major categories, food, housing, transportation, health care and clothing, in the United States, Canada, the United Kingdom and Japan in 2009.\n\nOverall, housing, food and transportation absorbed the bulk of expenditure in all four countries, whereas health care and clothing each took less than a tenth. The two North American countries spent relatively more on transportation, while the UK and Japan devoted larger shares to food.\n\nHousing was the largest item in three of the four countries, ranging from 21% in Canada to 26% in the United States. Only in Japan was food the leading category, at 23%, slightly ahead of housing. Food also took 21% of spending in the UK, compared with just 15% in Canada and 13% in the US.\n\nThe pattern was reversed for transportation, which accounted for 20% of Canadian and 18% of American spending but only 10% in Japan, half the Canadian figure. Health care was notably more costly in the US, at 8%, twice the share in Canada and Japan and four times that in the UK. Spending on clothing was low everywhere, between 4% and 6.5%.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1PTT6O","tp":"bar","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The bar chart shows the percentage of households with cars in a European country between 1971 and 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Households with cars in a European country, 1971–2001 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1971","1981","1991","2001"],"series":[{"n":"No car","v":[47,42,30,28]},{"n":"1 car","v":[35,31,47,43]},{"n":"2 or more cars","v":[16,28,21,28]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of households in one European country that had no car, one car, or two or more cars in 1971, 1981, 1991 and 2001.\n\nOverall, car ownership became far more widespread over the thirty years. Far fewer households were without a car by the end, while the shares owning one car and several cars both finished higher than they had started.\n\nIn 1971, almost half of all households (47%) had no car, compared with 35% that had one and only 16% that had two or more. The car-free share declined to 42% in 1981 and then dropped sharply to 30% in 1991, before edging down to 28% in 2001.\n\nOne-car households followed a less regular path, dipping to 31% in 1981, jumping to a peak of 47% a decade later and then slipping to 43%, which nevertheless made them the largest group in the last two years shown. The figure for households with at least two cars, by contrast, rose to 28% in 1981, fell back to 21% in 1991 and returned to 28% in 2001, level with the proportion that had no car at all.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1x9jLA","tp":"bar","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The charts below show the percentage of people who were born in Australia and who were born outside Australia living in cities, towns and rural areas in 1995 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Where people born in and outside Australia lived, 1995 and 2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Born in Australia (1995)","Born outside Australia (1995)","Born in Australia (2010)","Born outside Australia (2010)"],"series":[{"n":"Cities","v":[50,60,65,80]},{"n":"Towns","v":[20,11,15,10]},{"n":"Rural areas","v":[30,40,15,7]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ hình trong bộ đề nên là số xấp xỉ (tổng mỗi nhóm có thể không tròn 100%)."},"mo":"The bar charts compare where people born in Australia and people born overseas lived, in cities, towns or rural areas, in 1995 and 2010.\n\nOverall, cities were home to the largest share of both groups in both years, and this share grew markedly over the period. Living in rural areas, by contrast, became far less common, particularly among those born outside Australia.\n\nIn 1995, half of all Australian-born residents lived in cities, compared with 60% of the overseas-born. Rural areas ranked second for both groups, at 30% and 40% respectively, while towns were the least common place of residence, accounting for a fifth of people born in Australia and only about a tenth of those born elsewhere.\n\nFifteen years later, the proportion of city dwellers had risen to 65% among the Australian-born and to 80% among immigrants. The rural figures, meanwhile, had fallen sharply, halving to 15% for the former group and dropping to just 7% for the latter, roughly a sixth of the earlier level. The share living in towns also declined slightly in both cases, to 15% and 10% respectively.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1xPjd3","tp":"bar","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The graph below shows the hours of teaching per year done by each teacher in four different countries in 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Hours of teaching per year per teacher in four countries, 2001","unit":"Hours","x":["Japan","Spain","Iceland","USA"],"series":[{"n":"Primary","v":[600,650,575,750]},{"n":"Lower secondary","v":[650,750,600,1000]},{"n":"Upper secondary","v":[700,900,925,1175]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the number of hours that primary, lower secondary and upper secondary teachers spent teaching per year in Japan, Spain, Iceland and the USA in 2001.\n\nOverall, teachers in the USA taught for the most hours at every level, while those in Japan generally taught the least. In all four countries, teaching hours increased with the level of education, although the size of the increase differed.\n\nAmerican primary teachers taught for 750 hours a year, compared with 575 to 650 hours in the other three countries. The gap was wider at lower secondary level, where the US figure of 1,000 hours was a third higher than Spain's (750) and far above those of Japan and Iceland, at 650 and 600 respectively. Upper secondary teachers in the USA worked the longest of all, at nearly 1,200 hours.\n\nJapan showed the least variation, with only 100 hours separating its primary and upper secondary teachers. In Iceland, by contrast, upper secondary staff taught for 925 hours, over 300 more than their lower secondary colleagues and slightly more than their Spanish counterparts (900).","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1rIy69","tp":"bar","d":["2022-12"],"q":"The chart below gives information about birth and death rates in Switzerland from 1970 to 2020 according to UN statistics. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Switzerland: births and deaths, 1970–2020 (millions)","unit":"Millions","x":["1970","1980","1990","2000","2010","2020"],"series":[{"n":"Births","v":[1.2,1.2,1.7,2.7,3.0,3.2]},{"n":"Deaths","v":[1.0,1.3,1.5,1.1,1.0,0.8]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột (theo hình trong bộ đề) nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of births and deaths in Switzerland, in millions, at ten-year intervals between 1970 and 2020, according to UN statistics.\n\nOverall, births rose substantially over the period, whereas deaths ended slightly lower than they had started. Consequently, the gap between the two, which was small until 1990, became very wide in the following decades.\n\nIn 1970, there were 1.2 million births and 1 million deaths. The number of births was unchanged ten years later, while deaths rose to 1.3 million, making 1980 the only year in which deaths exceeded births. By 1990, both figures had increased, to 1.7 million births and 1.5 million deaths.\n\nAfter 1990, the two trends diverged. Births jumped to 2.7 million in 2000 and continued to climb, passing 3 million in 2010 and ending at 3.2 million in 2020, more than two and a half times the 1970 level. Deaths, by contrast, fell back to 1.1 million in 2000 and then declined gradually to 0.8 million. Births therefore outnumbered deaths by four to one at the end of the period, compared with a difference of only 0.2 million at the beginning.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1oVp6A","tp":"bar","d":["2022-11"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentages of waste disposed of by four different methods in four cities (Toronto, Madrid, Kuala Lumpur and Amman). Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Waste disposal methods in four cities (% of total waste)","unit":"% of total waste","x":["Toronto","Madrid","Kuala Lumpur","Amman"],"series":[{"n":"Landfill","v":[75,29,15,48]},{"n":"Incineration","v":[11,40,55,38]},{"n":"Recycling","v":[8,23,23,11]},{"n":"Composting","v":[7,9,7,3]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the percentages of waste disposed of by four methods, landfill, incineration, recycling and composting, in Toronto, Madrid, Kuala Lumpur and Amman.\n\nOverall, landfill and incineration are the dominant methods in all four cities, although their relative importance varies widely. Recycling and composting together handle a minority of waste everywhere, and composting ranks last in each city.\n\nToronto relies overwhelmingly on landfill, which takes 75% of its waste, while the other three methods each handle roughly a tenth or less. Landfill is also the leading method in Amman, at 48%, although incineration is not far behind there, at 38%.\n\nIn Madrid and Kuala Lumpur, by contrast, incineration comes first. It is used for 55% of waste in Kuala Lumpur, more than three times the share sent to landfill (15%), and for 40% in Madrid, where landfill takes a further 29%. These two cities also recycle the most, at 23% each, roughly twice the proportion in Amman and almost three times that in Toronto. Composting is used for under 10% of waste in every city and for only 3% in Amman.","lv":"ex","rc":1,"pn":["Toronto","Kuala Lumpur","Amman","Madrid"]},
{"id":"d1agP","tp":"bar","d":["2022-06"],"q":"The charts below show what UK graduate and postgraduate students who did not go into full-time work did after leaving college in 2008. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Destinations of UK graduates and postgraduates (excluding full-time work), 2008","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Graduates","x":["Part-time work","Voluntary work","Further study","Unemployment"],"series":[{"n":"Number of graduates","v":[17735,3500,29665,16235]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Postgraduates","x":["Part-time work","Voluntary work","Further study","Unemployment"],"series":[{"n":"Number of postgraduates","v":[2535,345,2725,1625]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề gốc."},"mo":"The two bar charts show what UK graduates and postgraduates who did not enter full-time work did after completing their courses in 2008.\n\nOverall, further study was the most common destination for both groups, and voluntary work the least common. The main difference was that part-time work was almost as popular as further study among postgraduates, whereas it lagged far behind among graduates.\n\nOf the graduates, 29,665 went on to further study, making this by far the largest group. Part-time work and unemployment accounted for similar numbers, at 17,735 and 16,235 respectively, while only 3,500 took up voluntary work, less than an eighth of the figure for further study.\n\nThe postgraduate figures were roughly ten times smaller, but their ranking was the same. Further study attracted 2,725 postgraduates, only slightly more than the 2,535 who worked part-time, with a further 1,625 unemployed. Just 345 postgraduates did voluntary work, about a tenth of the graduate figure.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1yjlZI","tp":"bar","d":["2022-05"],"q":"The charts below show the percentage of internet users who do three online activities, both overall and by age group. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Online activities of internet users, overall and by age group (%)","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"% of all internet users","unit":"%","x":["Social networking","Paying bills online or online banking","Emailing"],"series":[{"n":"","v":[60,63,95]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"% of internet users in each age group","unit":"%","x":["15–24","25–34","35–44","45–54","55–64","65 or over"],"series":[{"n":"Social networking","v":[95,83,62,42,35,22]},{"n":"Paying bills online or online banking","v":[36,62,58,52,50,40]},{"n":"Emailing","v":[85,88,87,85,87,89]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The charts show the percentage of internet users who use social networking sites, pay bills or bank online and send emails, both overall and by age group.\n\nOverall, emailing is by far the most widespread of the three activities and is equally common at all ages. Social networking, by contrast, is strongly associated with younger users, while online banking is most popular among people in early middle age.\n\nTaking all users together, 95% send emails, compared with just over 60% who bank or pay bills online and a similar proportion who use social networks. Email use varies very little with age, ranging only from 85% to 89% across the six groups.\n\nThe pattern for social networking is very different. It is used by 95% of 15-to-24-year-olds, but the figure is lower in each successive age group, at 62% among those aged 35 to 44 and only 22% among users aged 65 or over. Online banking is least common among the youngest users, at 36%, is at its highest (62%) in the 25–34 group and then becomes gradually less common, with 40% of the oldest group using it.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1HRBH","tp":"bar","d":["2022-04"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of the population in the UK who consumed the recommended daily amount of fruit and vegetables in 2002, 2006 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People in the UK who consumed the recommended daily amount of fruit and vegetables (%)","unit":"%","x":["2002","2006","2010"],"series":[{"n":"Men","v":[22,28,24]},{"n":"Women","v":[25,32,27]},{"n":"Children","v":[11,16,14]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of men, women and children in the UK who ate the recommended daily amount of fruit and vegetables in 2002, 2006 and 2010.\n\nOverall, women were the most likely to meet the recommendation in all three years, while children were by far the least likely. For every group, the figure rose between 2002 and 2006 and then fell back, although it ended above its starting level.\n\nIn 2002, a quarter of women consumed enough fruit and vegetables, compared with 22% of men and only 11% of children, less than half the female figure. Four years later, all three proportions had increased markedly: women reached 32%, the highest figure on the chart, men 28% and children 16%.\n\nBy 2010, however, each figure had declined, with the share of women meeting the target dropping by five percentage points to 27%. That of men fell to 24%, while the proportion of children slipped only slightly, to 14%. Consequently, the gap between adults and children, which had been widest in 2006, narrowed a little by the end of the period.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1k9QC","tp":"bar","d":["2022-02"],"q":"The bar chart shows the percentage of school children learning to play four different musical instruments (violin, guitar, piano, drums) in 2005, 2010 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"School children learning to play four musical instruments, 2005, 2010 and 2015 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2005","2010","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Guitar","v":[11,20,27]},{"n":"Piano","v":[10,17,25]},{"n":"Drums","v":[6,6,8]},{"n":"Violin","v":[8,8,7]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of school children who were learning to play four musical instruments, the guitar, piano, drums and violin, in 2005, 2010 and 2015.\n\nOverall, the guitar and the piano were the most popular instruments in every year, and both attracted a rapidly growing share of pupils. By contrast, the proportions learning the drums and the violin stayed low and changed very little.\n\nIn 2005, 11% of children were learning the guitar and 10% the piano, only slightly more than the 8% who played the violin and the 6% who played the drums. Five years later, the guitar figure had almost doubled to 20%, while the piano had risen to 17%. Both continued to climb, ending at 27% and 25% respectively in 2015, roughly two and a half times their original levels.\n\nThe other two instruments showed no such growth. The share of drum learners was still 6% in 2010 and rose only to 8% in 2015, whereas the violin stayed at 8% before slipping to 7%, which made it the least popular instrument at the end of the period.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1KbpA8","tp":"bar","d":["2022-01"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentages of people using three types of vehicle (car, motorcycle and bicycle) in four countries. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People using three types of vehicle in four countries (%)","unit":"%","x":["Great Britain","USA","Germany","France"],"series":[{"n":"Car","v":[60,60,40,75]},{"n":"Motorcycle","v":[10,30,15,10]},{"n":"Bicycle","v":[29,10,45,15]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình và đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the percentages of people using cars, motorcycles and bicycles in four countries, Great Britain, the USA, Germany and France.\n\nOverall, the car is more widely used than the other two vehicles in every country except Germany, where the bicycle is slightly more popular. Motorcycles are the least common choice in three of the four countries.\n\nCar use is highest in France, at 75%, five times the figure for bicycles there and far above that for motorcycles (10%). In Great Britain and the USA, 60% of people use a car. These two countries differ, however, in their second choice: 29% of Britons cycle, compared with only 10% who ride a motorcycle, whereas in the USA the proportions are reversed, with 30% using a motorcycle and 10% a bicycle.\n\nGermany shows a different pattern. Only 40% of people there use a car, the lowest figure of the four countries, while 45% travel by bicycle, three times the proportion of motorcyclists (15%). The German cycling rate is therefore well above that of Britain and more than four times that of the USA.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1MCcIA","tp":"bar","d":["2022-01"],"q":"The chart below shows the number of people employed in five types of work in one region of Australia in 2001 and 2008. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Number of people employed in five types of work, 2001 and 2008","x":["Sales","Accounting","Computing","Nursing","Farming"],"series":[{"n":"2001","v":[154000,60000,52000,52000,22000]},{"n":"2008","v":[166000,58000,65000,58000,19000]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo đề thí sinh nhớ lại. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the number of people employed in five types of work in one region of Australia in 2001 and 2008.\n\nOverall, sales employed far more people than any other sector in both years, while farming employed the fewest. Employment rose in sales, computing and nursing, whereas it declined slightly in accounting and farming.\n\nIn 2001, about 154,000 people worked in sales, more than twice as many as in accounting (60,000), the second-largest sector. By 2008, the sales workforce had grown to 166,000, widening the gap further, while the number of accountants had slipped to 58,000.\n\nComputing and nursing each employed 52,000 people in 2001. Computing then recorded the strongest growth of any sector, rising by a quarter to 65,000 and overtaking accounting, whereas nursing increased more modestly to 58,000, the same figure as accounting. Farming was by far the smallest of the five, and its workforce shrank from 22,000 to 19,000, roughly a ninth of the number working in sales.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d13UPlP","tp":"bar","d":["2021-12"],"q":"The graph below gives information about the common activities children of different ages in the United States do as part of their bedtime routines. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Common activities for children as part of bedtime routine (% of households)","unit":"% of households","x":["Reading","Brushing teeth","Having a bath","Watching TV","Eating"],"series":[{"n":"1–3 years","v":[65,30,40,16,20]},{"n":"4–5 years","v":[70,45,35,20,10]},{"n":"6–8 years","v":[58,50,32,18,8]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of American households in which children in three age groups do five activities as part of their bedtime routine.\n\nOverall, reading is by far the most common bedtime activity for all three age groups, while watching television and eating are the least common. Brushing teeth becomes more usual as children get older, whereas having a bath and eating become less so.\n\nReading forms part of the routine in 65% of households with children aged 1 to 3, 70% of those with 4-to-5-year-olds and just under 60% of those with children aged 6 to 8. Brushing teeth shows the clearest upward pattern, climbing from 30% for the youngest group to 50% for the oldest, which makes it the second most common activity for school-age children.\n\nHaving a bath, by contrast, is most typical of the youngest children, at 40%, and is down to roughly 32% among the oldest. Eating before bed follows the same pattern, halving from 20% to 10% between the first two age groups. Television is watched in fewer than a fifth of households at any age.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1HBBEd","tp":"bar","d":["2021-12"],"q":"The graph below shows information on employment rates across six countries in 1995 and 2005. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Employment rates across six countries, 1995 and 2005 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Australia","Switzerland","Iceland","UK","New Zealand","USA"],"series":[{"n":"Male, 1995","v":[57,65,72,55,60,59]},{"n":"Male, 2005","v":[69,78,82,73,72,72]},{"n":"Female, 1995","v":[28,55,40,52,25,45]},{"n":"Female, 2005","v":[38,68,52,62,43,62]}],"note":"Trên hình đề thiếu tên nước thứ sáu; theo đề gốc là USA. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the employment rates of men and women in six countries, Australia, Switzerland, Iceland, the UK, New Zealand and the USA, in 1995 and 2005.\n\nOverall, employment rose for both sexes in all six countries over the decade. Men were more likely to be in work than women everywhere and in both years, although the size of this gap varied considerably from country to country.\n\nAmong men, Iceland had the highest rate in both years, at 72% in 1995 and 82% in 2005, followed by Switzerland, where the figure climbed from 65% to 78%. The other four countries started between 55% and 60% and all ended at roughly 70% or slightly more, with the UK recording the largest gain, of 18 percentage points.\n\nFemale employment was lowest in New Zealand and Australia in 1995, at just 25% and 28% respectively, less than half the male rates there. By contrast, more than half of women in Switzerland and the UK were already working. Ten years later, New Zealand's figure had jumped to 43%, while Switzerland still led with 68%, well ahead of the UK and the USA, both at 62%.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1rDUDx","tp":"bar","d":["2021-12"],"q":"The bar chart gives information about the railway system in six cities in Europe. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Railway systems in six European cities: passengers per year (millions) and year of opening","unit":"Million passengers","x":["Paris (opened 1863)","Stockholm (1900)","Lisbon (1927)","Rome (1976)","Madrid (1981)","Berlin (2001)"],"series":[{"n":"Passengers per year (millions)","v":[775,1191,1927,144,45,50]}],"note":"Số liệu và năm khai trương chép theo hình trong bộ đề."},"mo":"The bar chart shows how many passengers use the railway systems of six European cities each year, together with the year in which each system opened.\n\nOverall, the three oldest systems carry vastly more passengers than the three newest ones. Lisbon's railway is by far the busiest, whereas those in Madrid and Berlin are used the least.\n\nThe railways in Paris, Stockholm and Lisbon all opened before 1930. Paris has the oldest system, dating from 1863, and it carries 775 million passengers a year. Stockholm's network, which opened in 1900, is used by 1,191 million people annually, while Lisbon's, opened in 1927, handles 1,927 million, roughly two and a half times the Paris figure.\n\nBy contrast, the three systems built in the last quarter of the twentieth century or later are far smaller in terms of traffic. Rome's railway, opened in 1976, carries 144 million passengers a year, less than a tenth of the Lisbon total. The figures for Madrid (opened in 1981) and Berlin (2001) are lower still, at 45 million and 50 million respectively, giving a combined total of under 100 million.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1remXn","tp":"bar","d":["2021-10"],"q":"The bar chart below provides information about the percentages of students who are proficient in a foreign language in different countries. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Students proficient in a foreign language, by country and gender (%)","unit":"%","x":["China","Romania","Thailand","India","Russia","Vietnam"],"series":[{"n":"Males","v":[17,42,32,56,35,39]},{"n":"Females","v":[32,65,27,68,43,56]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of male and female students who are proficient in a foreign language in six countries, China, Romania, Thailand, India, Russia and Vietnam.\n\nOverall, female students are more likely than male students to be proficient in every country except Thailand. India has the highest proficiency rates for both sexes, whereas China and Thailand have the lowest.\n\nIn India, 68% of female students and 56% of male students have mastered a foreign language. Romania comes second for girls, at 65%, although the figure for boys there is considerably lower, at 42%, which is the widest gender gap of the six countries. Vietnam shows a similar pattern, with 56% of females proficient compared with 39% of males.\n\nThe differences are smaller elsewhere. In Russia, 43% of girls and 35% of boys reach proficiency, while in China the corresponding figures are 32% and just 17%, the lowest male rate on the chart. Thailand is the only country where boys outperform girls, by 32% to 27%, the latter being the lowest figure for female students.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1xXU32","tp":"bar","d":["2021-08"],"q":"The chart below gives information on the percentage of British people giving money to charity by age range for the years 1990 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"British people giving money to charity, by age, 1990 and 2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["18–25","26–35","36–50","51–65","Over 65"],"series":[{"n":"1990","v":[17,30,41,35,31]},{"n":"2010","v":[7,24,35,39,34]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the proportion of British people in five age groups who gave money to charity in 1990 and 2010.\n\nOverall, people in the three younger age groups were less likely to donate in 2010 than in 1990, whereas the two oldest groups became more generous. Consequently, the group with the highest proportion of donors was no longer the middle-aged one but the next oldest.\n\nIn 1990, giving was most common among people aged 36 to 50, 41% of whom donated, followed by the group immediately above them (35%). The youngest adults were the least likely to give, at 17%. Two decades later, the youngest group's figure had more than halved to 7%, while the two groups in the middle had each fallen by six percentage points, to 24% and 35% respectively.\n\nBy contrast, the share of donors among people aged 51 to 65 rose to 39%, the highest figure in 2010, and that for the over-65s climbed from 31% to 34%. The gap between the youngest and oldest groups therefore widened considerably, from 14 to 27 percentage points.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1AH43","tp":"bar","d":["2021-07"],"q":"The graph below shows the percentage of people going to cinemas in one European country on different days. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"People going to cinemas in one European country on different days, 2003, 2005 and 2007 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri","Sat","Sun"],"series":[{"n":"2003","v":[10,20,16,12,30,40,30]},{"n":"2005","v":[14,17,14,9,30,45,34]},{"n":"2007","v":[12,19,9,14,30,43,32]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of people in one European country who went to the cinema on each day of the week in 2003, 2005 and 2007.\n\nOverall, cinemas were far busier from Friday to Sunday than on weekdays, with Saturday the most popular day in all three years. Attendance on most days changed only slightly over the period.\n\nOn Saturdays, 40% of people went to the cinema in 2003, a figure that rose to 45% in 2005 before easing to 43%. Sunday followed a similar pattern at a lower level, moving from 30% to 34% and then 32%, while the Friday figure was exactly 30% in every year.\n\nAttendance from Monday to Thursday was much lower, never exceeding 20%. Tuesday was the most popular of these days, at between 17% and 20%, roughly half the Saturday figure. Monday and Thursday were generally the quietest, each attracting 9 to 14% of people, whereas Wednesday showed the clearest decline, falling from 16% in 2003 to just 9% in 2007, the joint-lowest figure on the chart.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1qvcvs","tp":"bar","d":["2021-05"],"q":"The bar chart shows the number of people who visited different museums in London. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Visitors to four museums in London, June–October","x":["June","July","August","September","October"],"series":[{"n":"History Museum","v":[400000,400000,600000,380000,250000]},{"n":"British Museum","v":[420000,420000,720000,590000,450000]},{"n":"National Museum","v":[215000,185000,320000,200000,180000]},{"n":"Science Museum","v":[440000,400000,400000,500000,300000]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows how many people visited four museums in London, the History Museum, the British Museum, the National Museum and the Science Museum, in each month from June to October.\n\nOverall, the British Museum attracted the most visitors in four of the five months, while the National Museum was consistently the least visited. Attendance at three of the museums peaked in August, and all four received fewer visitors in October than in June.\n\nIn June and July, the History, British and Science Museums each welcomed roughly 400,000 visitors a month, about twice as many as the National Museum. August brought a sharp increase: the British Museum received 720,000 visitors and the History Museum 600,000, while the National Museum recorded its highest figure, at 320,000. The Science Museum, by contrast, stayed at 400,000.\n\nAfter August, numbers generally fell. The British Museum dropped to 590,000 in September and 450,000 in October, although it was still the most popular. The Science Museum peaked a month later than the others, at 500,000 in September, before falling to 300,000. The History Museum saw the steepest decline, ending at 250,000, less than half its August figure.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d17F5KK","tp":"bar","d":["2021-04"],"q":"The bar chart below shows the passenger kilometres travelled by different means of transport in the UK in 1990 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Passenger kilometres by different means of transport, UK, 1990 and 2000 (billions)","unit":"Billion km","x":["Bicycle","Motorbike","Air","Bus","Rail","Total"],"series":[{"n":"1990","v":[5,6,5,44,40,100]},{"n":"2000","v":[4,5,8,47,47,112]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the number of passenger kilometres travelled by five means of transport in the UK in 1990 and 2000, measured in billions.\n\nOverall, total passenger travel increased over the decade. Buses and trains carried the vast majority of traffic in both years, while bicycles, motorbikes and aircraft each made only a small contribution, with air travel the only one of the three to grow.\n\nIn 1990, buses were the most heavily used mode, at 44 billion passenger kilometres, followed by rail at 40 billion. Both figures had risen by 2000, although rail grew faster, climbing by 7 billion to draw level with buses at 47 billion. Together, these two modes were responsible for well over four-fifths of the total, which went up from 100 billion to 112 billion.\n\nThe remaining modes each accounted for fewer than 10 billion kilometres. Travel by bicycle and motorbike declined slightly, from 5 to 4 billion and from 6 to 5 billion respectively, whereas air travel rose from 5 to 8 billion, an increase of more than half, overtaking both of the others.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1I2On","tp":"bar","d":["2021-04"],"q":"The bar chart shows the types of media used to get daily news, by age group, in 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Types of media used to get daily news, by age group, 2011 (%)","unit":"%","x":["10–17","18–29","30–49","50–64","65+"],"series":[{"n":"Social networking","v":[80,80,50,40,25]},{"n":"Microblogging","v":[20,25,18,15,10]},{"n":"Radio","v":[40,92,90,88,85]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of people in five age groups who used social networking sites, microblogging services and radio to get daily news in 2011.\n\nOverall, radio was the main source of daily news for every age group except the youngest, whereas microblogging was the least used medium at all ages. Reliance on social networking declined steadily with age.\n\nAmong 10-to-17-year-olds, social networking was by far the most popular source, used by 80%, twice the proportion who listened to the radio. The same share of 18-to-29-year-olds used social networks, but the figure then dropped to half of those aged 30 to 49, 40% of the 50–64 group and only a quarter of people aged 65 or over.\n\nRadio showed almost the opposite pattern. Having been used by just 40% of the youngest group, it was the choice of over 90% of young adults and stayed above 85% in all the older groups. Microblogging, by contrast, never exceeded 25%, peaking among young adults and falling to 10% among the oldest respondents, less than an eighth of the figure for radio.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1bLjxW","tp":"bar","d":["2021-03"],"q":"The charts below give information about the amount of time children spend with their parents on weekdays and at weekends. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Time children spend with their parents, by age (hours per day)","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Weekdays","unit":"Hours","x":["Age 0–1","Age 2–3","Age 4–5","Age 6–7","Age 8–9"],"series":[{"n":"With Dad","v":[2,1,2.2,1,1.9]},{"n":"With Mom","v":[5.5,5.9,5.1,5.5,5]},{"n":"Both Dad & Mom","v":[1.5,1.8,1.4,2.4,2.4]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Weekends","unit":"Hours","x":["Age 0–1","Age 2–3","Age 4–5","Age 6–7","Age 8–9"],"series":[{"n":"With Dad","v":[0.5,1,1.5,0.6,0.3]},{"n":"With Mom","v":[3.5,3.2,3,3.9,3.7]},{"n":"Both Dad & Mom","v":[4.9,5.9,5.8,5.6,5.4]}]}],"note":"Hình gốc là biểu đồ cột chồng; ở đây vẽ thành cột ghép. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The charts show how many hours a day children of different ages, from birth to nine, spend with their father, with their mother and with both parents together on weekdays and at weekends.\n\nOverall, children spend around nine to ten hours a day with their parents, slightly more at weekends than on weekdays. On weekdays most of this time is spent with the mother alone, whereas at weekends time with both parents together is the largest component.\n\nOn weekdays, children in every age group spend between 5 and 6 hours with their mother, far more than the 1 to 2 hours they spend with their father. Time with both parents is also limited, at about 1.5 hours for the youngest children and 2.4 hours for those aged six and over.\n\nAt weekends, by contrast, children spend roughly 5 to 6 hours with both parents, three or four times the weekday figure. Time with the mother alone is reduced to between 3 and 4 hours, although this is still several times the amount spent with the father, which is highest for 4-to-5-year-olds, at 1.5 hours, and under half an hour for the oldest children.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1hpQmB","tp":"bar","d":["2021-02"],"q":"The bar chart shows the percentage of small, medium and large companies which used social media for business purposes between 2012 and 2016. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Businesses that have a social media presence, 2012–2016 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Small business","Medium business","Large business"],"series":[{"n":"2012","v":[27,32,78]},{"n":"2013","v":[30,48,78]},{"n":"2014","v":[35,49,77]},{"n":"2015","v":[30,30,53]},{"n":"2016","v":[49,53,79]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of small, medium-sized and large businesses that used social media for business purposes each year from 2012 to 2016.\n\nOverall, large companies were far more likely to have a social media presence than smaller ones throughout the period. Usage rose in all three categories over the five years, although each saw a temporary fall in the fourth year.\n\nBetween 2012 and 2014, just under 80% of large businesses used social media, more than twice the proportion of small firms, whose figure climbed gradually from 27% to 35%. Medium-sized companies started only slightly ahead of small ones, at 32%, but jumped to almost half in 2013 and stayed at that level the following year.\n\nIn 2015, usage fell in every category. The decline was steepest among large businesses, whose figure dropped to 53%, while both small and medium-sized firms slipped to 30%. All three groups then recovered strongly in 2016, reaching their highest levels of the period: 79% for large companies, 53% for medium-sized ones and 49% for small ones, which meant that the gap between the largest and smallest firms had narrowed considerably.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d14niMU","tp":"bar","d":["2020-08"],"q":"The bar chart below shows the results of a survey conducted over a three-year period to discover what people who live in London think of the city. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"What are the best things about living in London? (% of respondents)","unit":"%","x":["Range of shops","Job opportunities","Mixture of people","Museums & art galleries","Night life","Transport"],"series":[{"n":"Year 1","v":[45,38,36,22,25,22]},{"n":"Year 2","v":[42,35,36,24,20,20]},{"n":"Year 3","v":[43,35,35,26,22,25]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the results of a survey, carried out in three consecutive years, in which London residents were asked what they considered the best things about living in the city.\n\nOverall, the range of shops was the most frequently chosen advantage in all three years, followed by job opportunities and the mixture of people. Museums and galleries, night life and transport were each selected by far fewer respondents, and opinions changed only slightly from year to year.\n\nIn the first year, 45% of residents named the range of shops, a figure that dipped to 42% and then recovered to 43%. Job opportunities were mentioned by 38% at first and by 35% in the following two years, which was almost identical to the proportion choosing the mixture of people (35 to 36% throughout).\n\nThe other three features attracted between a fifth and a quarter of respondents. Support for museums and art galleries rose steadily, from 22% to 26%, whereas night life fell from 25% to 20% before recovering slightly. Transport was the least popular in the second year, at 20%, but climbed to 25% in the third, just below museums and galleries.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1X4dE","tp":"bar","d":["2020-03"],"q":"The bar chart shows the percentage of young people in higher education in four countries in 2000, 2005 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Young people in higher education in four countries, 2000, 2005 and 2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Country A","Country B","Country C","Country D"],"series":[{"n":"2000","v":[36,40,43,45]},{"n":"2005","v":[48,39,47,50]},{"n":"2010","v":[57,40,47,60]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of young people in higher education in four countries, labelled A to D, in 2000, 2005 and 2010.\n\nOverall, participation in higher education increased in three of the four countries, most strongly in Country A. Country D had the highest rate in every year, while Country B was the only one in which the figure did not rise.\n\nIn 2000, Country D led with 45% of young people in higher education, followed by Country C (43%), Country B (40%) and Country A (36%). Over the next ten years, Country A recorded the fastest growth, climbing by twelve percentage points to 48% in 2005 and reaching 57% in 2010, which moved it from last place to second.\n\nCountry D also rose steadily, to 50% in 2005 and 60% in 2010, the highest figure on the chart. Country C, by contrast, increased only slightly, to 47%, and then levelled off, whereas the proportion in Country B stayed at roughly 40% throughout. Consequently, the gap between the highest and lowest rates widened from nine to twenty percentage points.","lv":"in","pn":["Country"]},
{"id":"d1pF727","tp":"bar","d":["2020-02"],"q":"The bar chart below shows the amount of money invested in each category by five organisations. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Money invested in four categories by five organisations (billion euros)","unit":"Billion euros","x":["Organisation A","Organisation B","Organisation C","Organisation D","Organisation E"],"series":[{"n":"Machinery","v":[1.0,0.9,1.3,0.9,0.8]},{"n":"Building","v":[1.5,1.4,1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"n":"Staff training","v":[1.2,1.9,0.8,0.7,0.9]},{"n":"Research","v":[1.1,1.6,1.3,0.6,0.7]}]},"mo":"The bar chart shows how much money five organisations, labelled A to E, invested in machinery, building, staff training and research, measured in billions of euros.\n\nOverall, Organisation B invested the most in total and Organisations D and E the least. Spending priorities varied considerably: building was the largest or joint-largest item for three of the organisations, whereas B concentrated on staff training and C on machinery and research.\n\nOrganisation B spent 1.9 billion euros on staff training, the highest single figure on the chart, together with 1.6 billion on research and 1.4 billion on building, but only 0.9 billion on machinery. Organisation A, by contrast, put most of its money into building (1.5 billion), with between 1 and 1.2 billion going to each of the other three areas.\n\nOrganisation C invested 1.3 billion in both machinery and research, compared with 1 billion in building and just 0.8 billion in staff training. The two smallest investors, D and E, each spent 1 billion on building, while none of their other investments exceeded 0.9 billion. Research received the least in both cases, at 0.6 and 0.7 billion respectively.","lv":"in","pn":["Organisation"]},
{"id":"d1CiMv9","tp":"bar","d":["2019-08"],"q":"The chart below shows water levels in reservoirs in six cities in Australia in October 2009 and October 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Water levels in reservoirs in six Australian cities, October 2009 and October 2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["Perth","Canberra","Melbourne","Darwin","Sydney","Brisbane"],"series":[{"n":"Oct 2009","v":[47,30,70,50,47,70]},{"n":"Oct 2010","v":[37,40,80,70,47,90]}],"note":"Hình đề không ghi đơn vị; thang 0–100 được hiểu là phần trăm dung tích hồ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the water levels in the reservoirs of six Australian cities in October 2009 and October 2010.\n\nOverall, water levels rose in four of the six cities over the year, with Brisbane and Darwin recording the largest gains. Perth was the only city whose reservoir level fell, while Sydney's did not change.\n\nIn October 2009, the reservoirs of Melbourne and Brisbane were the fullest, both standing at 70%, compared with 50% in Darwin and just under that figure in Perth and Sydney. Canberra had by far the lowest level, at only 30%.\n\nA year later, Brisbane's level had climbed by 20 percentage points to 90%, the highest figure on the chart, and Darwin's had risen by the same amount to 70%. Melbourne and Canberra each gained 10 points, ending at 80% and 40% respectively. By contrast, the level in Perth dropped from 47% to 37%, which left it with the least water of the six cities, whereas Sydney was unchanged at 47%.","lv":"in","pn":["Melbourne","Brisbane","Darwin","Perth","Canberra"]},
{"id":"d1SdVo","tp":"bar","d":["2019-06"],"q":"The chart below shows the number of jobs in tourism-related industries in one UK city between 1989 and 2009. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Jobs in tourism-related industries in one UK city, 1989–2009","unit":"Jobs","x":["1989","1994","1999","2004","2009"],"series":[{"n":"Restaurants","v":[1100,1280,1430,1600,1340]},{"n":"Hotels","v":[1060,1400,1250,1160,1040]},{"n":"Sport and leisure","v":[900,670,1060,870,700]},{"n":"Travel and tours","v":[800,1050,870,670,440]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of jobs in four tourism-related industries, restaurants, hotels, sport and leisure, and travel and tours, in one UK city between 1989 and 2009.\n\nOverall, restaurants were the largest employer in most years and the only sector with more jobs at the end of the period than at the beginning. Employment in travel and tours fell the most, almost halving over the twenty years.\n\nIn 1989, restaurants and hotels each provided roughly 1,100 jobs. Restaurant employment then grew steadily to a peak of 1,600 in 2004, before falling back to about 1,340. Hotels, by contrast, peaked much earlier, at 1,400 jobs in 1994, when they briefly led the four sectors, and then declined in every subsequent period, ending slightly below their starting level.\n\nThe other two industries fluctuated. Sport and leisure dropped from 900 jobs to 670 in 1994, recovered to 1,060 five years later and finished at 700, whereas travel and tours moved in the opposite direction at first, rising from 800 to 1,050, but then shrank continuously to 440 in 2009, the lowest figure on the chart.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1yd2bK","tp":"bar","d":["2019-06"],"q":"The chart shows the number of students in a UK university who completed (on time or late), failed to write, and rewrote their dissertation in 1990, 2000 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Students at a UK university: dissertation outcomes, 1990, 2000 and 2010","unit":"Students","x":["Completed (on time)","Completed (late)","Failed to write","Rewrote"],"series":[{"n":"1990","v":[150,18,42,10]},{"n":"2000","v":[205,27,23,26]},{"n":"2010","v":[300,43,8,23]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of students at a UK university who completed their dissertation on time, completed it late, failed to write it or had to rewrite it in 1990, 2000 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the vast majority of students finished their dissertations on time in all three years, and their number doubled over the period. The number who failed to write a dissertation fell steadily, whereas late completions rose.\n\nIn 1990, 150 students completed on time, a figure that increased to just over 200 in 2000 and 300 in 2010, which was seven times the size of the next largest group. Late completions also grew, although from a much lower base, climbing from 18 to 27 and then to 43.\n\nBy contrast, the number of students who failed to write a dissertation declined sharply, from 42 in 1990 to 23 a decade later and only 8 in 2010, less than a fifth of the original figure. Those who rewrote their work numbered just 10 in 1990, rose to 26 in 2000 and then dipped slightly to 23.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1q3sev","tp":"bar","d":["2019-05"],"q":"The bar chart shows the number of cars per 1,000 people in five countries in Europe in three years, 2005, 2009 and 2015, compared with the European average. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Cars per 1,000 people in five European countries, 2005, 2009 and 2015","unit":"Cars per 1,000 people","x":["Country 1","Country 2","Country 3","Country 4","Country 5","Average of 5"],"series":[{"n":"2005","v":[310,500,650,700,920,700]},{"n":"2009","v":[400,550,600,700,880,680]},{"n":"2015","v":[310,530,560,700,970,780]}],"note":"Hình đề không có chú giải năm; ba cột được hiểu theo thứ tự 2005, 2009, 2015. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of cars per thousand people in five European countries in 2005, 2009 and 2015, together with the average for the five.\n\nOverall, car ownership differed widely between the countries, with the fifth country having roughly three times as many cars per head as the first. Ownership changed relatively little in most countries, although the average was higher at the end of the period than at the beginning.\n\nThe fifth country had the highest level throughout, at about 920 cars per thousand people in 2005, a slight dip to 880 in 2009 and a peak of 970 in 2015. The fourth country was the most stable, recording exactly 700 in all three years, which matched the five-country average in 2005. The average itself slipped slightly to 680 before rising to 780.\n\nThe other three countries were below average in every year. The third saw a steady decline, from 650 to 560, whereas the second fluctuated between 500 and 550. The first country had the lowest level of ownership, starting and ending at around 310 per thousand people, despite a temporary increase to 400 in 2009.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d11FIv6","tp":"bar","d":["2019-04"],"q":"The chart below shows information about fuel used in the transport sector in different countries in Europe, compared to the EU average, in 2009 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Percentage of fuel used in the transport sector in six EU countries, compared with the average, 2009 and 2010","unit":"%","x":["Slovakia","Italy","France","Austria","Spain","Greece","EU average"],"series":[{"n":"2009","v":[8.8,4.5,3.7,5.2,3.8,1.2,6]},{"n":"2010","v":[6.5,5.2,4.3,5.2,3,2,5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of fuel used in the transport sector in six European countries in 2009 and 2010, compared with the EU average.\n\nOverall, Slovakia had by far the highest figure in both years, while Greece had the lowest. The percentage fell in Slovakia and Spain, as did the EU average, whereas it rose in Italy, France and Greece.\n\nIn 2009, Slovakia's figure stood at 8.8%, well above the EU average of 6% and more than seven times the Greek figure of 1.2%. Although it dropped to 6.5% the following year, Slovakia was still the only country above the EU average, which had itself declined to 5%.\n\nAustria recorded 5.2% in both years, and Italy rose from 4.5% to the same level in 2010, so that both slightly exceeded the average that year. France also saw an increase, from 3.7% to 4.3%, while Spain moved in the opposite direction, falling from 3.8% to 3%. Greece's share almost doubled to 2%, although it was still the smallest of the six.","lv":"in","pn":["Slovakia"]},
{"id":"d1Tg3X","tp":"bar","d":["2019-04"],"q":"The chart gives information about the percentage of overweight men and women in Australia from 1980 to 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Percentage of overweight men and women, Australia, 1980–2010","unit":"%","x":["Male","Female"],"series":[{"n":"1980","v":[47,30]},{"n":"1990","v":[51,40]},{"n":"2000","v":[70,50]},{"n":"2010","v":[68,50]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of men and women in Australia who were overweight in 1980, 1990, 2000 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the proportion of overweight people rose substantially for both sexes over the thirty years, although it levelled off in the final decade. Men were more likely than women to be overweight in every year shown.\n\nIn 1980, 47% of Australian men were overweight, compared with 30% of women. The male figure edged up to 51% in 1990 and then jumped to 70% in 2000, the highest level recorded, before easing slightly to 68% in 2010.\n\nThe female figure rose more evenly at first, climbing by ten percentage points in each of the first two decades, from 30% to 40% and then to exactly half in 2000, after which it did not change. The gap between the sexes was therefore narrowest in 1990, at 11 percentage points, and widest in 2000, when it stood at 20 points. By the end of the period, roughly two in three men and one in two women were overweight.","lv":"pre"},
{"id":"d1WYEKw","tp":"bar","d":["2019-02"],"q":"The bar chart shows the average class size in primary schools and lower secondary schools in six countries compared to the world average in 2006. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Average number of pupils per class in primary and lower secondary schools, 2006","unit":"Pupils","x":["South Korea","Japan","UK","Mexico","Denmark","Iceland","World"],"series":[{"n":"Primary","v":[34,29,27,20,23,21,25]},{"n":"Lower secondary","v":[36,34,18,30,21,20,28]}]},"mo":"The bar chart compares the average number of pupils per class in primary and lower secondary schools in six countries with the world average in 2006.\n\nOverall, classes were largest in the two Asian countries and smallest in the two Nordic ones. In most cases lower secondary classes were bigger than primary classes, although the UK was a striking exception.\n\nSouth Korea had the largest classes at both levels, with 34 pupils in primary and 36 in lower secondary schools, well above the world averages of 25 and 28 respectively. Japan came second, at 29 and 34. Mexico's primary classes were the smallest of all, at 20 pupils, but its lower secondary classes were half as large again, at 30.\n\nIn the UK, by contrast, primary classes averaged 27 pupils, slightly more than the global figure, whereas lower secondary classes had only 18, the lowest number on the chart. Denmark and Iceland had similar class sizes at both levels, ranging from 20 to 23 pupils, which was below the world average in every case.","lv":"in","pn":["South Korea","Mexico","Denmark"]},
{"id":"d1nIXx","tp":"bar","d":["2019-01"],"q":"The chart below shows waste collection by a recycling centre from 2011 to 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Waste collected by a recycling centre, 2011–2015 (tons of waste)","unit":"Tons","x":["2011","2012","2013","2014","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Paper","v":[57,50,40,51,70]},{"n":"Glass","v":[48,41,48,46,52]},{"n":"Tins","v":[35,27,34,33,39]},{"n":"Garden","v":[32,15,31,27,35]}]},"mo":"The bar chart shows how many tons of paper, glass, tins and garden waste were collected by a recycling centre each year from 2011 to 2015.\n\nOverall, paper was the most collected material in four of the five years, and garden waste the least in every year. Although collections of all four materials dipped during the period, each ended at its highest level in 2015.\n\nIn 2011, the centre collected 57 tons of paper, 48 tons of glass, 35 tons of tins and 32 tons of garden waste. All four figures fell the following year, with garden waste more than halving to 15 tons. Paper continued to decline, reaching a low of 40 tons in 2013, when it was overtaken by glass, which had returned to 48 tons.\n\nFrom 2013 onwards, the amount of paper rose sharply, climbing to 51 tons in 2014 and 70 tons in 2015, almost twice the figure for tins. Glass, by contrast, stayed within a narrow range and finished at 52 tons, while tins and garden waste recovered from their 2012 lows to 39 and 35 tons respectively.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1DNgRi","tp":"bar","d":["2018-12"],"q":"The bar chart below shows the gross earnings from sales of five types of fiction book between 2006 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Fiction book sales: gross earnings, 2006–2010 ($)","unit":"$","x":["2006","2007","2008","2009","2010"],"series":[{"n":"Young Adult","v":[31000,31000,19000,25000,28000]},{"n":"Classics","v":[17000,31000,22000,26000,10000]},{"n":"Mystery","v":[61000,82000,53000,49000,55000]},{"n":"Romance","v":[83000,114000,71000,78000,86000]},{"n":"Sci-Fi & Fantasy","v":[17000,20000,14000,11000,15000]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the gross earnings from sales of five types of fiction, young adult, classics, mystery, romance and science fiction and fantasy, each year from 2006 to 2010.\n\nOverall, romance was the best-selling genre in every year, followed by mystery, while the other three categories earned far less. Most genres had their best year in 2007, and most earned slightly less in 2010 than they had at the start.\n\nRomance generated about $83,000 in 2006 and rose to a peak of roughly $114,000 the following year, before dropping to $71,000 in 2008 and then recovering to $86,000. Mystery followed a similar pattern at a lower level, climbing from $61,000 to $82,000 in 2007 and ending at $55,000, roughly two-thirds of the romance figure.\n\nEarnings from the other genres never exceeded $31,000. Young adult fiction earned this amount in the first two years, fell to $19,000 in 2008 and finished at $28,000. Classics almost doubled to match it in 2007 but had sunk to just $10,000 by 2010, the lowest figure on the chart, whereas science fiction and fantasy stayed between $11,000 and $20,000 throughout.","lv":"in","rc":1},
{"id":"d1LXu8","tp":"bar","d":["2018-08"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage shares of three sectors (agriculture, industry and services) in one country between 1960 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Shares of agriculture, industry and services in one country, 1960–2000 (%)","unit":"%","x":["1960","1970","1980","1990","2000"],"series":[{"n":"Agriculture","v":[63,54,46,31,14]},{"n":"Industry","v":[16,21,25,25,25]},{"n":"Services","v":[17,24,28,45,63]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi và không ghi đơn vị; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Cô nên đối chiếu với đề gốc."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage shares of agriculture, industry and services in one country at ten-year intervals between 1960 and 2000.\n\nOverall, the country shifted away from agriculture and towards services over the forty years. Agriculture went from being by far the largest sector to the smallest, whereas services moved in the opposite direction, and industry grew modestly before levelling off.\n\nIn 1960, agriculture represented 63% of the total, almost four times the share of either industry (16%) or services (17%). Its share then fell in every decade, to 54% in 1970 and 46% in 1980, before dropping more steeply to 31% in 1990 and just 14% in 2000, less than a quarter of the original figure.\n\nServices, by contrast, expanded throughout the period. Having risen gradually to 28% by 1980, the sector overtook agriculture during the following decade, reaching 45% in 1990 and 63% in 2000, exactly the share that agriculture had held forty years earlier. Industry increased from 16% to 25% between 1960 and 1980 and stayed at that level thereafter.","lv":"pre","rc":1},
{"id":"d1LPgzC","tp":"bar","d":["2018-06"],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of patients in three hospital wards who replied 'very good' about four aspects of their hospital experience. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Patients who replied 'very good' about their hospital experience, by ward (%)","unit":"%","x":["Hospital staff","Waiting time","Quality of treatment","Quality of aftercare"],"series":[{"n":"Holly Ward","v":[87,75,90,88]},{"n":"Maple Ward","v":[55,55,72,52]},{"n":"Oak Ward","v":[68,53,80,70]}],"note":"Ward: phòng hoặc khu dành cho một nhóm bệnh nhân. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of patients in three hospital wards, Holly, Maple and Oak, who rated four aspects of their stay as 'very good'.\n\nOverall, Holly Ward received the highest ratings for every aspect, while Maple Ward was rated lowest for three of the four. In all three wards, quality of treatment was the most highly rated aspect, and waiting time was generally among the least satisfactory.\n\nIn Holly Ward, around nine in ten patients gave the top rating to the quality of treatment (90%), aftercare (88%) and hospital staff (87%). Even its weakest area, waiting time, satisfied 75% of patients, more than in any other ward.\n\nOak Ward came second overall. Its treatment was rated very good by 80% of patients, compared with 70% for aftercare and 68% for staff, whereas only 53% were happy with the waiting time. Maple Ward scored 72% for treatment, but just over half of its patients gave the top rating to the other three aspects, with figures of 55% for both staff and waiting time and 52% for aftercare, which was 36 percentage points below the figure for Holly Ward.","lv":"in","pn":["Holly","Maple","Oak"]},
{"id":"d1mGyh2","tp":"bar","d":["2018-01"],"q":"The chart below shows the favourite leisure activities of teenagers in Canada. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Favourite leisure activities of teenagers in Canada (%)","unit":"%","x":["Sports","Computer games","Music","Shopping"],"series":[{"n":"Boys","v":[27,18,20,35]},{"n":"Girls","v":[12,15,20,7]}],"note":"Số liệu và chú giải chép đúng theo hình trong bộ đề; cô nên đối chiếu với đề gốc."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the favourite leisure activities of teenage boys and girls in Canada, comparing the percentages who prefer sports, computer games, music and shopping.\n\nOverall, the figures for boys are higher than or equal to those for girls in all four categories. The difference is greatest for shopping and sports, whereas music is equally popular with both sexes.\n\nAccording to the chart, shopping ranks first among boys, chosen by 35%, five times the proportion of girls (7%). Sports come second for boys at 27%, more than twice the figure for girls, which is 12%. Together, these two activities are the first choice of 62% of boys.\n\nThe two sexes are far closer in their liking for the other activities. Music is the favourite of exactly 20% of both boys and girls, which makes it the most popular choice among girls. Computer games attract 18% of boys and 15% of girls, a difference of only three percentage points. For girls, therefore, the order of preference is music, computer games, sports and shopping, while for boys it is almost the reverse.","lv":"in","rc":1},
{"id":"d1vrT36","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The bar chart below shows the percentage of Australian men and women in different age groups who did regular physical activity in 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Percentage of Australian men and women doing regular physical activity, 2010","unit":"%","x":["15 to 24","25 to 34","35 to 44","45 to 54","55 to 64","65 and over"],"series":[{"n":"Male","v":[52.8,42.2,39.5,43.1,45.1,46.7]},{"n":"Female","v":[47.7,48.9,52.5,53.3,53,47.1]}]},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of Australian men and women in six age groups who did regular physical activity in 2010.\n\nOverall, women were more active than men in every age group except the youngest. Male participation was lowest in early middle age, whereas among women it was highest in the middle-aged groups.\n\nIn the youngest group, aged 15 to 24, 52.8% of men exercised regularly, compared with 47.7% of women. This was the only group in which men led, and it was also the highest male figure on the chart. Male participation then dropped in the next two age groups, reaching a low of 39.5% among those aged 35 to 44, before recovering gradually to 46.7% in the oldest group.\n\nFemale participation followed the opposite pattern. It rose with age to a peak of 53.3% among women aged 45 to 54 and was almost as high in the groups on either side. The gap between the sexes was therefore widest in early middle age, at 13 percentage points, while among people aged 65 and over the two figures were virtually identical, at around 47%.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 12 · Test 1"},
{"id":"d1dYd9x","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below shows how frequently people in the USA ate in fast food restaurants between 2003 and 2013. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Frequency of eating at fast food restaurants among people in the USA, 2003–2013 (% of people)","unit":"% of people","x":["Every day","Several times a week","Once a week","Once or twice a month","A few times a year","Never"],"series":[{"n":"2003","v":[4,17,31,30,13,5]},{"n":"2006","v":[3,20,33,25,15,4]},{"n":"2013","v":[3,16,28,33,15,4]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows how often people in the USA ate at fast food restaurants in 2003, 2006 and 2013.\n\nOverall, the majority of Americans ate fast food between once a week and once or twice a month in all three years, while very few ate it every day or never. Over the decade, eating fast food weekly or more often became slightly less common.\n\nIn 2003, 31% of people visited a fast food restaurant once a week and 30% did so once or twice a month. The weekly figure rose to 33% in 2006 but dropped to 28% in 2013, whereas the monthly figure moved in the opposite direction, falling to 25% before climbing to 33%, the highest share that year. Similarly, the proportion eating fast food several times a week peaked at 20% in 2006 and then declined to 16%.\n\nAt the two extremes, only 3 to 4% of respondents ate fast food daily, and roughly the same proportion never did. Those who went a few times a year made up 13% in 2003 and 15% in both later years.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 12 · Test 3"},
{"id":"d1AzI6w","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below shows the percentage of households in owned and rented accommodation in England and Wales between 1918 and 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Households owning and renting accommodation in England and Wales, 1918 to 2011 (% of households)","unit":"% of households","x":["1918","1939","1953","1961","1971","1981","1991","2001","2011"],"series":[{"n":"Households in owned accommodation","v":[23,32,32,41,50,60,68,69,64]},{"n":"Households in rented accommodation","v":[77,68,68,59,50,40,32,31,36]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of households in England and Wales living in owned and in rented accommodation in nine selected years between 1918 and 2011.\n\nOverall, home ownership rose substantially over the period, while renting declined to a similar extent. Renting was far more common until 1971, when the two were equal, after which owning became the majority arrangement.\n\nIn 1918, 77% of households rented their homes, more than three times the proportion that owned them (23%). The share of owners rose to 32% by 1939 and, after staying at that level until 1953, climbed to 41% in 1961 and exactly half in 1971, with renting falling correspondingly.\n\nFrom 1971 onwards, the positions were reversed. Ownership continued to grow, rising to 60% in 1981 and a peak of 69% in 2001, by which time fewer than a third of households were renting. The final decade saw a slight change of direction, however: the proportion of owner-occupiers dropped to 64% in 2011, whereas that of renters rose to 36%, the first increase in renting in almost a century.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 13 · Test 2"},
{"id":"d1mmo4s","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The bar chart below shows the top ten countries for the production and consumption of electricity in 2014. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Top ten countries for production and consumption of electricity, 2014 (billion kWh)","unit":"Billion kWh","x":["China","United States","Russia","Japan","India","Canada","France","Brazil","Germany","Korea, Rep."],"series":[{"n":"Production","v":[5398,4099,1057,936.2,871,618.9,561.2,530.7,526.6,485.1]},{"n":"Consumption","v":[5322,3866,1038,856.7,698.8,499.9,462.9,455.8,582.5,449.5]}]},"mo":"The bar chart ranks the ten countries that produced and consumed the most electricity in 2014, measured in billions of kilowatt-hours.\n\nOverall, China and the United States generated and used far more electricity than any other country, with a very large gap separating them from the rest. Almost every country in the list produced slightly more electricity than it consumed, the only exception being Germany.\n\nChina led the ranking, producing 5,398 billion kWh and consuming 5,322 billion. The United States came second, with production of 4,099 billion kWh and consumption of 3,866 billion. Together, these two countries produced more than all the other eight combined.\n\nRussia, in third place, generated 1,057 billion kWh, roughly a quarter of the American total, and Japan and India followed with 936.2 and 871 billion respectively. The remaining five countries, Canada, France, Brazil, Germany and South Korea, each produced between about 485 and 620 billion kWh. Germany was unusual in that its consumption, at 582.5 billion kWh, exceeded its production of 526.6 billion, whereas India and Canada had the largest surpluses relative to their output.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 13 · Test 3"},
{"id":"d1Vl91g","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below shows the results of a survey about people's coffee and tea buying and drinking habits in five Australian cities. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Coffee and tea buying and drinking habits in five cities in Australia (% of city residents)","unit":"% of city residents","x":["Sydney","Melbourne","Brisbane","Adelaide","Hobart"],"series":[{"n":"Bought fresh coffee in last 4 weeks","v":[44,42.5,34.5,34.5,38.5]},{"n":"Bought instant coffee in last 4 weeks","v":[45.5,48,52.5,50,54.5]},{"n":"Went to a café for coffee or tea in last 4 weeks","v":[61,63.5,55.5,49.5,63]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the percentage of residents in five Australian cities who had bought fresh coffee, bought instant coffee or visited a café for coffee or tea in the previous four weeks.\n\nOverall, going to a café was the most common of the three activities in every city except Adelaide, while buying fresh coffee was the least common everywhere. Instant coffee was bought by more people than fresh coffee in all five cities.\n\nCafé visits were most widespread in Melbourne and Hobart, where around 63% of residents had been to one, closely followed by Sydney at 61%. The figures were lower in Brisbane (roughly 55%) and Adelaide, where just under half of residents had visited a café, marginally fewer than had bought instant coffee.\n\nPurchases of instant coffee ranged from about 45% in Sydney to 54% in Hobart. Fresh coffee, by contrast, was most popular in Sydney and Melbourne, at 44% and 42% respectively, whereas only about 34% of people in Brisbane and Adelaide had bought it. Sydney was therefore the only city in which the two types of coffee were bought by almost equal proportions of residents.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 15 · Test 1","pn":["Sydney","Melbourne","Hobart","Brisbane","Adelaide"]},
{"id":"d1ceA9x","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below gives information about how families in one country spent their weekly income in 1968 and in 2018. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"1968 and 2018: average weekly spending by families (% of weekly income)","unit":"% of weekly income","x":["Food","Housing","Fuel and power","Clothing and footwear","Household goods","Personal goods","Transport","Leisure"],"series":[{"n":"1968","v":[35,10,6,10,8,8,8,9]},{"n":"2018","v":[17,19,4,5,8,4,14,22]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart compares the proportions of weekly income that families in one country spent on eight categories in 1968 and in 2018.\n\nOverall, the share of income devoted to food fell markedly, whereas spending on housing, transport and leisure took up considerably more of the family budget. Food was by far the largest expense in 1968, but leisure had overtaken it fifty years later.\n\nIn 1968, families spent 35% of their weekly income on food, more than three times the share for any other category. By 2018, this figure had halved to 17%. The proportions spent on clothing and footwear and on personal goods also halved, from 10% to 5% and from 8% to 4% respectively, while fuel and power declined slightly to 4%.\n\nBy contrast, leisure rose from 9% to 22%, making it the largest item in 2018. Housing almost doubled, from 10% to 19%, and transport climbed from 8% to 14%. Household goods were the only category to show no change, accounting for 8% of income in both years.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 17 · Test 3"},
{"id":"d1lZnco","tp":"bar","d":[],"q":"The chart below shows the number of households in the US by their annual income in 2007, 2011 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"bar","title":"Number of US households (in millions), by annual income","unit":"Millions","x":["2007","2011","2015"],"series":[{"n":"Less than $25,000","v":[25.3,29,28]},{"n":"$25,000–$49,999","v":[27,30,28.8]},{"n":"$50,000–$74,999","v":[21,21.2,21]},{"n":"$75,000–$99,999","v":[14.5,14,15.2]},{"n":"$100,000 or more","v":[29.5,28,33.5]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the number of households in the United States in five annual income brackets in 2007, 2011 and 2015.\n\nOverall, the two lowest and the highest income brackets contained the most households in all three years, while the second-highest bracket contained the fewest. The top bracket saw the largest growth over the period, becoming clearly the biggest group by 2015.\n\nIn 2007, 29.5 million households earned $100,000 or more, slightly ahead of the 27 million in the second-lowest bracket and the 25 million earning less than $25,000. By 2011, the two lower groups had each grown by about 3 to 4 million, overtaking the top bracket, which had slipped to 28 million. This was reversed in 2015, when the number of the richest households rose to roughly 33.5 million, while both lower-income groups declined slightly.\n\nThe middle brackets changed far less. About 21 million households fell into the third bracket in every year, and the second-highest group was consistently the smallest, at 14 to 15 million, roughly half the size of the top bracket.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 18 · Test 2"},
{"id":"d1DNRbp","tp":"table","d":["2023-06"],"q":"The table below shows the number of mobile phones and personal computers per 1,000 people in 2003 in 6 different countries. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Mobile phones and personal computers per 1,000 people, 2003","head":["Country","Mobile holders (per thousand people)","PC owners (per thousand people)"],"rows":[["Luxembourg","872","900"],["Italy","737","480"],["Singapore","684","690"],["Germany","586","452"],["South Korea","380","452"],["China","35","88"]]},"mo":"The table compares the number of mobile phone holders and personal computer owners per thousand people in six countries in 2003.\n\nOverall, Luxembourg had the highest rate of ownership of both devices and China by far the lowest. Mobile phones were more widespread than computers in Italy and Germany, whereas the opposite was true in the other four countries.\n\nIn Luxembourg, there were 872 mobile holders and 900 computer owners per thousand people. Singapore also had similar figures for the two devices, at 684 and 690 respectively. Italy ranked second for mobile phones, with 737 holders, but its figure for computers was far lower, at 480, a gap of more than 250.\n\nGermany showed the same pattern as Italy on a smaller scale, with 586 mobile holders compared with 452 computer owners. South Korea had exactly the same level of computer ownership as Germany, although only 380 people per thousand held a mobile phone. China lagged well behind in both respects, recording just 88 computer owners and 35 mobile holders per thousand, both the lowest figures in the table.","lv":"ex","pn":["Luxembourg","Singapore","Italy","Germany","South Korea","China"]},
{"id":"d1YPEfL","tp":"table","d":["2023-06"],"q":"The table below shows daily oil production in 4 countries from 2000 to 2004. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Daily oil production in four countries, 2000–2004","head":["","2000","2001","2002","2003","2004"],"rows":[["Nigeria","205,000","201,000","190,000","210,000","213,000"],["Chad","0","0","0","8,000","50,000"],["Congo","275,000","234,000","222,000","215,000","203,000"],["Somalia","5,000","8,000","17,000","21,000","50,000"]],"note":"Hình đề không ghi đơn vị; bài mẫu hiểu là thùng dầu (barrels) mỗi ngày."},"mo":"The table shows the daily oil production of four African countries, Nigeria, Chad, Congo and Somalia, between 2000 and 2004.\n\nOverall, Nigeria and Congo were by far the largest producers throughout, although Congo's output fell steadily while Nigeria's ended slightly higher. Chad and Somalia produced very little oil at first but had both expanded rapidly by the end of the period.\n\nCongo was the leading producer in 2000, at 275,000 barrels a day, well ahead of Nigeria on 205,000. Congolese output then declined every year, dropping to 203,000 barrels by 2004. Nigerian production, by contrast, dipped to a low of 190,000 barrels in 2002 before recovering to 213,000, which meant that it had overtaken Congo by the final year.\n\nThe other two countries produced far less. Somalia's output rose in each year, from just 5,000 barrels a day to 21,000 in 2003, and then more than doubled to 50,000. Chad produced no oil at all until 2003, when it extracted 8,000 barrels a day, after which its output jumped to 50,000, the same level as Somalia.","lv":"ex","pn":["Congo","Chad","Somalia","Nigeria","Congolese","Nigerian"]},
{"id":"d1Fy8tN","tp":"table","d":["2023-03"],"q":"The table shows the production of milk annually in four countries in 1990, 2000 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Annual milk production in four countries (litres)","head":["Nation","1990","2000","2010"],"rows":[["Netherlands","11,262,000","11,155,000","11,466,000"],["Australia","11,246,000","11,105,000","9,165,000"],["Tanzania","87,000","142,000","155,000"],["Guatemala","26,000","55,000","84,000"]],"note":"Hình trong bộ đề ghi số của Tanzania là '1.42.000' và '1.55.000'; ở đây hiểu là 142,000 và 155,000."},"mo":"The table shows how many litres of milk were produced each year in the Netherlands, Australia, Tanzania and Guatemala in 1990, 2000 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the Netherlands and Australia produced vastly more milk than the other two countries in all three years. Output in Tanzania and Guatemala grew strongly, whereas the Dutch figure changed little and Australian production fell.\n\nIn 1990, the Netherlands and Australia produced almost identical amounts, at roughly 11.26 and 11.25 million litres respectively. Both figures dipped slightly over the next decade, to about 11.1 million litres. After that, the two countries diverged: Dutch output recovered to 11.47 million litres in 2010, the highest figure in the table, while Australian production dropped to 9.17 million, nearly a fifth below its 2000 level.\n\nMilk production in the two developing countries was far smaller, although it rose steadily. Tanzania's output climbed from 87,000 litres in 1990 to 142,000 in 2000 and 155,000 in 2010. Guatemala started from a much lower base of 26,000 litres, but its production more than tripled over the period, reaching 84,000 litres, which was still less than 1% of the Dutch total.","lv":"ft","pn":["Dutch","Tanzania","Guatemala","Australian"]},
{"id":"d1KIixV","tp":"table","d":["2023-02"],"q":"The table describes the changes of people who went for international travel in 1990, 1995, 2000 and 2005 (million). Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"International travellers by destination area, 1990–2005 (million)","head":["Area","1990","1995","2000","2005"],"rows":[["Total","448.9","615.2","669.2","693.7"],["Africa","18.2","20.8","26.9","28.7"],["America","80.5","112.5","118.2","113.2"],["Asia and the Pacific","60.2","80.3","117.4","135.8"],["Europe","280.2","390.3","393.2","400.2"],["Middle East","9.8","11.3","13.5","15.8"]]},"mo":"The table shows how many million people travelled internationally to five regions of the world in 1990, 1995, 2000 and 2005, together with the global total.\n\nOverall, international travel grew in every region over the fifteen years, with the fastest growth in Asia and the Pacific. Europe was by far the most visited region throughout, while Africa and the Middle East attracted the fewest travellers.\n\nThe world total rose from 448.9 million in 1990 to 693.7 million in 2005, although most of this growth occurred in the first five years. Europe followed the same pattern, jumping from 280.2 million to 390.3 million by 1995 and then edging up to just over 400 million, which was still more than half of the total.\n\nAmerica was the second most popular destination until 2000, when it received 118.2 million travellers, before slipping to 113.2 million. It was overtaken by Asia and the Pacific, where the figure more than doubled, from 60.2 million to 135.8 million. Africa and the Middle East had much lower numbers, ending at 28.7 million and 15.8 million respectively, although both had grown by more than half since 1990.","lv":"ex","pn":["Europe","America","Africa","Asia"]},
{"id":"d1sdDxK","tp":"table","d":["2023-02"],"q":"The table below shows the number of motor vehicles per 1,000 inhabitants in eight countries in 1990 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Motor vehicles per 1,000 inhabitants in eight countries","head":["Country","1990","2000"],"rows":[["Bahamas","235","187"],["Brazil","55","136"],["Cambodia","1","1"],["Chile","68","88"],["Jordan","50","157"],["Luxembourg","548","728"],["New Zealand","531","557"],["Romania","68","80"]],"note":"Hình trong bộ đề ghi số năm 2000 của Brazil là '13580' và Romania là '8012' (lỗi đánh máy, không thể vượt 1,000). Ở đây tạm hiểu là 136 và 80; cô nên đối chiếu với đề gốc."},"mo":"The table compares the number of motor vehicles per thousand inhabitants in eight countries in 1990 and 2000.\n\nOverall, vehicle ownership was by far the highest in Luxembourg and New Zealand and the lowest in Cambodia in both years. Ownership rose in six of the eight countries, most rapidly in Jordan and Brazil, whereas it fell in the Bahamas.\n\nIn 1990, Luxembourg had 548 vehicles per thousand people and New Zealand 531, more than twice the figure for the Bahamas (235), which ranked third. Over the next decade, Luxembourg's rate climbed sharply to 728, while New Zealand's rose only slightly, to 557. The Bahamas, by contrast, was the only country to record a decline, ending at 187.\n\nThe other five countries all had fewer than 70 vehicles per thousand inhabitants in 1990. Jordan's figure more than tripled, from 50 to 157, and Brazil's grew from 55 to 136, whereas the gains in Chile and Romania were smaller, to 88 and 80 respectively. Cambodia had just one vehicle per thousand people in both years, which was the lowest rate in the table.","lv":"ft","pn":["Luxembourg","New Zealand","Jordan","Cambodia","Brazil","Chile","Romania"]},
{"id":"d1SMSYT","tp":"table","d":["2022-12"],"q":"The table below shows the number of calories per person consumed per day from three cereals (rice, wheat and maize) in four countries. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Calories per person consumed per day","head":["Country","Rice","Wheat","Maize"],"rows":[["Cambodia","1,450","18","100"],["Poland","19","860","0"],["Egypt","500","1,090","570"],["Mexico","57","275","1,085"]],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Hình ghi số gạo của Mexico là '-57' (lỗi đánh máy); ở đây hiểu là 57."},"mo":"The table shows how many calories per person are obtained each day from rice, wheat and maize in Cambodia, Poland, Egypt and Mexico.\n\nOverall, each country except Egypt depends heavily on a single cereal: rice in Cambodia, wheat in Poland and maize in Mexico. Egypt has the most varied diet and the largest total intake from the three crops.\n\nCambodians obtain 1,450 calories a day from rice, by far the largest figure in the table, whereas maize provides only 100 calories and wheat just 18. Poland shows the opposite pattern, with wheat supplying 860 calories, compared with 19 from rice and none at all from maize.\n\nIn Mexico, maize dominates, providing 1,085 calories per person, almost four times as much as wheat (275), while rice contributes only 57. Egyptians, by contrast, consume substantial amounts of all three cereals. Wheat comes first at 1,090 calories, slightly more than the Mexican maize figure, followed by maize at 570 and rice at 500, which gives a combined total of more than 2,100 calories, well above that of any other country.","lv":"ft","rc":1,"pn":["Cambodians","Poland","Egypt","Egyptians","Mexico"]},
{"id":"d1GmZHV","tp":"table","d":["2021-10","2022-08"],"q":"The table details the international tourist arrivals in millions in several countries in 2009 and 2010 and the changes (in percentages). Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"International tourist arrivals, 2009 and 2010","head":["Country","2009 (millions)","2010 (millions)","Change"],"rows":[["France","76","76","0"],["US","66","67","1.5%"],["Spain","55","56","1.8%"],["UK","55","57","3.6%"],["Italy","44","45","2.3%"],["Turkey","33","45","33%"],["China","22","34","55%"],["Germany","11","23","109%"],["Hong Kong","10","9","-11%"]],"note":"Số liệu chép theo bản đề ngày 02-10-2021. Bản ngày 27-08-2022 có vài số hơi khác (Turkey 32 → 46, Germany 10 → 24)."},"mo":"The table shows the number of international tourist arrivals, in millions, in nine destinations in 2009 and 2010, together with the percentage change between the two years.\n\nOverall, France was the most visited country in both years, while Hong Kong received the fewest tourists in 2010 and was the only destination where arrivals fell. The leading destinations saw little or no change, whereas the largest gains occurred in Germany, China and Turkey.\n\nFrance welcomed 76 million visitors in each year, followed by the US, where arrivals edged up from 66 to 67 million. Spain and the UK both received 55 million tourists in 2009, after which the UK grew slightly faster, by 3.6%, to reach 57 million. Italy also recorded a small gain, ending at 45 million.\n\nThe changes further down the table were far greater. Arrivals in Germany more than doubled, from 11 to 23 million, which was the largest increase of all at 109%. China's figure climbed by 55% to 34 million, and Turkey's by a third to 45 million, the same level as Italy. By contrast, Hong Kong lost about a tenth of its visitors, dropping from 10 to 9 million.","lv":"ft","pn":["France","Spain","Italy","China","Hong Kong","Germany","Turkey"]},
{"id":"d1M6P8F","tp":"table","d":["2018-03","2022-06"],"q":"The table below describes the number of employees and factories in England and Wales from 1851 to 1901. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Employees and factories in England and Wales, 1851–1901","head":["Year","Male employees","Female employees","Total employees","Factories"],"rows":[["1851","287,100","190,000","477,100","225"],["1861","131,780","160,000","291,780","227"],["1871","80,123","60,000","140,123","622"],["1881","76,132","50,000","126,132","721"],["1891","65,000","40,000","105,000","625"],["1901","31,000","30,000","61,000","600"]]},"mo":"The table shows the number of male and female employees and the number of factories in England and Wales at ten-year intervals between 1851 and 1901.\n\nOverall, the workforce shrank in every decade, falling to roughly an eighth of its original size, whereas the number of factories was considerably higher at the end of the period than at the beginning. Men outnumbered women in most years, although the gap had almost disappeared by 1901.\n\nAt the start of the period, there were 477,100 employees, of whom 287,100 were men and 190,000 women. Ten years later, male employment had more than halved to 131,780, while the female figure had dropped only slightly, making 1861 the only year in which women formed the majority. Both groups continued to decline, and by 1901 just 31,000 men and 30,000 women were employed, a total of 61,000.\n\nThe number of factories moved in the opposite direction. Having stayed at about 225 for the first decade, it almost tripled to 622 in the following one and reached a peak of 721 in 1881, before slipping back to 600 at the end of the period.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1Rg35E","tp":"table","d":["2022-03"],"q":"The table shows the income and expenditure of Brooklyn Hall, a building available for hire, over a period of three years. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Income and expenditure of Brooklyn Hall over three years (pounds)","head":["","Year 1","Year 2","Year 3"],"rows":[["Hire of rooms","32,000","34,000","33,000"],["Café","3,000","4,000","6,000"],["Funding from the council","20,000","20,000","15,000"],["Funding from other bodies","22,000","20,000","20,000"],["Total income","72,000","77,000","78,000"],["Expenditure","63,000","71,000","77,000"],["Profit","9,000","6,000","1,000"]],"note":"Số liệu chép đúng theo hình trong bộ đề. Dòng 'Total income' trong hình không khớp với tổng các dòng phía trên, cô nên đối chiếu với đề gốc."},"mo":"The table shows the income and expenditure of Brooklyn Hall, a building available for hire, over a period of three years.\n\nOverall, the hall's total income rose slightly, but its expenditure grew much faster, so that its profit almost disappeared. Room hire was the largest source of income throughout, while the café contributed the least.\n\nHiring out rooms brought in between 32,000 and 34,000 pounds each year. Café income, although far smaller, doubled from 3,000 to 6,000 pounds, making it the only source that grew strongly. By contrast, funding from the local council was cut from 20,000 to 15,000 pounds in the final year, and money from other bodies slipped from 22,000 to 20,000 pounds after the first year.\n\nTotal income was recorded as 72,000 pounds in year one and 78,000 in year three. Expenditure, however, increased by more than a fifth over the same period, from 63,000 to 77,000 pounds. Consequently, the profit shrank from 9,000 pounds to 6,000 and finally to just 1,000, which was only a ninth of the first-year figure.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1GlalH","tp":"table","d":["2021-11"],"q":"The two tables below show the number of international students from different source countries in Canada and the USA in two school years. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"International students in Canada and the USA, by source country","parts":[{"kind":"table","title":"Canada","head":["Source country","2002–2003","2003–2004","Change"],"rows":[["China","5,400","7,850","+45%"],["The USA","5,000","5,450","+9%"],["India","2,100","2,835","+35%"],["Total","59,870","70,004","+17%"]]},{"kind":"table","title":"USA","head":["Source country","2002–2003","2003–2004","Change"],"rows":[["India","200,000","182,000","-9%"],["China","110,000","116,600","+6%"],["Canada","31,000","33,170","+7%"],["Total","581,600","570,000","-2%"]]}]},"mo":"The two tables show how many international students from three main source countries, and in total, were studying in Canada and the USA in the academic years 2002–2003 and 2003–2004.\n\nOverall, the USA hosted far more foreign students than Canada, although its total fell slightly while Canada's grew. China was the leading source country for Canada, whereas India sent the most students to the USA.\n\nIn Canada, the total number of international students rose by 17%, from 59,870 to just over 70,000. Chinese students formed the largest group, increasing by 45% to 7,850, followed by Americans, whose numbers grew more slowly, to 5,450. India sent far fewer students, although its figure climbed by more than a third, to 2,835.\n\nThe USA had 581,600 foreign students in the first year, almost ten times the Canadian total, but this number slipped by 2% to 570,000. The decline came from India, the largest source, where the figure fell from 200,000 to 182,000. By contrast, the numbers arriving from China and Canada both rose modestly, to 116,600 and 33,170 respectively.","lv":"ft","pn":["China","India","Chinese","Canada"]},
{"id":"d1eWvda","tp":"table","d":["2021-10"],"q":"The table below shows information about age, average income per person and population below poverty line in three states in the USA. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Age, average income and poverty in three US states","head":["","California","Utah","Florida"],"rows":[["Aged under 18","17%","28%","16%"],["Aged over 60","13%","8%","23%"],["Average income per person ($)","23,000","17,000","22,000"],["Population below poverty line","16%","9%","12%"]]},"mo":"The table compares three American states, California, Utah and Florida, in terms of the age of their populations, average income per person and the proportion of residents living below the poverty line.\n\nOverall, Utah had the youngest population and the lowest average income, yet also the lowest poverty rate. California was the richest of the three states but had the largest share of poor residents, while Florida had by far the most elderly people.\n\nIn Utah, 28% of the population was under 18, compared with 17% in California and 16% in Florida. The pattern for older residents was reversed: almost a quarter of Floridians (23%) were over 60, nearly three times the Utah figure of 8%, with California in between at 13%.\n\nAverage income per person was similar in California and Florida, at $23,000 and $22,000 respectively, whereas in Utah it was only $17,000. Despite this, just 9% of Utah's residents lived below the poverty line, which was well under the rates of 12% in Florida and 16% in California.","lv":"ex","pn":["California","Utah","Florida"]},
{"id":"d1y8I45","tp":"table","d":["2021-07"],"q":"The table shows information about department stores and online stores in Australia in 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Department stores and online stores in Australia, 2011","head":["","Department stores","Online stores"],"rows":[["Number of businesses","67","368"],["Profit (million AUD)","807","863"],["Sales revenue (million AUD)","12,700","13,400"],["Growth","0.4%","0.6%"]]},"mo":"The table compares department stores and online stores in Australia in 2011 in terms of the number of businesses, profit, sales revenue and growth.\n\nOverall, online stores outnumbered department stores many times over and performed slightly better on every financial measure. However, an individual department store generated far more revenue and profit than a typical online business.\n\nThere were 368 online retailers in Australia in 2011, more than five times the 67 department stores. Despite this large difference, the financial results of the two sectors were similar. Online stores recorded sales of 13,400 million Australian dollars, compared with 12,700 million for department stores, a gap of only about 5%. Profits followed the same pattern, at 863 million and 807 million dollars respectively.\n\nGrowth was modest in both sectors, although online stores again had the higher figure, at 0.6%, whereas department stores grew by just 0.4%. Taken together, the figures show that the average department store was a much larger business, earning roughly 190 million dollars in sales, which was about five times the average for an online store.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1Po9UZ","tp":"table","d":["2021-02"],"q":"The table shows the export values of various products in 1997 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Export values of various products ($HK billion)","head":["","1997","2010","Change"],"rows":[["Equipment","10.3","11.6","13%"],["Telecommunications","7.9","12.7","61%"],["Clothing","6","5","-17%"],["Manufacturing","5.5","4","-27%"],["Metals","2.3","5.1","122%"],["Overall","32","38.4","20%"]],"note":"Câu hỏi in trong hình ghi '2009 and 2010' nhưng bảng ghi 1997 và 2010; ở đây theo bảng."},"mo":"The table shows the value of five categories of exports, in billions of Hong Kong dollars, in 1997 and 2010, together with the percentage change in each.\n\nOverall, the total value of exports rose by a fifth between the two years. Exports of metals and telecommunications products grew strongly, whereas those of clothing and manufactured goods declined.\n\nEquipment was the largest export category in 1997, worth 10.3 billion dollars, and it grew by 13% to 11.6 billion in 2010. It was overtaken, however, by telecommunications, whose exports climbed from 7.9 billion to 12.7 billion dollars, an increase of 61%, making it the leading category.\n\nMetals recorded the fastest growth of all. Although they were the least valuable export in 1997, at only 2.3 billion dollars, their value more than doubled to 5.1 billion, which put them ahead of both clothing and manufacturing. These two categories moved in the opposite direction: clothing exports fell from 6 billion to 5 billion dollars, while manufacturing dropped by 27% to 4 billion, the lowest figure in 2010. Total exports reached 38.4 billion dollars, compared with 32 billion thirteen years earlier.","lv":"ex","pn":["Equipment","Metals","Total"]},
{"id":"d1ArPM","tp":"table","d":["2020-10"],"q":"The table below shows a survey on the preference of different age groups in a European country on different TV programmes in 2012. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Preferred TV programmes by age group in a European country, 2012","head":["TV programme","11–15","16–20","21–25"],"rows":[["Cartoons","29%","18%","4%"],["Feature films","18%","23%","20%"],["News","6%","19%","23%"],["Soap operas","17%","12%","19%"],["TV dramas","8%","10%","13%"],["Sports","22%","18%","21%"]],"note":"Hình trong bộ đề ghi ba nhóm tuổi là '11-15, 15-20, 21-15' (lỗi đánh máy); ở đây hiểu là 11–15, 16–20 và 21–25."},"mo":"The table shows the results of a survey carried out in one European country in 2012, in which people in three age groups were asked which type of television programme they preferred.\n\nOverall, cartoons were the favourite of the youngest group but lost popularity sharply with age, whereas interest in news moved in the opposite direction. Sports and feature films were widely liked by all three groups, and TV dramas were among the least popular choices throughout.\n\nAmong those aged 11 to 15, cartoons came first with 29%, followed by sports (22%), while only 6% chose the news. In the middle group, aged 16 to 20, feature films were the most popular at 23%, and cartoons, news and sports each attracted between 18% and 19%.\n\nThe oldest respondents, aged 21 to 25, preferred the news (23%), which was almost four times the figure for the youngest group, whereas just 4% of them chose cartoons. Sports and feature films stayed at around a fifth in this group, and soap operas, at 19%, were more popular than with either younger group. TV dramas gained support gradually with age, although they never exceeded 13%.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d17qplG","tp":"table","d":["2020-08"],"q":"The table below shows the average weekly salaries of men and of women working in different occupations. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Average weekly salary by occupation, 2015","head":["Occupation","Men","Women"],"rows":[["Technicians","$1,400","$900"],["Service workers","$1,300","$950"],["Professionals","$2,000","$1,900"],["Administrative/clerical workers","$1,350","$1,000"],["Manual labourers","$1,100","$850"]]},"mo":"The table compares the average weekly salaries of men and women in five types of occupation in 2015.\n\nOverall, men earned more than women in every occupation, although the size of the difference varied widely. Professionals were the best paid of both sexes, while manual labourers received the lowest wages.\n\nMale professionals earned $2,000 a week on average, only $100 more than their female counterparts, which was the smallest gap in the table. Pay in the other occupations was considerably lower. Among men, technicians came second with $1,400, followed by administrative and clerical workers ($1,350) and service workers ($1,300), whereas manual labourers received $1,100.\n\nThe female ranking was slightly different. After professionals, the highest-paid women were administrative and clerical workers, at $1,000 a week, followed by service workers at $950. Female technicians earned $900, which was $500 less than men in the same job and the widest gap of all. Women in manual work were paid $850, less than half the salary of a female professional.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1hQ82L","tp":"table","d":["2020-02"],"q":"The table below gives information about the values of exports of kiwi fruit from New Zealand to five countries between 2010 and 2012. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Value of kiwi fruit exports from New Zealand to five countries (NZ$)","head":["Country","2010","2011","2012"],"rows":[["Japan","$271,100,000","$287,400,000","$325,300,000"],["China","$74,900,000","$84,800,000","$94,000,000"],["Mexico","$6,000,000","$2,400,000","$3,300,000"],["Russia","$968,000","$1,585,000","$2,404,000"],["Saudi Arabia","$290,000","$407,000","$82,000"]]},"mo":"The table shows the value, in New Zealand dollars, of kiwi fruit exported from New Zealand to five countries in 2010, 2011 and 2012.\n\nOverall, Japan was by far the most important market in all three years, followed by China, and exports to both grew steadily. Sales to the other three countries were tiny by comparison, with only Russia showing consistent growth.\n\nExports to Japan were worth $271.1 million in 2010 and climbed to $325.3 million two years later, an increase of about a fifth. China bought roughly a quarter as much, although its purchases also rose each year, from $74.9 million to $94 million.\n\nThe other markets were far smaller. Mexico imported kiwi fruit worth $6 million in 2010, but this figure more than halved the following year, before recovering slightly to $3.3 million. Sales to Russia, by contrast, grew rapidly, from just under $1 million to $2.4 million, which was two and a half times the original value. Saudi Arabia was the smallest market throughout: its imports rose to $407,000 in 2011 and then fell back to only $82,000.","lv":"in","pn":["Japan","China","Mexico","Russia","Saudi Arabia"]},
{"id":"d14YCDY","tp":"table","d":["2019-06","2019-07"],"q":"The table below shows the salaries of secondary/high school teachers in 2009. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Salaries of secondary/high school teachers, 2009","head":["Country","Starting","After 15 years","Maximum","Years to reach maximum salary"],"rows":[["Australia","28,000","48,000","48,000","9"],["Denmark","45,000","54,000","54,000","8"],["Luxembourg","80,000","119,000","132,000","30"],["Japan","34,000","65,000","86,000","37"],["Korea","30,000","48,000","62,000","34"]],"note":"Hình đề không ghi đơn vị tiền; bài mẫu hiểu là đô la mỗi năm."},"mo":"The table compares the salaries of secondary school teachers in five countries in 2009, showing starting pay, pay after fifteen years, maximum pay and the number of years needed to reach the top of the scale.\n\nOverall, teachers in Luxembourg earned by far the most at every stage of their careers. Teachers in Australia and Denmark reached their maximum salary much sooner than those in the other three countries, although their top salaries were the lowest.\n\nIn Luxembourg, new teachers were paid $80,000, almost twice the starting salary in Denmark ($45,000), which ranked second, and nearly three times the Australian figure of $28,000. Beginners in Japan and Korea earned $34,000 and $30,000 respectively.\n\nAfter fifteen years, salaries in Australia and Denmark had already reached their ceilings, at $48,000 and $54,000, having taken only nine and eight years to do so. By contrast, pay continued to rise in the other countries. Luxembourg's teachers moved from $119,000 to a maximum of $132,000 after 30 years, while Japanese teachers needed 37 years to reach $86,000 and Korean teachers 34 years to earn $62,000.","lv":"in","pn":["Luxembourg","Japan","Korea","Denmark","Japanese","Korean"]},
{"id":"d1guJT","tp":"table","d":["2019-07"],"q":"The table shows the cost of water in 5 cities in Australia. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Cost of water in five Australian cities","head":["City","Usage charge per kilolitre (up to 125 KL)","Usage charge per kilolitre (over 125 KL)","Average bill per household"],"rows":[["Adelaide","$0.42","$1.00","$312"],["Brisbane","$0.81","$0.84","$310"],["Melbourne","$0.78","$0.78","$253"],["Perth","$0.42","$1.50","$332"],["Sydney","$0.98","$0.98","$319"]]},"mo":"The table shows the price of water per kilolitre at two levels of consumption, together with the average household bill, in five Australian cities.\n\nOverall, Sydney charged the most for low water use, while Perth and Adelaide charged the least but raised their prices steeply for heavier use. Average bills were similar in four of the cities, with Melbourne households paying noticeably less.\n\nFor consumption of up to 125 kilolitres, Adelaide and Perth had the lowest rate, at $0.42 per kilolitre, less than half the $0.98 charged in Sydney. Brisbane ($0.81) and Melbourne ($0.78) fell between these extremes.\n\nAbove that level, the pattern was different. Sydney and Melbourne kept the same price, and Brisbane added only three cents, whereas the charge more than doubled in Adelaide, to $1.00, and more than tripled in Perth, to $1.50, which was the highest rate in the table. Perth also had the largest average bill, at $332, followed by Sydney ($319), Adelaide ($312) and Brisbane ($310). The Melbourne figure, $253, was about a fifth lower than any of the others.","lv":"in","pn":["Sydney","Perth","Adelaide","Brisbane","Melbourne"]},
{"id":"d1qk7vu","tp":"table","d":["2019-02"],"q":"The table below presents the food consumption per person weekly in a European country in 1992, 2002 and 2012. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Weekly food consumption per person in a European country (grams)","head":["Food","1992","2002","2012"],"rows":[["Vegetables","2,140","2,190","2,220"],["Meat","1,148","1,211","1,132"],["Wheat","837","920","977"],["Beans","532","546","590"],["Cheese","113","125","125"]]},"mo":"The table shows the amount of five types of food, in grams, eaten per person per week in one European country in 1992, 2002 and 2012.\n\nOverall, vegetables were consumed in by far the largest quantities and cheese in the smallest in all three years. Consumption of every food except meat was higher in 2012 than in 1992, although most of the changes were modest.\n\nPeople ate 2,140 grams of vegetables a week in 1992, nearly twice as much as meat (1,148 grams), and this figure rose gradually to 2,220 grams by 2012. Meat consumption climbed to 1,211 grams in 2002, after which it dropped to 1,132 grams, slightly below its original level.\n\nWheat recorded the largest increase of the five foods, growing from 837 grams to 920 and then 977 grams, a gain of about 17%. Beans also became more popular, rising from 532 to 590 grams over the two decades. Cheese was eaten in much smaller amounts, at 113 grams in 1992 and 125 grams in both later years, which was roughly a ninth of the figure for meat.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1SZUP","tp":"table","d":["2019-01"],"q":"The table below shows expenditures of four car companies on advertising in the UK in 2002. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"table","title":"Advertising expenditure of four car companies in the UK, 2002 (millions of dollars)","head":["","Press","Cinema","Outdoor","Radio","TV"],"rows":[["Renault","45","6","7","15","59"],["Rover","38","0","2","15","45"],["Vauxhall","8","5","7","15","65"],["Citroen","12","0","3","15","70"]],"note":"Hình trong bộ đề ghi tên hãng thứ tư là 'Cetirizine' (tên một loại thuốc, lỗi đánh máy); ở đây hiểu là Citroen."},"mo":"The table shows how much four car manufacturers, Renault, Rover, Vauxhall and Citroen, spent on five types of advertising in the UK in 2002, in millions of dollars.\n\nOverall, television was the main advertising medium for all four companies, while cinema and outdoor advertising received very little. Renault had the largest total budget, and spending on radio was identical across the four firms.\n\nCitroen devoted 70 million dollars to television, the highest single figure in the table, followed by Vauxhall with 65 million. Renault and Rover spent less on this medium, at 59 and 45 million respectively, but far more on the press, which received 45 million from Renault and 38 million from Rover. By contrast, Citroen and Vauxhall put only 12 and 8 million into newspapers and magazines.\n\nEach company spent exactly 15 million dollars on radio. Outdoor advertising cost Renault and Vauxhall 7 million each, more than twice the amounts paid by the other two. Cinema was the least used medium, with Rover and Citroen spending nothing on it, whereas Renault and Vauxhall invested 6 and 5 million.","lv":"in","pn":["Renault","Rover","Vauxhall","Citroen"]},
{"id":"d17JCn5","tp":"pie","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The two pie charts show the percentage of the pollutants entering a particular part of the ocean in 1997 and 2007. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"Pollutants entering a particular part of the ocean (%)","unit":"%","x":["Pollutants from air","Litter","Farm waste","Industrial waste","Marine","Offshore oil","Domestic sewage"],"series":[{"n":"1997","v":[30,4,4,17,22,5,18]},{"n":"2007","v":[29,0,2,9,17,14,29]}]},"mo":"The two pie charts show the percentage of pollutants from seven sources entering a particular part of the ocean in 1997 and 2007.\n\nOverall, pollutants from the air were the largest source in both years, although domestic sewage had drawn level by 2007. The shares of sewage and offshore oil grew considerably, whereas those of industrial waste and marine sources shrank, and litter disappeared altogether.\n\nIn 1997, air pollutants made up 30% of the total, followed by marine sources (22%), domestic sewage (18%) and industrial waste (17%). The other three sources were minor, with offshore oil contributing 5% and litter and farm waste 4% each.\n\nTen years later, the proportion coming from the air was almost unchanged at 29%, while domestic sewage had climbed by eleven percentage points to the same level. Offshore oil had nearly tripled its share to 14%, overtaking industrial waste, which had almost halved to 9%. Marine pollution had also declined, to 17%, and farm waste had dropped to just 2%. Litter, by contrast, no longer appeared in the chart.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d196GWG","tp":"pie","d":["2023-01"],"q":"The charts below show the percentage of time working adults spent on different activities in a particular country in 1958 and 2008. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"How working adults spent their time (%)","unit":"%","x":["Working","Sleeping","Going out (with friends/family)","Relaxing at home","Other interests or playing sports","Travel to work"],"series":[{"n":"1958","v":[33,32,19,8,6,2]},{"n":"2008","v":[42,25,6,13,8,8]}],"note":"Số liệu chép đúng theo hình trong bộ đề; các số của năm 2008 trong hình cộng lại thành 102%."},"mo":"The two pie charts show how working adults in a particular country divided their time among six activities in 1958 and 2008.\n\nOverall, working and sleeping took up the bulk of adults' time in both years. Over the fifty years, adults came to spend more of their time working, travelling to work and relaxing at home, whereas the share devoted to sleeping and, in particular, going out declined.\n\nIn 1958, work and sleep occupied almost equal shares of the day, at 33% and 32% respectively. Going out with friends or family was the third most important activity (19%), well ahead of relaxing at home (8%) and other interests or sports (6%), while travelling to work required only 2%.\n\nBy 2008, the proportion of time spent working had grown to 42%, and sleep had dropped to a quarter. Time spent going out had shrunk to 6%, less than a third of the earlier figure. By contrast, relaxing at home had risen to 13% and commuting had quadrupled to 8%, which was the same share as other interests and sports.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1hkHA0","tp":"pie","d":["2022-09"],"q":"The pie charts below show employment in the UK in 1988 and 2012, for men and women working full-time and part-time. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Employment in the UK, 1988 and 2012","parts":[{"kind":"pie","title":"Share of all workers (%)","unit":"%","x":["Men (full-time)","Women (full-time)","Men (part-time)","Women (part-time)"],"series":[{"n":"1988","v":[53,19,6,22]},{"n":"2012","v":[47,26,7,20]}]},{"kind":"table","title":"Number (in 1000s)","head":["","1988","2012"],"rows":[["Men (full-time)","12,539","13,794"],["Women (full-time)","4,482","7,696"],["Men (part-time)","1,550","2,131"],["Women (part-time)","5,268","5,979"],["Total","23,839","29,600"]]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình."},"mo":"The two pie charts show the proportions of men and women in full-time and part-time employment in the UK in 1988 and 2012, together with the number of workers in each group.\n\nOverall, the total workforce grew considerably and all four groups increased in number. Full-time male workers were the largest group in both years, although their share declined, whereas the proportion of women working full-time rose markedly.\n\nIn 1988, there were about 23.8 million workers, of whom 53% were men in full-time jobs. Female part-timers formed the second largest group (22%), slightly ahead of female full-timers (19%), while only 6% were male part-timers.\n\nBy 2012, the workforce had expanded to 29.6 million. The number of full-time female workers had risen from roughly 4.5 to 7.7 million, lifting their share to 26%, which was the largest gain of any group. Full-time male workers had also grown in number, to 13.8 million, but their share had slipped to 47%. Female part-timers had edged down to 20% of the workforce, and male part-timers were almost unchanged at 7%.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1Nush","tp":"pie","d":["2022-07"],"q":"The charts below give information about different types of waste disposed of in one country in 1960 and 2011. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"Waste disposal in one country (%)","unit":"%","x":["Paper","Green waste","Metal","Glass","Plastic","Wood","Food","Textiles","Other"],"series":[{"n":"1960","v":[25,9,8,5,8,4,12,17,12]},{"n":"2011","v":[15,9,9,5,18,8,21,11,4]}]},"mo":"The two pie charts show the proportions of different types of waste disposed of in one country in 1960 and 2011.\n\nOverall, paper and textiles made up a much smaller share of waste in 2011 than in 1960, whereas the shares of food and plastic grew substantially. The proportions of green waste, glass and metal hardly changed.\n\nIn 1960, paper was the largest category, at a quarter of all waste, followed by textiles (17%). Food and 'other' waste each represented 12%, while green waste, metal and plastic contributed between 8% and 9% apiece. Glass and wood were the smallest categories, at 5% and 4% respectively.\n\nBy 2011, food had become the leading type of waste at 21%, and plastic had more than doubled to 18%. Wood had also doubled its share, to 8%. By contrast, paper had fallen by ten percentage points to 15%, textiles had dropped to 11%, and other waste had shrunk to a third of its former level. Green waste and glass were unchanged, and metal had edged up by a single point, which left all three below a tenth of the total.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1nu5h9","tp":"pie","d":["2021-11"],"q":"The pie charts show the amount of money that a children's charity located in the USA spent and received in one year, 2016. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"A children's charity in the USA, 2016","parts":[{"kind":"pie","title":"Revenue sources (total $53,561,580)","unit":"%","x":["Donated food","Community contributions","Program revenue","Investment income","Government grants","Other income"],"series":[{"n":"Revenue","v":[86.6,10.4,2.2,0.2,0.2,0.4]}]},{"kind":"pie","title":"Expenditures (total $53,224,896)","unit":"%","x":["Program services","Fundraising","Management and general"],"series":[{"n":"Expenditure","v":[95.8,2.6,1.6]}]}]},"mo":"The two pie charts show where a children's charity in the USA obtained its money in 2016 and how that money was spent.\n\nOverall, the charity's income and spending were almost equal, at roughly fifty-three million dollars each. Donated food was by far the most important source of revenue, while nearly all expenditure went on program services.\n\nDonated food made up 86.6% of the charity's total revenue of $53,561,580. Community contributions were the second largest source, at 10.4%, and program revenue provided a further 2.2%. By contrast, the other three sources were negligible: investment income and government grants each supplied only 0.2%, and other income 0.4%.\n\nSpending was even more concentrated. Program services received 95.8% of the total expenditure of $53,224,896, which left less than 5% for running costs. Of this, fundraising took 2.6% and management and general costs 1.6%. Total spending was slightly lower than total revenue, leaving the charity with a small surplus of about $337,000 at the end of the year.","lv":"ft","pn":["Donated","Program","Community"]},
{"id":"d11Yy1","tp":"pie","d":["2020-05"],"q":"The charts give information about world forest in five different regions. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"World forest and timber in five regions (%)","unit":"%","x":["South America","Africa","Asia","Europe","North America"],"series":[{"n":"Percentage of world forest","v":[16,27,14,18,25]},{"n":"Percentage of timber","v":[23,9,18,20,30]}]},"mo":"The two pie charts show how the world's forest is divided among five regions and what share of the world's timber each region produces.\n\nOverall, Africa and North America have the largest proportions of forest, but only North America is also a leading timber producer. Africa supplies far less timber than its forest area would imply, whereas South America, Asia and Europe all produce a larger share of timber than they hold of forest.\n\nAfrica contains 27% of the world's forest, slightly more than North America (25%). Europe has 18% and South America 16%, while Asia has the smallest share, at 14%.\n\nThe pattern for timber is rather different. North America provides 30% of the total, the highest figure in either chart, followed by South America with 23%, seven percentage points above its share of forest. Europe and Asia supply 20% and 18% respectively, in each case slightly more than their forest share. Africa, by contrast, produces only 9% of the world's timber, which is just a third of its proportion of forest.","lv":"in","pn":["Africa","Europe","North America","South America","Asia"]},
{"id":"d1OZ4QB","tp":"pie","d":["2019-01"],"q":"The charts below show the percentage of volunteers by organisations in 2008 and 2014. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"Volunteers by type of organisation (%)","unit":"%","x":["Environmental","Art","Sport","Health care","Educational","Others"],"series":[{"n":"2008","v":[21,18,15,7,24,15]},{"n":"2014","v":[29,12,25,8,17,9]}]},"mo":"The two pie charts show how volunteers were distributed among six types of organisation in 2008 and 2014.\n\nOverall, environmental and sports organisations attracted a growing share of volunteers, whereas educational and arts organisations lost ground. Health care had the smallest share of the named categories in both years.\n\nIn 2008, educational organisations had the largest proportion of volunteers, at 24%, followed by environmental groups (21%) and the arts (18%). Sports organisations and the 'others' category each had 15%, while health care attracted only 7%.\n\nSix years later, environmental organisations had moved into first place with 29% of all volunteers, a gain of eight percentage points. The share of sport had grown even faster, climbing to a quarter of the total, which made it the second most popular area. By contrast, the proportion volunteering in education had dropped to 17% and the figure for the arts to 12%, a third lower than before. Health care was almost unchanged at 8%, and other organisations had shrunk to 9%.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1V0A4w","tp":"pie","d":["2018-06"],"q":"The pie charts below show the percentage shares of six types of electronic device in 2009 and 2019. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"Shares of six types of electronic device (%)","unit":"%","x":["Mobile phone","Laptop","Tablet","Desktop computer","Flat-screen TV","Conventional TV"],"series":[{"n":"2009","v":[15,20,5,18,8,34]},{"n":"2019","v":[26,12,19,12,27,4]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi nên không rõ phần trăm đo điều gì (ví dụ doanh số hay mức sở hữu); đề bài ghi trung tính theo nội dung hình. Cô nên đối chiếu với đề gốc."},"mo":"The two pie charts compare the percentage shares of six types of electronic device in 2009 and 2019.\n\nOverall, there was a clear shift away from conventional televisions and computers towards flat-screen televisions, mobile phones and tablets. Conventional TVs went from having by far the largest share to the smallest over the decade.\n\nIn 2009, conventional televisions represented 34% of the total, well ahead of laptops (20%), desktop computers (18%) and mobile phones (15%). Flat-screen TVs and tablets were the least common devices, with shares of only 8% and 5% respectively.\n\nBy 2019, the picture had changed completely. The share of conventional TVs had shrunk to just 4%, whereas flat-screen models had more than tripled to 27%, the highest figure in that year. Mobile phones were close behind at 26%, having gained eleven percentage points, and tablets had almost quadrupled to 19%. Both types of computer, by contrast, had lost ground, with laptops and desktops each making up 12% of the total, which left them in joint fourth place.","lv":"in","rc":1},
{"id":"d1q3Zdq","tp":"pie","d":[],"q":"The charts below show the proportion of holidaymakers using four different types of accommodation in three different years. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"Accommodation used by holidaymakers (%)","unit":"%","x":["Staying with family and friends","Camping","Hotels","Rented apartments"],"series":[{"n":"1988","v":[52,26,15,7]},{"n":"1998","v":[54,13,22,11]},{"n":"2008","v":[44,12,22,22]}],"note":"Hình trong bộ đề ghi Camping năm 1998 là 26%, nhưng khi đó tổng thành 113% và phần hình quạt chỉ khoảng 13%; ở đây dùng 13%. Cô nên đối chiếu với đề gốc."},"mo":"The three pie charts show the proportions of holidaymakers who used four types of accommodation in 1988, 1998 and 2008.\n\nOverall, staying with family and friends was the most common choice in all three years, although its share eventually declined. Rented apartments and hotels became more popular over the period, whereas camping lost much of its appeal.\n\nIn 1988, just over half of holidaymakers (52%) stayed with family and friends, twice the proportion who went camping (26%). Hotels were used by 15% and rented apartments by only 7%.\n\nTen years later, the figure for family and friends had edged up to 54%, while camping had halved to 13%. Hotels had grown to 22%, and rented apartments had also gained ground, reaching 11%. By 2008, the share of holidaymakers staying with relatives or friends had dropped to 44%, which was still twice as high as any other category. Rented apartments had doubled again to 22%, the same level as hotels, whose share had not changed since 1998. Camping was now the least popular option, at 12%.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1idTeO","tp":"pie","d":[],"q":"The first chart below shows how energy is used in an average Australian household. The second chart shows the greenhouse gas emissions which result from this energy use. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"Australian household energy use and greenhouse gas emissions (%)","unit":"%","x":["Heating","Water heating","Other appliances","Refrigeration","Lighting","Cooling"],"series":[{"n":"Energy use","v":[42,30,15,7,4,2]},{"n":"Greenhouse gas emissions","v":[15,32,28,14,8,3]}]},"mo":"The first pie chart shows how energy is used in an average Australian household, and the second shows the greenhouse gas emissions that result from this use.\n\nOverall, heating and water heating together consume most household energy, but only water heating is a major source of emissions. Other appliances, refrigeration and lighting produce a far larger share of emissions than their energy use would suggest.\n\nHeating consumes the most energy, at 42% of the total, yet it is responsible for only 15% of emissions. Water heating, by contrast, has a similar share in both charts, using 30% of the energy and generating 32% of the greenhouse gases, the highest figure for emissions.\n\nThe other categories use relatively little energy but pollute disproportionately. Other appliances consume 15% of household energy while producing 28% of emissions, almost twice as much. The same is true of refrigeration and lighting, which use 7% and 4% of the energy respectively but whose shares of emissions are exactly double these figures. Cooling has the smallest share in both charts, at 2% of energy and 3% of emissions.","lv":"in","src":"Cambridge IELTS 10 · Test 1"},
{"id":"d1gFD2i","tp":"pie","d":[],"q":"The charts below show the percentage of water used for different purposes in six areas of the world. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"Percentage of water used for different purposes in six areas of the world","unit":"%","x":["Industrial use","Agricultural use","Domestic use"],"series":[{"n":"North America","v":[48,39,13]},{"n":"South America","v":[10,71,19]},{"n":"Europe","v":[53,32,15]},{"n":"Africa","v":[7,84,9]},{"n":"Central Asia","v":[5,88,7]},{"n":"South East Asia","v":[12,81,7]}]},"mo":"The six pie charts show the proportions of water used for industrial, agricultural and domestic purposes in six regions of the world.\n\nOverall, agriculture consumes by far the most water in four of the six regions, whereas industry is the main user in North America and Europe. Domestic use takes the smallest or second smallest share everywhere.\n\nIn Central Asia, Africa and South East Asia, more than four-fifths of all water goes to agriculture, with figures of 88%, 84% and 81% respectively. Industrial and domestic use are correspondingly small in these regions, each taking between 5% and 12%. South America follows a similar pattern, although its agricultural share is somewhat lower, at 71%, and domestic use is higher than anywhere else, at 19%.\n\nNorth America and Europe are quite different. Industry uses roughly half of the water in both regions (48% and 53%), which is at least four times the proportion in any other part of the world. Agriculture comes second, taking 39% in North America and 32% in Europe, while households use 13% and 15%.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 11 · Test 1","pn":["North America","South America","Central Asia","Africa","Europe"]},
{"id":"d1kag4Y","tp":"pie","d":[],"q":"The charts below show the proportions of British students at one university in England who were able to speak other languages in addition to English, in 2000 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"% of British students able to speak languages other than English","unit":"%","x":["No other language","French only","German only","Spanish only","Another language","Two other languages"],"series":[{"n":"2000","v":[20,15,10,30,15,10]},{"n":"2010","v":[10,10,10,35,20,15]}]},"mo":"The two pie charts show the proportions of British students at one university in England who could speak languages other than English in 2000 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the students became more multilingual over the decade, as the share speaking no other language halved. Spanish was by far the most widely spoken additional language in both years.\n\nIn 2000, 30% of students spoke Spanish only, which was twice the proportion who spoke French only (15%). A fifth of the students knew no language other than English, while those who spoke German only or two other languages each made up 10%. The remaining 15% spoke another language.\n\nTen years later, the proportion of Spanish-only speakers had grown to 35%, whereas the share with no other language had dropped to 10%. French-only speakers had also declined, to 10%, which put them level with German speakers, whose share had not changed. By contrast, the proportions able to speak another language or two other languages had both risen by five percentage points, to 20% and 15% respectively.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 11 · Test 2","pn":["Spanish","French","German"]},
{"id":"d1SK5WF","tp":"pie","d":[],"q":"The charts below show the average percentages in typical meals of three types of nutrients, all of which may be unhealthy if eaten too much. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"Average percentages of sodium, saturated fats and added sugars in typical meals consumed in the USA","unit":"%","x":["Breakfast","Lunch","Dinner","Snacks"],"series":[{"n":"Sodium","v":[14,29,43,14]},{"n":"Saturated fat","v":[16,26,37,21]},{"n":"Added sugar","v":[16,19,23,42]}]},"mo":"The three pie charts show what proportion of the sodium, saturated fat and added sugar in the typical American diet comes from each of four meals: breakfast, lunch, dinner and snacks.\n\nOverall, dinner supplies the largest share of both sodium and saturated fat, whereas snacks are by far the main source of added sugar. Breakfast contributes the least, or close to the least, of all three nutrients.\n\nDinner provides 43% of the sodium consumed and 37% of the saturated fat. Lunch comes second in both cases, at 29% and 26% respectively. Breakfast and snacks each contribute 14% of sodium, while for saturated fat snacks are somewhat more significant than breakfast, at 21% compared with 16%.\n\nThe picture for added sugar is quite different. Snacks are responsible for 42% of the total, almost twice the share of dinner (23%), which ranks second. Lunch supplies 19% and breakfast 16%, the same proportion as it does of saturated fat. Taken together, the main meals of lunch and dinner provide well over half of the sodium and saturated fat but less than half of the added sugar.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 14 · Test 1"},
{"id":"d1A3E9T","tp":"pie","d":[],"q":"The pie charts below show units of electricity production by fuel source in Australia and France in 1980 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"pie","title":"Units of electricity by fuel source in Australia and France","unit":"units","x":["Coal","Oil","Natural gas","Hydro power","Nuclear power"],"series":[{"n":"Australia 1980 (total 100 units)","v":[50,10,20,20,0]},{"n":"Australia 2000 (total 170 units)","v":[130,2,2,36,0]},{"n":"France 1980 (total 90 units)","v":[25,20,25,5,15]},{"n":"France 2000 (total 180 units)","v":[25,25,2,2,126]}]},"mo":"The four pie charts show how many units of electricity were produced from five fuel sources in Australia and France in 1980 and 2000.\n\nOverall, total production rose in both countries, doubling in France. Australia became increasingly dependent on coal, whereas France shifted overwhelmingly to nuclear power, and natural gas declined sharply in both.\n\nIn Australia, output grew from 100 to 170 units. Coal supplied half of the total in 1980 and 130 units in 2000, more than three-quarters of the country's electricity. Hydro power also expanded, from 20 to 36 units, while natural gas and oil shrank to just 2 units each. Australia used no nuclear power in either year.\n\nIn France, production climbed from 90 to 180 units. Nuclear power, which had generated only 15 units in 1980, produced 126 in 2000, by far the largest amount. Coal stayed at 25 units and oil rose slightly, from 20 to 25. By contrast, natural gas and hydro power fell to 2 units each, having started at 25 and 5 units.","lv":"in","src":"Cambridge IELTS 7 · Test 4","pn":["Australia","France","Nuclear","Coal","Hydro"]},
{"id":"d108Yx4","tp":"mixed","d":["2023-04"],"q":"The charts below show the main reasons for study among students of different age groups and the amount of support they received from employers. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Reasons for study and employer support, by age group","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Reasons for study according to age of student (%)","unit":"%","x":["Under 26","26–29","30–39","40–49","Over 49"],"series":[{"n":"For career","v":[80,70,58,41,18]},{"n":"For interest","v":[10,15,25,41,70]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Employer support, by age group (time off and help with fees) (%)","unit":"%","x":["Under 26","26–29","30–39","40–49","Over 49"],"series":[{"n":"Employer support","v":[64,51,34,38,45]}]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The first bar chart shows the main reasons why students of different ages study, and the second shows the proportion of each age group who received support from their employers.\n\nOverall, the younger the students, the more likely they were to study for their careers, whereas older students mostly studied out of interest. Employer support was greatest for the youngest students and lowest for those in their thirties.\n\nAmong the youngest students, 80% studied for career reasons and only 10% for interest. The career figure declined steadily with age, to 70% of those aged 26 to 29 and 58% of those in their thirties, while the proportion studying for interest rose. The two reasons were equally common in the 40 to 49 age group, at just over 40% each, and among the over-49s the pattern was reversed, with 70% studying for interest, almost four times the career figure.\n\nEmployer support, in the form of time off and help with fees, was given to 64% of the youngest group, the highest proportion of all. This fell to roughly a third of students aged 30 to 39, before rising again to 45% of the oldest group.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1OgJvR","tp":"mixed","d":["2023-04"],"q":"The chart below shows the value of one country's exports in various categories during 2015 and 2016. The table shows the percentage change in each category of exports in 2016 compared with 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Export earnings (2015–2016)","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Export earnings ($ billions)","unit":"$ billions","x":["Petroleum products","Engineered goods","Gems and jewellery","Agricultural products","Textiles"],"series":[{"n":"2015","v":[61,57,43,31,25.5]},{"n":"2016","v":[63,61,41,31.5,31]}]},{"kind":"table","title":"Percentage change in values (2015–2016)","head":["Product category","Change"],"rows":[["Petroleum products","↑ 3%"],["Engineered goods","↑ 8.5%"],["Gems and jewellery","↓ 5.18%"],["Agricultural products","↑ 0.81%"],["Textiles","↑ 15.24%"]]}],"note":"Giá trị của biểu đồ cột là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the value of one country's exports in five categories in 2015 and 2016, and the table shows the percentage change in each category between the two years.\n\nOverall, export earnings rose in four of the five categories, with textiles growing the fastest, whereas gems and jewellery declined. Petroleum products and engineered goods were by far the most valuable exports in both years.\n\nPetroleum products earned about $61 billion in 2015 and $63 billion in 2016, a gain of 3%. Engineered goods were close behind and grew more quickly, by 8.5%, from roughly $57 billion to $61 billion, which narrowed the gap between the two.\n\nThe other three categories were worth considerably less. Gems and jewellery was the only one to shrink, dropping by 5.18% from $43 billion to $41 billion. Agricultural products were almost unchanged, edging up by just 0.81% to stay at around $31 billion. Textiles, by contrast, recorded the largest percentage gain, 15.24%, climbing from about $25.5 billion to $31 billion, which brought them level with agricultural products.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 14 · Test 2","pn":["Petroleum","Textiles","Gems","Engineered"]},
{"id":"d12OAy","tp":"mixed","d":["2023-02"],"q":"The first graph gives the number of passengers travelling by train in Sydney. The second graph provides information on the percentage of trains running on time. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Train travel in Sydney, 1995–2004","parts":[{"kind":"line","title":"Number of train passengers, 1995–2004 (millions)","unit":"Millions","min":220,"x":["1995","1996","1997","1998","1999","2000","2001","2002","2003","2004"],"series":[{"n":"Passengers","v":[250,259,261,265,266,280,300,280,279,279]}]},{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of trains running on time, 1995–2004","unit":"%","min":70,"x":["1995","1996","1997","1998","1999","2000","2001","2002","2003","2004"],"series":[{"n":"On time","v":[92,89,86,92,93,85,90,93,91,72]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không ghi đơn vị số hành khách; bài mẫu hiểu là triệu lượt. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The first line graph shows the number of passengers travelling by train in Sydney between 1995 and 2004, and the second shows the percentage of trains that ran on time over the same period.\n\nOverall, passenger numbers were higher at the end of the period than at the beginning, despite falling back after a peak in 2001. Punctuality fluctuated at a high level for most of the period but deteriorated sharply in the final year.\n\nThere were 250 million train passengers in 1995, and this figure rose gradually to about 266 million in 1999. It then climbed much more steeply, reaching 300 million in 2001, the highest figure on the graph, before dropping to 280 million the following year, where it stayed until 2004.\n\nPunctuality, by contrast, was more erratic. In 1995, 92% of trains ran on time, after which the figure slipped to 86% in 1997, recovered to 93% in 1999 and then dipped to a low of 85% in 2000. It returned to 93% in 2002, the same level as three years earlier, and was still above 90% in 2003. In 2004, however, it fell to just 72%, by far the lowest figure in the period.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1kQL6H","tp":"mixed","d":["2023-02"],"q":"The first chart below shows the number of train passengers from 2000 to 2009. The second chart shows the percentage of trains running on time compared with the target. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Train passengers and punctuality, 2000–2009","parts":[{"kind":"line","title":"Number of train passengers from 2000 to 2009 (billions)","unit":"Billions","x":["2000","2001","2002","2003","2004","2005","2006","2007","2008","2009"],"series":[{"n":"Passengers","v":[37,40,43,38,43,48,44.5,41,38,43]}]},{"kind":"line","title":"Percentage of trains running on time compared with target","unit":"%","min":90,"x":["2000","2002","2004","2006","2008","2009"],"series":[{"n":"Trains on time","v":[92,95,96,92,97,97]},{"n":"Target","v":[95,95,95,95,95,95]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi và không ghi đơn vị số hành khách; bài mẫu hiểu là tỉ lượt. Số liệu là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The first line graph shows the number of train passengers, in billions, between 2000 and 2009, while the second compares the percentage of trains running on time with the target over the same period.\n\nOverall, passenger numbers fluctuated without any lasting trend, ending slightly higher than they began. Punctuality also varied, falling below the target in about half of the years, although it finished the period above it.\n\nRoughly 37 billion passengers travelled by train in 2000. The figure climbed to 43 billion in 2002, dipped to 38 billion a year later and then rose to its highest level, 48 billion, in 2005. After that it declined for three years, returning to 38 billion in 2008, before recovering to about 43 billion.\n\nThe target for punctuality was 95% throughout. Only 92% of trains ran on time in 2000, but the figure improved steadily, meeting the target in 2002 and exceeding it slightly in 2004, at 96%. It then dropped back to 92% in 2006, the same level as at the start. From that point, performance improved again, reaching 97% in 2008, where it stayed in the final year.","lv":"ft","rc":1},
{"id":"d1qiaWM","tp":"mixed","d":["2023-02"],"q":"The charts below show the percentage of males and females who do housework and the average time they spend on it. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Housework done by males and females","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Percentage of males and females who do housework","unit":"%","x":["Cooking","Cleaning","Pet care","House repairs"],"series":[{"n":"Males","v":[60,40,22,18]},{"n":"Females","v":[82,62,20,8]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Average time males and females do housework (minutes)","unit":"Minutes","x":["Cooking","Cleaning","Pet care","House repairs"],"series":[{"n":"Males","v":[60,45,20,18]},{"n":"Females","v":[85,68,20,5]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The two bar charts show the percentage of males and females who do four kinds of housework and the average number of minutes they spend on each.\n\nOverall, cooking and cleaning are the most common household tasks for both sexes and also take up the most time. Females are more involved than males in these two tasks, whereas males are more likely to do house repairs and spend longer on them.\n\nCooking is done by 82% of females and 60% of males, while the figures for cleaning are 62% and 40% respectively, a gap of 22 percentage points in each case. Pet care involves about a fifth of both sexes. House repairs, by contrast, are carried out by 18% of males, more than twice the proportion of females (8%).\n\nThe time spent follows a similar pattern. Females devote 85 minutes to cooking and 68 to cleaning, compared with 60 and 45 minutes for males. Both sexes spend 20 minutes looking after pets. Males, however, give 18 minutes to repairs, which is over three times as long as females, who spend only 5 minutes.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1quy7G","tp":"mixed","d":["2022-11"],"q":"The table and charts below give information on the police budget for 2017 and 2018 in one area of Britain. The table shows where the money came from and the charts show how it was distributed. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Police budget 2017–2018 (in £m)","parts":[{"kind":"table","title":"Sources","head":["Sources","2017","2018"],"rows":[["National Government","175.5m","177.8m"],["Local Taxes","91.2m","102.3m"],["Other sources (eg grants)","38m","38.5m"],["Total","304.7m","318.6m"]]},{"kind":"pie","title":"How the money was spent (%)","unit":"%","x":["Salaries (officers and staff)","Technology","Buildings and transport"],"series":[{"n":"2017","v":[75,8,17]},{"n":"2018","v":[69,14,17]}]}]},"mo":"The table shows the sources of the police budget in one area of Britain in 2017 and 2018, and the pie charts show how the money was spent in each year.\n\nOverall, the total budget grew, with most of the extra money coming from local taxes. Salaries took by far the largest share of spending in both years, although a growing proportion went on technology.\n\nThe national government was the main source of funding, providing £175.5 million in 2017 and slightly more, £177.8 million, in 2018. Income from local taxes rose much faster, from £91.2 million to £102.3 million, whereas other sources such as grants contributed about £38 million in each year. In total, the budget grew from £304.7 million to £318.6 million.\n\nTurning to expenditure, salaries for officers and staff made up three-quarters of the total in 2017, but this share fell to 69% a year later. Spending on technology, by contrast, almost doubled, from 8% to 14%. The proportion devoted to buildings and transport was 17% in both years, which made it the second largest item.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 17 · Test 2"},
{"id":"d1Nojty","tp":"mixed","d":["2022-09"],"q":"The bar chart shows the estimated sales of jeans for two companies next year in Turkey. The pie chart shows the projected market share of the two companies in jeans at the end of next year. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Jeans in Turkey next year","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Estimated sales of jeans next year in Turkey (thousands of pairs)","unit":"Thousands of pairs","x":["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","June","July","Aug","Sept","Oct","Nov","Dec"],"series":[{"n":"Mango Co.","v":[150,125,140,150,200,260,440,500,490,500,500,600]},{"n":"Jack & Jones Co.","v":[450,250,300,330,360,400,400,400,600,600,700,900]}]},{"kind":"pie","title":"Projected market share (%)","unit":"%","x":["Mango Co.","Jack & Jones Co.","Others"],"series":[{"n":"Projected market share","v":[20,30,50]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The bar chart shows the estimated monthly sales of jeans by two companies in Turkey next year, and the pie chart shows their projected shares of the market.\n\nOverall, sales of both companies are forecast to grow over the year, with Jack & Jones selling more than Mango in almost every month. Together the two firms will hold half of the market, Jack & Jones having the larger share.\n\nMango's sales are predicted to start at about 150,000 pairs in January and to stay near that level until April. They will then climb steeply, reaching 500,000 pairs in August, when Mango will briefly outsell its rival, and finish the year at 600,000.\n\nJack & Jones will sell 450,000 pairs in January, three times the Mango figure, although its sales will drop to 250,000 the following month, the lowest level of its year. After that, sales will rise gradually to 400,000 pairs in the middle of the year and then accelerate, hitting 900,000 in December, which will be the highest figure for either company. In terms of market share, Jack & Jones is projected to have 30% and Mango 20%, while other companies will take the other 50%.","lv":"ex","rc":1,"pn":["Mango","Jack & Jones","Jack"]},
{"id":"d1AaxlE","tp":"mixed","d":["2022-08"],"q":"The line graph shows the sales of cars of three brands from 2015 to 2019. The pie chart shows car sales by type. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Car sales, 2015–2019","parts":[{"kind":"line","title":"The sales of cars from 2015 to 2019 (millions)","unit":"Millions","x":["2015","2016","2017","2018","2019"],"series":[{"n":"Brand X","v":[0.9,1.4,1.9,2.4,2.9]},{"n":"Brand Y","v":[1.9,2.4,2.7,2.2,1.8]},{"n":"Brand Z","v":[3.2,2.4,1.9,2.0,2.3]}]},{"kind":"pie","title":"Car sales by type (%)","unit":"%","x":["MPV","Hatchback city car","SUV","Sedan","Sports car"],"series":[{"n":"Car sales by type","v":[35,27,25,10,3]}]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình. Số liệu đường biểu diễn là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The line graph shows the sales of three car brands, X, Y and Z, between 2015 and 2019, and the pie chart shows how car sales were divided among five types of vehicle.\n\nOverall, sales of Brand X grew strongly and steadily, whereas those of Brands Y and Z were lower at the end of the period than at the start. MPVs, hatchback city cars and SUVs together made up the vast majority of sales.\n\nIn 2015, Brand Z was the best seller, at about 3.2 million cars, compared with 1.9 million for Brand Y and just 0.9 million for Brand X. Sales of Brand X then rose by roughly half a million every year, reaching 2.9 million in 2019, the highest figure that year. Brand Y climbed to a peak of about 2.7 million in 2017, after which it declined to 1.8 million. Brand Z, by contrast, dropped to 1.9 million in 2017 before recovering slightly to 2.3 million.\n\nTurning to vehicle types, MPVs were the most popular, at 35% of sales, followed by hatchbacks (27%) and SUVs (25%). Sedans represented 10%, while sports cars made up only 3%.","lv":"ft","rc":1,"pn":["Brand","MPVs"]},
{"id":"d1LVZlA","tp":"mixed","d":["2021-12"],"q":"The graphs below give information about computer ownership as a percentage of the population between 2002 and 2010, and by the level of education for the years 2002 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Computer ownership, 2002–2010","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Computer ownership, 2002–2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["2002","2004","2006","2008","2010"],"series":[{"n":"Computer ownership","v":[56,61,66,72,76]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Computer ownership by education level, 2002 and 2010 (%)","unit":"%","x":["No high school diploma","High school graduate","College (incomplete)","Bachelor's degree","Postgraduate qualification"],"series":[{"n":"2002","v":[15,37,55,70,78]},{"n":"2010","v":[43,66,86,90,96]}]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The first bar chart shows the percentage of the population who owned a computer between 2002 and 2010, and the second shows ownership by level of education in 2002 and 2010.\n\nOverall, computer ownership rose steadily over the period and increased at every level of education. In both years, the more educated a group was, the higher its rate of ownership, although the gap between the groups narrowed.\n\nIn 2002, 56% of the population owned a computer. This figure grew by about five percentage points every two years, reaching 76% in 2010.\n\nOwnership was closely linked to education. In 2002, only 15% of those without a high school diploma had a computer, compared with 37% of high school graduates, 55% of those with an incomplete college education and over 70% of those with degrees. By 2010, the largest gains had been made by the less educated groups: ownership had almost tripled to 43% among those without a diploma, and had climbed to 66% among high school graduates. Postgraduates were still the most likely to own a computer, at 96%, followed by holders of a bachelor's degree (90%).","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1CZhy6","tp":"mixed","d":["2021-08"],"q":"The charts below give information about the price of tickets on one airline between Sydney and Melbourne, Australia, over a two-week period in 2013. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Price of airline ticket ($), 2013","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Sydney to Melbourne ($)","unit":"$","x":["Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri","Sat","Sun"],"series":[{"n":"Week 1","v":[80,35,35,40,75,70,50]},{"n":"Week 2","v":[50,35,35,40,75,70,50]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Melbourne to Sydney ($)","unit":"$","x":["Mon","Tue","Wed","Thu","Fri","Sat","Sun"],"series":[{"n":"Week 1","v":[60,35,35,35,80,60,50]},{"n":"Week 2","v":[40,35,35,35,60,50,50]}]}]},"mo":"The two bar charts show the price of tickets on one airline for flights from Sydney to Melbourne and from Melbourne to Sydney on each day of a two-week period in 2013.\n\nOverall, tickets in both directions were cheapest from Tuesday to Thursday and most expensive on Fridays and at weekends, with Mondays also costly in the first week. Prices were generally lower in the second week, particularly for flights to Sydney.\n\nFrom Sydney to Melbourne, the highest fare was $80 on the first Monday, after which it fell to $35 on Tuesday and Wednesday and $40 on Thursday. It then jumped to $75 on Friday, before easing to $70 on Saturday and $50 on Sunday. The second week was identical, except that the Monday price was only $50.\n\nIn the opposite direction, midweek tickets cost $35 throughout. In the first week, the Friday fare was $80, the same as the highest price on the other route, while Monday and Saturday flights cost $60. A week later, Friday was still the most expensive day, although the price had dropped to $60 on that day and to $50 on Saturday, and the Monday fare had fallen by a third to $40.","lv":"ft","pn":["Sydney","Melbourne","Monday","Friday","Sunday","Prices"]},
{"id":"d1zAkGh","tp":"mixed","d":["2021-05"],"q":"The graphs below show the percentage of men and women aged 60–64 who were employed in four countries in 1970 and 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Men and women aged 60–64 in employment (%)","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"1970","unit":"%","x":["Belgium","USA","Japan","Indonesia"],"series":[{"n":"Men","v":[79,86,76,84]},{"n":"Women","v":[63,78,56,65]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"2000","unit":"%","x":["Belgium","USA","Japan","Indonesia"],"series":[{"n":"Men","v":[52,78,63,74]},{"n":"Women","v":[8,45,47,50]}]}]},"mo":"The two bar charts show the percentage of men and women aged between sixty and sixty-four who were employed in Belgium, the USA, Japan and Indonesia in 1970 and 2000.\n\nOverall, employment in this age group declined for both sexes in all four countries, and the male rate was higher than the female rate in every case. Belgium saw the steepest decline, while the USA had the highest male employment rate in both years.\n\nIn 1970, between 76% and 86% of men in this age group were in work, with the USA at the top and Japan at the bottom. The female figures were lower, ranging from 56% in Japan to 78% in the USA.\n\nBy 2000, the male rate had dropped only slightly in the USA, to 78%, and by ten to thirteen percentage points in Indonesia and Japan, whereas in Belgium it had fallen sharply to 52%. The decline among female workers was greater. Just 8% of Belgian women were still working, compared with 63% thirty years earlier, which was by far the lowest figure in either chart. In the other three countries, between 45% and 50% of women were employed, the American figure having fallen the most, by 33 percentage points.","lv":"ex","pn":["Belgium","Belgian","Japan","Indonesia","American"]},
{"id":"d1AwJhe","tp":"mixed","d":["2021-01"],"q":"The graphs below show the cinema attendance in Australia and the average cinema visits by different age groups from 1996 to 2000. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Cinema attendance in Australia, 1996–2000","parts":[{"kind":"table","title":"Cinema visits on all age groups","head":["","1996","1997","1998","1999","2000"],"rows":[["Attendance (% of population)","62%","72%","72%","70%","72%"],["Frequency (average visits)","10.3","11.1","8.3","8.2","8.3"]]},{"kind":"line","title":"Cinema visits according to age groups","unit":"Visits","x":["1996","1997","1998","1999","2000"],"series":[{"n":"14–24","v":[15,22,16,23,18]},{"n":"25–35","v":[13,21,15,21,17]},{"n":"35–49","v":[12,19,13,20,15]},{"n":"50+","v":[10,17,11,18,13]}]}],"note":"Số liệu của biểu đồ đường là số xấp xỉ."},"mo":"The table shows the percentage of Australians who went to the cinema and the average number of visits they made each year from 1996 to 2000, while the line graph breaks down cinema visits by age group.\n\nOverall, a larger proportion of the population went to the cinema at the end of the period, although the average number of visits was lower. Younger age groups were the most frequent cinema-goers in every year, and visits by all four age groups fluctuated in a similar way.\n\nIn 1996, 62% of Australians attended the cinema, a figure that rose to 72% the following year and stayed at or close to that level thereafter. The average number of visits, by contrast, climbed from 10.3 to 11.1 in 1997 but then fell to about 8.3, where it stayed.\n\nThose aged 14 to 24 made the most visits, starting at around 15, rising to 22 in 1997, dipping in 1998 and reaching a high of 23 in 1999. The two middle age groups followed the same pattern at slightly lower levels. The over-50s went least often, with their figure ranging from 10 in 1996 to 18 in 1999.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1e0Y4","tp":"mixed","d":["2021-01"],"q":"The bar charts show average house prices in England and its capital city, London, in 1995 and 2013, and compare average house prices in different areas of England in 2013. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Average house prices in England (£)","parts":[{"kind":"bar","title":"Average house prices in England and its capital, London (£)","unit":"£","x":["1995","2013"],"series":[{"n":"England","v":[130000,200000]},{"n":"London","v":[200000,420000]}]},{"kind":"bar","title":"Average house prices in other areas of England, 2013 (£)","unit":"£","x":["London","Midlands","North East","South East","South West","North West"],"series":[{"n":"2013","v":[400000,115000,100000,320000,270000,200000]}]}],"note":"Số liệu đọc từ biểu đồ cột nên là số xấp xỉ (hình trong bộ đề vẽ London khoảng 420,000 ở biểu đồ trên và 400,000 ở biểu đồ dưới)."},"mo":"The first bar chart compares average house prices in England as a whole and in London in 1995 and 2013, while the second shows average prices in six areas of England in 2013.\n\nOverall, house prices rose in both England and London over the period, but far more rapidly in the capital. In 2013, London was the most expensive area, followed by the southern regions, whereas the North East and the Midlands were the cheapest.\n\nIn 1995, an average house cost about £130,000 in England, compared with £200,000 in London. By 2013, the national figure had increased to £200,000, a gain of roughly a half, while the London price had more than doubled to around £420,000, over twice the English average.\n\nThe second chart shows wide regional differences in 2013. After London, where the average was at least £400,000, the South East was the most expensive area at £320,000, followed by the South West (£270,000). Prices in the North West were considerably lower, at £200,000, and the cheapest homes were in the Midlands and the North East, at £115,000 and £100,000 respectively, a quarter of the London figure.","lv":"ft","pn":["London","England"]},
{"id":"d1sCkd0","tp":"mixed","d":[],"q":"The table below shows the numbers of visitors to Ashdown Museum during the year before and the year after it was refurbished. The charts show the result of surveys asking visitors how satisfied they were with their visit, during the same two periods. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Ashdown Museum before and after refurbishment","parts":[{"kind":"table","title":"Total number of visitors to Ashdown Museum","head":["","Visitors"],"rows":[["During the year before refurbishment","74,000"],["During the year after refurbishment","92,000"]]},{"kind":"pie","title":"Results of surveys of visitor satisfaction (%)","unit":"%","x":["Very satisfied","Satisfied","Dissatisfied","Very dissatisfied","No response"],"series":[{"n":"Year before refurbishment","v":[15,30,40,10,5]},{"n":"Year after refurbishment","v":[35,40,15,5,5]}]}]},"mo":"The table shows how many visitors Ashdown Museum received in the year before and the year after it was refurbished, and the pie charts show how satisfied visitors were in the same two periods.\n\nOverall, the museum attracted more visitors after the refurbishment, and satisfaction improved markedly. Most visitors were dissatisfied before the work, whereas a clear majority were satisfied afterwards.\n\nVisitor numbers rose from 74,000 in the year before refurbishment to 92,000 in the year after, a gain of about a quarter. Before the work, dissatisfied visitors formed the largest group, at 40%, and a further 10% were very dissatisfied. Only 30% said they were satisfied and 15% very satisfied.\n\nAfter the refurbishment, the proportion of very satisfied visitors more than doubled to 35%, while those who were satisfied rose to 40%, which meant that three-quarters of visitors were happy with their visit. Meanwhile, the share of dissatisfied visitors dropped to 15%, less than half the earlier figure, and that of very dissatisfied visitors halved to 5%. In both years, 5% of those surveyed gave no response.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 11 · Test 4","pn":["Ashdown Museum","Ashdown"]},
{"id":"d16fLlr","tp":"mixed","d":[],"q":"The chart below shows what Anthropology graduates from one university did after finishing their undergraduate degree course. The table shows the salaries of the anthropologists in work after five years. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"multi","title":"Anthropology graduates from one university","parts":[{"kind":"pie","title":"Destination of Anthropology graduates (%)","unit":"%","x":["Full-time work","Part-time work","Part-time work + postgrad study","Full-time postgrad study","Unemployed","Not known"],"series":[{"n":"Destination","v":[52,15,5,8,12,8]}]},{"kind":"table","title":"Salaries of Anthropology graduates (after 5 years' work)","head":["Type of employment","$25,000–49,999","$50,000–74,999","$75,000–99,999","$100,000+"],"rows":[["Freelance consultants","5%","15%","40%","40%"],["Government sector","5%","15%","30%","50%"],["Private companies","10%","35%","25%","30%"]]}]},"mo":"The pie chart shows what anthropology graduates from one university did after completing their first degree, and the table shows the salaries of those working as anthropologists after five years in three types of employment.\n\nOverall, most graduates went into work, with just over half in full-time jobs. After five years, the majority of anthropologists were in the two highest salary bands, and those in the government sector and freelance consultants were better paid than those in private companies.\n\nFull-time work was the destination of 52% of graduates, more than three times the proportion in part-time work (15%). A further 5% combined part-time work with postgraduate study, while 8% studied full-time. The unemployed made up 12%, and the destination of the other 8% was not known.\n\nRegarding salaries, half of those in the government sector earned $100,000 or more, compared with 40% of freelance consultants and 30% of private-sector employees. Taking the two highest salary bands together, 80% of both freelance and government anthropologists earned at least $75,000, whereas the figure for private companies was only 55%. The private sector also had the largest share in the lowest band, at 10%, twice the proportion in the other two groups.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 15 · Test 4"},
{"id":"d1jb2VI","tp":"map","d":["2023-07"],"q":"The plans below show a community centre 10 years ago and now. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Community centre: 10 years ago and now","maps":[{"n":"10 years ago","items":["Centre: hall (seats 100), with a storage room behind it (north)","East wing, from north to south: four offices, storage room, kitchen","West: garden (north) and car park (south)","Front (south): reception beside the entrance, cloakroom, coffee bar, toilets"]},{"n":"Now","items":["Hall (seats 100), kitchen, car park, reception, cloakroom: unchanged","West: indoor sports area in place of the garden","East wing: large café area in place of the four offices and the storage room","North of the hall: smaller storage room, with new toilets beside it","Front: office in place of the coffee bar and the old toilets"]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình."},"mo":"The two plans show the layout of a community centre ten years ago and today.\n\nOverall, the centre has been modernised to offer more space for leisure, at the cost of its garden and most of its offices. The hall, the kitchen, the car park and the reception area are the only parts that are the same as before.\n\nTen years ago, the centre was built around a hall with seating for one hundred, which had a storage room behind it. The east wing contained four offices, a second storage room and a kitchen. To the west lay a garden, with a car park to the south of it. Visitors entered through a reception at the front, next to which were a cloakroom, a coffee bar and toilets.\n\nSince then, the garden has been replaced by an indoor sports area. The four offices and the adjoining storage room have been knocked together to create a large café area, while the kitchen beside it has been retained. The storage room behind the hall has been reduced in size, making room for new toilets. At the front, the coffee bar and the old toilets have been converted into a single office, whereas the reception and cloakroom are unchanged.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1xyc9W","tp":"map","d":["2021-08","2023-05"],"q":"The diagrams below show the changes that have taken place at West Park Secondary School since its construction in 1950. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"West Park Secondary School: 1950, 1980 and 2010","maps":[{"n":"1950","items":["Main road along the north of the site","North-west: houses","North-east: school","South-west: farmland","South-east: playground"]},{"n":"1980","items":["North-west: car park and science block (in place of the houses)","North-east: school (main building)","South-west: sports field (in place of the farmland)","South-east: playground (unchanged)"]},{"n":"2010","items":["North: science block and school (main building)","South-west: car park, larger than before","South, centre: sports field, much smaller","South-east: playground, smaller"]}]},"mo":"The three diagrams show how West Park Secondary School changed between its construction in 1950 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the school expanded to take over the whole site, as the neighbouring houses and farmland were replaced by school facilities. The main building stayed in the same position throughout, while the playground and sports field ended up much smaller and parking came to occupy the largest area.\n\nIn 1950, the school stood in the north-east of the site, next to the main road, with a playground behind it. The western half was not part of the school: there were houses beside the road and farmland to the south.\n\nBy 1980, the houses had been demolished and a car park and a science block had been built in their place. The farmland had been turned into a sports field, whereas the playground was unchanged. Thirty years later, the car park had been moved to the south-west corner, where it was considerably larger than before. This left the science block and the main building as the only features along the road. The sports field had been reduced to a small area between the new car park and the playground, which had also lost some of its space.","lv":"ex","pn":["West Park Secondary School","West"]},
{"id":"d1cshqC","tp":"map","d":["2021-01","2023-04"],"q":"The plans below show the changes at a small theatre in 2010 and 2012. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"A small theatre: 2010 and 2012","maps":[{"n":"2010","items":["North end: media room, directly behind the stage","Stage, with the auditorium in front of it (centre)","West side of the stage: storage room","Front (south): main entrance in the centre","Left of the entrance: admin office and ticket office","Right of the entrance: café"]},{"n":"2012","items":["North end: hall, behind the stage","Stage: wider than before; auditorium unchanged","West side: media room (where the storage was), with showers below it","East side: storage, in a new extension","Front: main entrance in the same place","Left of the entrance: admin office, with a restaurant below it","Right of the entrance: ticket office (in place of the café)"]}]},"mo":"The two plans show how a small theatre changed between 2010 and 2012.\n\nOverall, the theatre was extended on both sides, and its backstage and front-of-house areas were rearranged. The stage and the auditorium kept their central positions, although the stage was enlarged, while the café gave way to a restaurant.\n\nIn 2010, the media room lay directly behind the stage at the northern end of the building, and a storage room adjoined the stage on the western side. The auditorium occupied the centre. At the front, the admin office and the ticket office were to the left of the main entrance, and a café stood to the right.\n\nBy 2012, a hall had been built behind the stage, and the media room had been moved to the western side, where the storage room had been. Showers had been installed next to it. The storage room had been relocated to a new extension on the eastern side of the stage, which had itself been widened. At the front, the café had been removed and the ticket office had been transferred to its place, after which a restaurant was opened on the left of the entrance, just below the admin office.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1qGrNA","tp":"map","d":["2023-04"],"q":"The maps below show the changes that have taken place at Queen Mary Hospital since its construction in 1960. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Queen Mary Hospital: 1960, 1980 and 2000","maps":[{"n":"1960","items":["Main road along the north of the site","North-west: shopping centre","North-east: hospital","South-west: farmland","South-east: car park"]},{"n":"1980","items":["North-west: cancer centre (in place of the shopping centre)","North, centre: pharmacy, next to the hospital","North-east: hospital (unchanged)","South-west: nursing school (in place of the farmland)","South-east: car park (unchanged)"]},{"n":"2000","items":["West: cancer centre, now L-shaped and extended to the south","North: pharmacy and hospital (unchanged)","South, centre: nursing school, much smaller","South-east: car park, about half its former size"]}]},"mo":"The three maps show how the site of Queen Mary Hospital changed between its construction in 1960 and 2000.\n\nOverall, the site became entirely devoted to medical use, as the shopping centre and farmland that originally stood beside the hospital were replaced by health facilities. The hospital building itself was the only feature that did not change, while the cancer centre grew to become the largest structure.\n\nIn 1960, the site lay south of a main road and was divided into four roughly equal plots. The hospital occupied the north-east corner, with a car park directly behind it, whereas the western half consisted of a shopping centre next to the road and farmland to the south.\n\nBy 1980, the shopping centre had been demolished and a cancer centre and a pharmacy had been built in its place, the pharmacy adjoining the hospital. The farmland had been converted into a nursing school. Twenty years later, the cancer centre had been extended southwards into an L-shaped building, taking over most of the land used by the nursing school. The school had therefore been moved to a much smaller plot beside the car park, which had itself been reduced to about half its former size.","lv":"ft","pn":["Queen Mary Hospital","Queen"]},
{"id":"d1OqYH","tp":"map","d":["2022-05"],"q":"The maps below show the town of Pebbleton 20 years ago and now. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Pebbleton 20 years ago and now","maps":[{"n":"20 years ago","items":["Town on a peninsula, with the sea to the west and north","North-west tip: lighthouse, with the old fort just south of it","North: short row of houses","Lighthouse Lane running south from the houses to Old Fort Road (west–east)","West of Lighthouse Lane: film studio","South of Old Fort Road: playing field","River in the south; school beside Coast Road, south of the river","South-east: road bridge over the river"]},{"n":"Now","items":["Lighthouse, playing field, school, road bridge, roads: unchanged","Children's playground in place of the old fort","Community centre next to the houses","About twice as many houses, in a longer row","Tennis courts beside Lighthouse Lane","Flats in place of the film studio","New footpath running north from Coast Road, west of the playing field"]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình."},"mo":"The two maps show how the coastal town of Pebbleton has changed over the last twenty years.\n\nOverall, the town has become more residential and has gained several leisure facilities, while its road layout and natural features are largely unchanged. The old fort and the film studio have both disappeared.\n\nTwenty years ago, a lighthouse stood at the north-western tip of the peninsula, with an old fort just below it and a short row of houses to the east. Lighthouse Lane ran south from these houses to Old Fort Road, which crossed the town from west to east. A film studio lay west of the lane and a playing field south of the road. Further south, a school stood beside Coast Road, across the river, which was spanned by a road bridge in the east.\n\nSince then, the old fort has been replaced by a children's playground, and a community centre has been built next to the houses, whose number has roughly doubled. Tennis courts have been laid out beside Lighthouse Lane, and the film studio has been converted into flats. A footpath has also been created, running north from Coast Road past the playing field, whereas the lighthouse, school and bridge are as they were.","lv":"ft","rc":1,"pn":["Pebbleton","Lighthouse Lane","Old Fort Road","Coast Road","Lighthouse","Tennis"]},
{"id":"d1c6juR","tp":"map","d":["2022-04"],"q":"The maps below show the changes in an Art Centre from 2010 to today. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"An art centre: 2010 and today","maps":[{"n":"2010","items":["Main road along the north","Open-air car park north of the main building","Main building: theatre (200 seats) on the west, cinema on the east, small café at the front","South-east: three separate art galleries"]},{"n":"Today","items":["Multipurpose car park (same position)","Main building: theatre (300 seats) on the west, art gallery on the east (in place of the cinema)","Café along the front (south side) of the main building","South: new cinema with three screens","Old art galleries: gone","New access road from the main road around the east side to the art gallery"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show how an art centre has changed since 2010.\n\nOverall, the main building has been reorganised and the centre's facilities have been upgraded. The cinema and the art gallery have effectively swapped places, the theatre has gained more seats, and access by road has been improved.\n\nIn 2010, an open-air car park lay to the north of the main building, which contained a theatre with 200 seats on the western side and a cinema on the eastern side, with a small café at the front. Three separate art galleries, by contrast, stood to the south-east.\n\nSince then, the theatre has been expanded to 300 seats, and the cinema has been replaced by a single art gallery inside the main building. The café has been moved so that it now runs along the front of the building. The old galleries have been demolished, and a new cinema with three screens has been built to the south. The car park, which has been converted into a multipurpose facility, is still in its original position, while a new road has been laid from the main road around the eastern side of the site to the gallery.","lv":"ex"},
{"id":"d1YcSh","tp":"map","d":["2022-03"],"q":"The diagrams show a museum and its surroundings in 1990 and 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"A museum and its surroundings: 1990 and 2010","maps":[{"n":"1990","items":["Main road along the south","West: museum, with a garden to the north of it","Inside the museum: store room at the northern end; entrance on the east side; small shop in the south-east corner","South of the museum: cottage","Path running north from the main road past the entrance","East of the path: car park in the north, garden in the south"]},{"n":"2010","items":["Museum, store room, entrance, path, northern garden: unchanged","Shop: extended westwards, several times larger","Café in place of the cottage","East of the path: car park covering the whole area (garden removed)"]}]},"mo":"The two diagrams show a museum and its surroundings in 1990 and 2010.\n\nOverall, the museum's visitor facilities were improved over the twenty years, with a larger shop, a café and more parking. These gains came at the expense of the cottage and one of the gardens, while the layout of the museum itself changed little.\n\nIn 1990, the museum stood on the western side of the site, with a garden to the north and a cottage to the south, next to the main road. Inside, there was a store room at the northern end and a small shop in the south-east corner, near the entrance. A path ran north from the main road past the entrance. East of the path, the land was divided between a car park in the north and a second garden in the south.\n\nBy 2010, the shop had been extended westwards so that it was several times its original size, and the cottage had been converted into a café. The eastern garden had been removed and the car park had been enlarged to cover the whole area beside the path, whereas the northern garden and the store room were unchanged.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1YippH","tp":"map","d":["2022-02"],"q":"The diagrams below show the site of a school in 2004 and the plan for changes to the school site in 2024. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"School site: 2004 (600 students) and 2024 (1,000 students)","maps":[{"n":"2004: 600 students","items":["West: car park and main entrance, reached by a road along the western edge","School Building 1 just inside the entrance","Path from Building 1 to School Building 2, further south","East of the buildings: large sports field","Trees in the north-east and across the south"]},{"n":"2024: 1,000 students","items":["West: Car Park 1 and main entrance (unchanged)","School Buildings 1 and 2 joined together","School Building 3 in the east, on the old sports field, linked by the path","Road extended around the south of the buildings to Car Park 2 in the north-east","Sports field moved to the south-east, smaller","Trees in the north and across the south"]}]},"mo":"The two diagrams show the site of a school in 2004 and the changes planned for 2024.\n\nOverall, the school will be expanded to accommodate far more students. A third building and a second car park will be added, the road will be extended and the sports field will be moved to a smaller plot.\n\nIn 2004, when the school had 600 students, the car park and main entrance were on the western side of the site. School Building 1 stood just inside the entrance, and a path led from it to School Building 2 further south. A large sports field lay to the east of the two buildings, whereas the south and north-east of the site were covered with trees. A road ran up the western edge, ending at the car park.\n\nBy 2024, with 1,000 students on the roll, the two original buildings will have been joined together, and School Building 3 will have been constructed on the land occupied by the sports field. The road will have been extended around the southern side of the buildings to a new car park in the north-east corner. The sports field will be relocated to the south-east, where it will be smaller, while most of the trees will be kept.","lv":"ex","pn":["School Building"]},
{"id":"d1gwAdD","tp":"map","d":["2022-02"],"q":"The plans below show a building and its grounds in 2010 and the changes planned for 2025. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"A building and its grounds: 2010 and 2025","maps":[{"n":"2010","items":["North-west corner: two rooms and a hall around the reception","Car park east of the hall, extending south","Large garden in the centre and south-west","Second garden in a strip along the eastern edge"]},{"n":"2025","items":["Rooms, hall and reception: unchanged","North-east: resident hall (20 rooms), on the northern part of the old car park","Car park moved to the eastern strip (in place of the garden there)","Central garden: still there, but smaller","South-west: IT centre, canteen and kitchen, two meeting rooms","South-east: game room and music room"]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi nên không rõ đây là công trình gì; đề bài ghi trung tính theo nội dung hình. Cô nên đối chiếu với đề gốc."},"mo":"The two plans show the layout of a building and its grounds in 2010 and the changes planned for 2025.\n\nOverall, the site will be developed much more intensively, with accommodation and a range of new rooms added. This will be achieved by relocating the car park and reducing the gardens, while the original rooms, hall and reception will stay where they are.\n\nIn 2010, the buildings were concentrated in the north-west corner, where two rooms and a hall surrounded a reception area. A car park lay to the east of the hall, extending south alongside a large central garden, and a second garden occupied a strip along the eastern edge of the site.\n\nBy 2025, a residents' hall with twenty rooms will have been built on the northern part of the old car park. The car park will have been moved to the eastern strip, which means that the garden there will be lost. The central garden will still exist, although its southern end will have been taken over by two groups of new rooms: an IT centre, a canteen and kitchen and two meeting rooms in the south-west, and a games room and a music room in the south-east.","lv":"ex","rc":1},
{"id":"d1eRoBj","tp":"map","d":["2021-11"],"q":"The maps below show a plan of a city in 1950 and at the present. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"A city in 1950 and now","maps":[{"n":"1950 (population 20,000)","items":["River flowing from west to east across the map","Business district on the north bank, where the major roads meet","Residential area: small, north of the business district","One bridge carrying the north–south road across the river","Government building: south of the river, in open country","Airport: far to the east, outside the city"]},{"n":"Now (population 200,000)","items":["Residential area: much larger, on both banks, reaching the airport and surrounding the government building","Business district: extended onto the south bank","Dam in the east; the central part of the river is now a lake","Second bridge, east of the original one","New major roads in the south-west","Airport and government building: same positions"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show how a city has developed between 1950 and the present.\n\nOverall, the city has grown substantially in every direction, its population having increased tenfold. The most striking changes are the spread of housing to the south of the river and the creation of a lake in the city centre.\n\nIn 1950, the city had 20,000 inhabitants and was confined to the north bank of the river. A small business district stood where the major roads met, surrounded by a residential area on its northern side. A single bridge carried the main road south across the river towards a government building, which stood in open country, whereas the airport lay some distance to the east.\n\nToday, the population is 200,000, and the residential area has been extended across the river so that it now reaches the airport and encloses the government building. The business district has also spread onto the south bank. A dam has been built in the east, turning the central stretch of the river into a lake, and a second bridge has been constructed to the east of the original one, while new roads have been added in the south-west.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d12GgsD","tp":"map","d":["2021-05"],"q":"The maps below show the changes in the art gallery ground floor in 2015 and present day. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Art gallery ground floor: 2015 and present day","maps":[{"n":"2015","items":["Entrance in the south, leading into the central entrance hall","Centre: reception desk towards the north; stairs at the far (north) end","East side, from south to north: Exhibition Rooms 1, 2 and 3","West side, from south to north: Exhibition Room 4 (the largest room), gallery office, café"]},{"n":"Present day","items":["Entrance, entrance hall, stairs: unchanged","Lift: new, next to the stairs","Reception desk: moved south, nearer the entrance","East side: Exhibition Rooms 1, 2 and 3 (unchanged)","West side, from south to north: children's area, temporary exhibition (both in place of Exhibition Room 4), vending machine (in place of the gallery office), gallery shop (in place of the café)"]}]},"mo":"The two plans show how the ground floor of an art gallery has changed since 2015.\n\nOverall, the western side of the floor has been completely reorganised, whereas the three exhibition rooms on the eastern side and the central entrance hall are unchanged. The new layout offers more facilities for visitors, including a shop, a lift and a space for children.\n\nIn 2015, visitors entered from the south into a central hall, which led past a circular reception desk to the stairs at the far end. To the right were Exhibition Rooms 1, 2 and 3. To the left, the largest room was Exhibition Room 4, beyond which were the gallery office and, in the north-west corner, a café.\n\nSince then, Exhibition Room 4 has been divided into two, with a children's area nearest the entrance and a temporary exhibition space behind it. The gallery office has been removed and a vending machine has been installed in its place, while the café has been replaced by a gallery shop. A lift has been added beside the stairs, improving access to the upper floors. The reception desk has also been moved closer to the entrance.","lv":"ft","pn":["Exhibition Room","Exhibition"]},
{"id":"d1Ts5G","tp":"map","d":["2021-04"],"q":"The maps give information about a university sports court. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"A university sports centre: 1990 and now","maps":[{"n":"1990","items":["North-west: park with trees","South-west: car park","East, north to south: café, reception, small changing room","North-east: outdoor pool","South-east: two tennis courts"]},{"n":"Now","items":["North-west: basketball court (in place of the park)","South-west: four tennis courts (in place of the car park)","East: one building containing all the facilities below","Café and reception: same positions","Indoor pool (the old outdoor pool, now covered)","Changing room: larger, south of the pool","Fitness centre: on the site of the old tennis courts"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show how a university sports centre has changed since 1990.\n\nOverall, the site has become entirely devoted to sport, as the park and the car park have been replaced by courts. The facilities on the eastern side have been brought together in a single building, which now includes an indoor pool and a fitness centre.\n\nIn 1990, the western half of the site consisted of a park with trees in the north and a car park in the south. On the eastern side, a café and a reception stood beside an outdoor pool, with a small changing room below the reception. Two tennis courts lay in the south-east corner.\n\nSince then, the park has been converted into a basketball court, and four tennis courts have been laid out where the car park used to be, doubling the original number. In the east, the pool has been covered to make it an indoor one, while the changing room has been enlarged and moved to the southern side of the pool. A fitness centre has been built on the site of the old tennis courts, whereas the café and the reception are in their original positions.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1Y6JgB","tp":"map","d":["2021-03"],"q":"The maps below show a beachfront area in Australia in 1950 and today. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"A beachfront area in Australia: 1950 and now","maps":[{"n":"1950","items":["Road from the city along the north, ending at a car park in the north-east","West of the car park, from east to west: pavilion, dining tables, playground","Two 25m pools at the edge of the beach, one in the west and one in the east","Lighthouse on the eastern point","Beach in the south"]},{"n":"Now","items":["Road extended east and then south to the lighthouse","Two car parks: the original one and a new one in place of the playground","Pavilion and dining tables: unchanged","West: restaurant and surf club near the shore","West pool lengthened to 50m; east pool still 25m","Second surf club in the east, near the lighthouse","Lighthouse and beach: unchanged"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show a beachfront area in Australia in 1950 and today.\n\nOverall, the area has been developed for visitors, with better road access, more parking and new places to eat and surf. The beach, the lighthouse, the pavilion and the dining tables are unchanged, while the playground has disappeared.\n\nIn 1950, the road from the city ended at a car park in the north-east. To the west of the car park stood a pavilion, a group of dining tables and a playground. There were two 25-metre swimming pools at the edge of the beach, one in the west and one in the east, and a lighthouse stood on the eastern point.\n\nSince then, the road has been extended eastwards and then south, so that it now reaches the lighthouse. The playground has been replaced by a second car park. In the west, a restaurant and a surf club have been built near the shore, where the pool beside them has been lengthened to 50 metres. The eastern pool is the same size as before, although another surf club has been constructed next to it, close to the lighthouse.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1ruqtj","tp":"map","d":["2021-03"],"q":"The diagrams give information about changes in a student accommodation. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Student accommodation: 2010 and now","maps":[{"n":"2010","items":["North side: living room and one student bedroom","East side: kitchen, with a student bedroom to the south of it","Centre: student bedroom and bathroom","South: hallway, with the main entrance in the south-west","Gardens to the north, north-west and south"]},{"n":"Now","items":["North side: three student bedrooms (living room converted; building extended over the north-west garden)","East side: kitchen / social area, with a student bedroom to the south of it","New en-suite room beside the south-east bedroom, in part of the hallway","Centre: student bedroom and bathroom (unchanged)","South: car parking in place of the garden","Garden to the north (unchanged)"]}]},"mo":"The two plans show how a student accommodation building has changed since 2010.\n\nOverall, the building has been altered to house more students, with the number of bedrooms rising from three to five. This has been achieved at the expense of the living room and part of the garden, while the layout of the hallway, bathroom and entrance is largely the same.\n\nIn 2010, the accommodation had a living room and one bedroom along its northern side, a kitchen and another bedroom on the east, and a third bedroom next to the bathroom in the centre. There were gardens to the north, north-west and south of the building.\n\nSince then, the living room has been converted into a bedroom, and the building has been extended over the north-western garden to create a further bedroom beside it. The kitchen has been turned into a combined kitchen and social area, which makes up for the loss of the living room. In the south-east, an en-suite room has been added to the bedroom, taking up part of the hallway. Outside, the southern garden has been replaced by a car park, whereas the garden to the north has been kept.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d18TqDq","tp":"map","d":["2021-02"],"q":"The diagrams show changes in a student common room. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Student common room: 5 years ago and now","maps":[{"n":"5 years ago","items":["Back wall, from left to right: sink, cupboard, bench, bench, microwave, refrigerator","Four round tables, each with six chairs, filling most of the floor","Entrance on the left-hand wall"]},{"n":"Now","items":["Back wall, from left to right: sink, cupboard, bench, microwave, microwave, refrigerator","Long rectangular table with eight chairs, near the entrance","Two round tables with six chairs each","Drinks machine against the right-hand wall","Bottom right corner: three armchairs facing a television","Front wall: bench for laptops"]}]},"mo":"The two diagrams show how a student common room has changed over the last five years.\n\nOverall, the room has been turned from a simple dining space into a more varied area for eating, relaxing and studying. The kitchen units along the back wall are much the same, while the seating has been almost completely rearranged.\n\nFive years ago, the furniture consisted of four round tables, each with six chairs, which filled most of the floor. Along the back wall, from left to right, were a sink, a cupboard, two benches, a microwave and a refrigerator.\n\nSince then, one of the benches has been replaced by a second microwave. Two of the round tables have been removed, and a long rectangular table with eight chairs has been placed near the entrance. A drinks machine has been installed against the right-hand wall. In the corner below it, a lounge area has been created, where three armchairs face a television. A bench for laptops has also been added along the front wall, providing a place to study, whereas the sink, cupboard and refrigerator are still in their original positions.","lv":"ft"},
{"id":"d1QETBX","tp":"map","d":["2020-08"],"q":"The maps below show changes to the ground floor plan of a university department in 2000 and 2015. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"University department: 2000 and 2015","maps":[{"n":"2000","items":["Building north of the main road; main entrance on the south side","Corridor running north from the entrance through the centre of the building","West side: seminar rooms S1 (south) and S2 (north)","East side: seminar room S3 (north), library and office","Trees to the east of the building"]},{"n":"2015","items":["Building extended to the north; longer corridor","Two new seminar rooms: S4 (north-west) and S5 (north-east)","S1, S2, S3 and the office: unchanged","Library moved to a separate building in the north-east, shared with a new IT centre","Car park east of the main entrance","Trees: gone"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show how the ground floor of a university department changed between 2000 and 2015.\n\nOverall, the department was enlarged to provide more teaching space, and the library was moved out of the main building. The trees that once stood beside the department were removed to make room for a car park and a new building.\n\nIn 2000, the building stood north of a main road and was entered from the south through a corridor that ran up its centre. There were three seminar rooms, S1 and S2 on the western side and S3 on the eastern side, where the library and an office were also situated. The land to the east was covered with trees.\n\nBy 2015, the building had been extended northwards, and two further seminar rooms, S4 and S5, had been added at the end of a longer corridor. The library had been relocated to a separate building in the north-east of the site, which it shared with a new IT centre. The office was still in its original position, while the trees had been cut down and a car park had been laid out next to the main entrance.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d15NdQM","tp":"map","d":["2020-07"],"q":"The diagrams below show changes in Felixstone in the UK between 1967 and 2001. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Felixstone: 1967 and 2001","maps":[{"n":"1967","items":["North of the main road: golf course (west), high street with shops on both sides (centre), farmland (east)","South of the main road: dunes (west), hotel and café (centre), fish market (east)","Seafront: pier extending into the sea, with a marina beside it"]},{"n":"2001","items":["Golf course: unchanged","High street: shops on the west side, apartments on the east side","North-east: hotel with swimming pool and tennis courts (in place of the farmland)","Dunes: still there, with wind turbines beside them on the shore","Original hotel, now with a car park; café unchanged","Fish market, marina and pier: gone","Seafront: public beach in the centre, private beach in the east"]}]},"mo":"The two diagrams show how the seaside town of Felixstone in the UK changed between 1967 and 2001.\n\nOverall, the town shifted from fishing and farming towards tourism and housing. The golf course, the dunes, the café and the shops on one side of the high street were the only features that survived, while the marina, pier and fish market all disappeared.\n\nIn 1967, a golf course and farmland lay on either side of the high street, which had shops along both sides. South of the main road, there were dunes in the west, a hotel and a café in the centre and a fish market in the east. A pier extended into the sea, with a marina beside it.\n\nBy 2001, the farmland had been replaced by a large hotel with a swimming pool and tennis courts, and the shops on the eastern side of the high street had been converted into apartments. On the seafront, a car park had been added next to the original hotel, and wind turbines had been erected beside the dunes. The fish market, marina and pier had been removed, after which the shore was divided into a public beach and a private beach in the east.","lv":"in","pn":["Felixstone"]},
{"id":"d10xSQX","tp":"map","d":["2020-05"],"q":"The maps show a road system as it is now and the proposed changes in future to reduce the number of accidents. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Road system: now and proposed","maps":[{"n":"Road system as it is now","items":["City Road running from west to east","Forest Road running diagonally from south-west to north-east, crossing City Road","Low Lane: from City Road (near the school) south to Forest Road, lined with houses","School north of City Road in the west","Supermarket north of City Road in the east, entered from City Road","Frequent accidents at four places: on City Road outside the school, at the southern end of Low Lane, at the City Road / Forest Road crossroads, at the supermarket entrance"]},{"n":"Proposed road system in future","items":["Roundabout in place of the City Road / Forest Road crossroads","New road from the roundabout directly to the supermarket; old entrance on City Road closed","Four sets of traffic lights on and around City Road near the school","School and houses: same positions"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show a road system as it is now and the changes that have been proposed to reduce the number of accidents.\n\nOverall, the plan focuses on the junctions where accidents frequently occur. The main changes will be a roundabout in the centre, a new access road to the supermarket and several sets of traffic lights near the school.\n\nAt present, City Road runs from west to east and is crossed by Forest Road, which runs diagonally from the south-west to the north-east. Low Lane, which is lined with houses, links City Road with Forest Road further south. A school stands north of City Road in the west, and a supermarket in the east. Four accident black spots have been identified: outside the school, at the southern end of Low Lane, at the crossroads and at the supermarket entrance.\n\nUnder the proposals, the crossroads will be replaced by a roundabout, and the supermarket will be reached by a new road leading directly from it, while the existing entrance on City Road will be closed. Traffic lights will be installed at four points on and around City Road near the school, where pupils and local traffic meet. The houses along Low Lane, by contrast, will not be affected.","lv":"in","pn":["City Road","Forest Road","Low Lane","Low","City","Forest","Traffic"]},
{"id":"d1bFmE8","tp":"map","d":["2020-05"],"q":"The maps show an art gallery in Australia in 1950 and now. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"An art gallery in Australia: 1950 and now","maps":[{"n":"1950","items":["Entrance in the south, leading into the central sculpture hall","West wing, from north to south: café, temporary exhibition, bookshop","East wing, from north to south: Early European Art, Middle European Art, Australian Art","Courtyard along the whole northern side"]},{"n":"Now","items":["Entrance and sculpture hall: unchanged","Courtyard: gone, replaced by a new row of rooms","West wing, from north to south: children's art school (new), toilet and café, temporary exhibition, bookshop","Centre, north of the sculpture hall: Australian Aboriginal Art (new)","East wing, from north to south: Australian Art (moved), Early European Art, Middle European Art, Asian Art (new)"]}]},"mo":"The two plans show the layout of an art gallery in Australia in 1950 and today.\n\nOverall, the gallery has been enlarged by building over the courtyard that once lay behind it, which has allowed new exhibition spaces and facilities to be added. The central sculpture hall, the entrance and most of the original rooms are still in the same places.\n\nIn 1950, visitors entered from the south into a central sculpture hall. The west wing contained a café, a temporary exhibition room and a bookshop, while the east wing housed three collections: early European, middle European and Australian art. An open courtyard ran along the entire northern side of the building.\n\nSince then, the courtyard has been replaced by a new row of rooms. A children's art school has been added to the west wing, where a toilet now shares a space with a smaller café. In the centre, a gallery of Australian Aboriginal art has been created behind the sculpture hall. In the east wing, Australian art has been moved to the new room at the northern end, and its former room near the entrance has been given over to Asian art, whereas the two European collections are unchanged.","lv":"in","pn":["Australian","European","Asian","Aboriginal"]},
{"id":"d1v3FPW","tp":"map","d":["2020-02"],"q":"The map below shows the changes that have taken place at the waterfront area of a town called Darwin between 2009 and 2014. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Darwin waterfront: 2009 and 2014","maps":[{"n":"2009","items":["North: school (north-west), convention centre, lake","Footpath circling the lake; small industrial area inside it, west of the lake","Three residential buildings south of the lake","South: large industrial area covering the southern half","East: swimming pool by the sea","North-east: fishing area","Sea to the south-east"]},{"n":"2014","items":["School, convention centre, lake, fishing area: unchanged","New swimming pool west of the lake (in place of the small industrial area)","Six residential buildings (three more)","University building west of the residential buildings","South: trees in place of the industrial area","New footpath across the south, leading east to a harbour","Harbour on the coast in the south-east","Original swimming pool by the sea: unchanged"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show how the waterfront area of a town called Darwin changed between 2009 and 2014.\n\nOverall, the area became greener and more residential, as its industrial land was replaced by trees, housing and leisure and educational facilities. The northern part, including the school, convention centre and lake, saw little change.\n\nIn 2009, a footpath circled a lake in the north, enclosing a small industrial area, with a school and a convention centre just outside it. Three residential buildings stood to the south of the lake, and beyond them a large industrial area covered the whole southern half of the site. There was a swimming pool by the sea in the east and a fishing area in the north-east.\n\nBy 2014, the small industrial area beside the lake had been replaced by a second swimming pool. The number of residential buildings had been doubled to six, and a university building had been constructed to the west of them. The southern industrial area had been cleared and planted with trees, while a new footpath had been laid across it from west to east, leading to a harbour that had been built on the coast. The fishing area and the original pool, by contrast, were unchanged.","lv":"in","pn":["Darwin"]},
{"id":"d1cR6yR","tp":"map","d":["2019-03"],"q":"The maps below show the changes in a town after the construction of a hydroelectric power dam. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"An area before and after a hydroelectric dam: 1990 and 2010","maps":[{"n":"1990","items":["River flowing from north to south through the centre","West bank: forest, ancient structure, rare plants and animals","East bank: irrigated farmlands beside the river, villages next to them","Mountains in the north, south-east and south-west"]},{"n":"2010","items":["North: large lake between the mountains, used for boating and fishing","Hotel on the eastern shore of the lake","Hydroelectric dam at the southern end of the lake","River flowing south from the dam","Electricity pylons along both banks of the river","Villages on the east bank, in the south","Forest, ancient structure, rare plants and animals, farmlands: gone"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show how the area around a river changed between 1990 and 2010, following the construction of a hydroelectric dam.\n\nOverall, the natural and agricultural landscape of 1990 was almost entirely replaced by a lake, a dam and power infrastructure. Only the mountains and the villages survived, while tourism appeared for the first time.\n\nIn 1990, the river flowed from north to south through the middle of the area. On its west bank stood a forest and an ancient structure, with a habitat for rare plants and animals further south. On the east bank, irrigated farmland lay beside the river, next to a group of villages, and there were mountains in the north, south-east and south-west.\n\nBy 2010, a hydroelectric dam had been built across the river between two mountains, and the land to the north had been flooded to form a large lake, which was used for boating and fishing. A hotel had been constructed on the lake's eastern shore. Below the dam, the river followed its original course, although both banks had been lined with electricity pylons. The forest, the ancient structure and the wildlife habitat had all disappeared, as had the farmland, whereas the villages were still standing on the east bank.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1jqvOt","tp":"map","d":["2019-01"],"q":"The plans below show a student room for two people and a student room for one person at an Australian university. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Student rooms at an Australian university","maps":[{"n":"Student room for two people, $350/week (6 metres × 4 metres)","items":["North-west corner: bathroom, next to the door","East end, by the windows: Bed 1 (north) and Bed 2 (south)","Study desk 1 on the north wall; study desk 2 on the south wall","Bookshelf near the bathroom","West wall: sink and stove","Centre: round table and chairs; cupboard between the table and the beds"]},{"n":"Student room for one person, $200/week (6 metres × 2.5 metres)","items":["North-west corner: bathroom, next to the door","East end, by the windows: bed","Study desk on the north wall","South wall: microwave, stove and sink; television further along","No table and chairs, cupboard or bookshelf"]}]},"mo":"The two plans compare a student room for two people with a room for one person at an Australian university.\n\nOverall, the double room is considerably larger and better equipped than the single room, although it costs less per person. Both rooms have a private bathroom, a kitchen area and a sleeping and study area beside the windows.\n\nThe room for two measures six metres by four and costs $350 a week, whereas the single room is only 2.5 metres wide and costs $200. In both, the bathroom is in the north-west corner, next to the door, and the beds have been placed at the eastern end, where the windows are. Each student has a study desk, and the double room also has a bookshelf.\n\nThe main differences are in the living space. In the double room, a sink and a stove stand against the west wall, and a round table with chairs has been provided in the centre, with a cupboard separating this area from the beds. The single room, by contrast, has no table, cupboard or bookshelf. Its kitchen, which consists of a microwave, a stove and a sink, is arranged along the south wall, while a television is mounted further along the same wall.","lv":"in"},
{"id":"d1f5GMm","tp":"map","d":["2018-09"],"q":"The maps below show the changes that have taken place in a village since 1986. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"A village on the River Stoke: 1986 and present","maps":[{"n":"1986","items":["River Stoke on the west; bridge in the north","Main road running south from the bridge, with a few houses along it","West of the road: two shops and a post office","East of the road: primary school","Farmland in the north-east and the south-west","South-east: large house surrounded by gardens"]},{"n":"Present","items":["Bridge, main road, post office: unchanged","Shops: gone, replaced by houses","Farmland: gone; new roads lined with houses in the north-east and south-west","Primary school: extended with two new wings","Large house: extended and now a retirement home","Gardens: much smaller, with houses along a new access road"]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình."},"mo":"The two maps show how a village on the River Stoke has changed since 1986.\n\nOverall, the village has grown considerably, as most of the farmland and gardens have been given over to housing. The shops have disappeared and the large house has become a retirement home, whereas the bridge, the post office and the primary school are still in place.\n\nIn 1986, the village consisted of a few houses along the main road, which ran south from the bridge. To the west of this road were two shops and a post office, and to the east stood a primary school. Farmland lay to the north-east and south-west, and a large house surrounded by gardens occupied the south-east.\n\nSince then, new roads have been built on both areas of farmland, where rows of houses now stand. The shops have been demolished to make way for more homes, although the post office has been retained. The primary school has been enlarged by the addition of two wings. The large house has also been extended and converted into a retirement home, while much of its garden has been lost to housing along a new access road.","lv":"in","rc":1,"pn":["River Stoke","Farmland"]},
{"id":"d1isGyw","tp":"map","d":["2018-04"],"q":"The maps below show a science park in 2008 and the same park today. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"A science park: 2008 and today","maps":[{"n":"2008","items":["North-east: offices and reception, beside a road along the eastern edge","West: business units","Centre: car park, linked to the buildings by paths","South: IT centre (centre) and cyber security building (south-east)","Grassland in the north-west and east of the car park","Woodland in the south-west and near the offices","Railway along the southern boundary"]},{"n":"Today","items":["North-west: research and development building (on the grassland)","North-east: offices and university hub (in place of the reception)","Business units and south-west woodland: unchanged","Car park: smaller","Cyber security building: extended northwards over the grassland","Innovation centre (the former IT centre)","Station on the railway in the south","Bus stop on the eastern road","Cycle paths alongside the eastern road and across the middle of the park"]}],"note":"Hình đề không in câu hỏi; đề bài ghi theo nội dung hình."},"mo":"The two maps show a science park in 2008 and the same park today.\n\nOverall, the park has been developed considerably, with new research and transport facilities added. Most of the grassland has been built on, while the business units and the woodland in the south-west are unchanged.\n\nIn 2008, the offices and reception stood in the north-east, beside a road that ran down the eastern edge of the site. Business units lay in the west, with an IT centre and a cyber security building in the south, all linked by paths to a central car park. Grassland covered the north-west and the area east of the car park, and a railway line passed along the southern boundary.\n\nSince then, a research and development building has been constructed on the north-western grassland, and the reception has been turned into a university hub. The car park has been reduced in size, whereas the cyber security building has been extended northwards over the other grassed area. Access has also been improved: a station has been opened on the railway, a bus stop has been placed on the eastern road, and cycle paths have been laid across the park.","lv":"in","rc":1},
{"id":"d1ZB9TR","tp":"map","d":[],"q":"The maps below show the centre of a small town called Islip as it is now, and plans for its development. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Islip town centre: now and planned development","maps":[{"n":"Now","items":["Main road running from west to east, with a row of shops on each side","Countryside to the north","South of the shops: housing, with a park on the eastern side","Further south: school and more housing","Two side roads leading south from the main road"]},{"n":"Planned development","items":["Dual carriageway forming a ring road around the centre","Main road inside the ring: pedestrians only","North side: bus station, shopping centre, car park and new housing (in place of the northern shops)","South side: row of shops kept","Housing, new housing and a smaller park south of the shops","School and housing just outside the ring road, in the south"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show the centre of a small town called Islip as it is now and as it is planned to be developed.\n\nOverall, the plan will turn the main street into a pedestrian zone enclosed by a ring road. The shops on the northern side will make way for new facilities, and additional housing will be built, whereas the school and the southern row of shops will stay as they are.\n\nAt present, a main road runs from west to east through the centre, with a row of shops on each side and countryside to the north. South of the shops lies a housing area, with a park on its eastern side, and a school and more housing lie further south.\n\nUnder the plan, a dual carriageway will have been built in a ring around the centre, and the stretch of main road inside it will be open to pedestrians only. The northern shops will have been replaced by a bus station, a shopping centre and a car park, with new housing to the east. More new housing will be built beside the park, which will be reduced in size, while the school will then stand just outside the ring road.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 12 · Test 2","pn":["Islip"]},
{"id":"d1yJPQ8","tp":"map","d":[],"q":"The two maps below show road access to a city hospital in 2007 and in 2010. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"City Hospital: 2007 and 2010","maps":[{"n":"2007","items":["City Hospital in the north, surrounded by the Ring Road","Hospital Road linking the Ring Road with City Road in the south","Three bus stops on each side of Hospital Road","Car park for staff and public, east of Hospital Road"]},{"n":"2010","items":["Hospital and Ring Road: unchanged","Roundabouts at both ends of Hospital Road (at the Ring Road and at City Road)","Bus station west of Hospital Road, linked to both roundabouts","Bus stops on Hospital Road: removed","Original car park: staff only","New public car park on the eastern side of the Ring Road"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show how road access to a city hospital changed between 2007 and 2010.\n\nOverall, access was improved by the addition of two roundabouts, a bus station and a separate car park for the public. The hospital building and its ring road were not altered.\n\nIn 2007, the hospital was surrounded by a ring road, which was linked to City Road in the south by Hospital Road. There were three bus stops on each side of Hospital Road, with a single car park to the east shared by staff and the public.\n\nBy 2010, roundabouts had been built at both ends of Hospital Road, one at its junction with the ring road and the other at its junction with City Road. A bus station had been constructed to the west of Hospital Road, connected to both roundabouts by its own access roads, whereas the bus stops along the road had been removed. The original car park had been reserved for staff, while a new public car park had been opened on the eastern side of the ring road, next to the hospital.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 13 · Test 1","pn":["City Road","Hospital Road","City Hospital","Hospital"]},
{"id":"d1mgja","tp":"map","d":[],"q":"The plans below show the layout of a university's sports centre now, and how it will look after redevelopment. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"University sports centre: present and future plans","maps":[{"n":"Present","items":["One central building, with outdoor courts to the west and to the east","Centre of the building: 25m pool","West of the pool: changing room; east of the pool: seating","North: gym","South: reception and entrance"]},{"n":"Future plans","items":["25m pool, seating, reception and entrance: unchanged","West: leisure pool (in place of the outdoor courts)","East: sports hall and two dance studios (in place of the outdoor courts)","Gym extended to the east","Two more changing rooms, in the south-west and south-east corners","Sports shop and café on either side of the reception"]}]},"mo":"The two plans show the present layout of a university sports centre and how it will look after redevelopment.\n\nOverall, the centre will be enlarged considerably, with new indoor facilities built on both sides of the existing building. The outdoor courts will disappear, whereas the central pool, gym and reception will stay in the same positions.\n\nAt present, the centre consists of a single building flanked by outdoor courts to the west and east. Inside, a 25-metre pool lies in the middle, with a changing room on its western side, seating on its eastern side and a gym to the north. Visitors enter from the south through a reception area.\n\nAfter the redevelopment, the western courts will have been replaced by a leisure pool, and the eastern courts by a sports hall and two dance studios. The gym will have been extended eastwards so that it runs above the new sports hall. Two additional changing rooms will be provided, one in each of the southern corners, while a sports shop and a café will be opened on either side of the reception.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 13 · Test 4"},
{"id":"d16aFxn","tp":"map","d":[],"q":"The plans below show a public park when it first opened in 1920 and the same park today. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Grange Park: 1920 and today","maps":[{"n":"1920","items":["Entrances on Arnold Avenue (north) and Eldon Street (south)","Centre: fountain","West: stage for musicians","Three rose gardens: north-west, north-east and south-west","North-east corner: pond for water plants","South-east: glasshouse","Seats around the edges of the park"]},{"n":"Today","items":["Both original entrances still in use","Centre: large rose garden with seats on all four sides (in place of the fountain)","West: amphitheatre for concerts (in place of the stage)","North-west rose garden: kept","North-east: café and children's play area (in place of the rose garden and the pond)","South-east: water feature (glasshouse removed)","New entrance in the east, leading to an underground car park"]}]},"mo":"The two plans show Grange Park when it opened in 1920 and as it is today.\n\nOverall, the park has been changed considerably, with facilities for children, refreshments and concerts taking the place of several of the original features. The two entrances, on Arnold Avenue to the north and Eldon Street to the south, are still in use.\n\nIn 1920, a fountain stood in the centre of the park, with a stage for musicians to its west. There were three rose gardens, two beside the northern boundary and one in the south-west, while seats were placed around the edges. A pond for water plants lay in the north-east corner, and a glasshouse stood beside Eldon Street in the south-east.\n\nSince then, the fountain has been replaced by a large rose garden with seats on all four sides, and only one of the original rose gardens has been kept. The stage has been rebuilt as an amphitheatre for concerts. In the east, the pond has given way to a children's play area with a café next to it, while the glasshouse has been removed and a water feature has been created nearby. A third entrance, which leads to an underground car park, has also been added.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 14 · Test 4","pn":["Grange Park","Arnold Avenue","Eldon Street"]},
{"id":"d1voKWV","tp":"map","d":[],"q":"The plans below show the site of an airport now and how it will look after redevelopment next year. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Southwest Airport: now and after development","maps":[{"n":"Now","items":["Two entrances at the front: departures (left) and arrivals (right)","Departures hall: check-in desk and café","Beyond the departures hall: security and passport control","Arrivals side: passport control and customs; no other services","Walkway leading to a single pier with gates 1–8"]},{"n":"After development","items":["Wider entrances for departures and arrivals","Departures hall: bag drop (new), check-in desk moved to the right, café near the entrance","Security and passport control: same position; shops added beyond them","Arrivals hall: café, ATM and car hire (car hire in a small extension)","Sky train in place of the walkway","Y-shaped pier with gates 1–18"]}]},"mo":"The two plans show Southwest Airport as it is now and as it will look after redevelopment next year.\n\nOverall, the airport will be expanded substantially, with more than twice as many gates and a wider range of facilities for passengers. The basic division between departures on the left and arrivals on the right will not change.\n\nAt present, passengers enter through separate doors for departures and arrivals. The departures hall contains a check-in desk and a café, beyond which are security and passport control. A walkway then leads to a single pier with eight gates, whereas the arrivals side has no services at all. Arriving passengers pass through passport control and customs before leaving the building.\n\nAfter the redevelopment, the single pier will have been replaced by a Y-shaped structure with eighteen gates, and the walkway by a sky train. Shops will have been added beyond security. In the departures hall, a bag drop will be installed, while the check-in desk will be moved to the right-hand side and the café to a position near the entrance. The arrivals hall will gain a café, an ATM and a car hire desk, the last of which will be housed in a small extension. Both entrances will also be enlarged.","lv":"ft","src":"Cambridge IELTS 16 · Test 3","pn":["Southwest Airport","Southwest","Shops"]},
{"id":"d1XL7tA","tp":"map","d":[],"q":"The maps below show an industrial area in the town of Norbiton, and planned future development of the site. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Norbiton industrial area: now and planned future development","maps":[{"n":"Now","items":["Town to the west; river to the north, with farmland beyond it","Main road along the south","Access road running north from the main road to a roundabout","Three factories around the roundabout","Road leading east from the roundabout, with five factories along it"]},{"n":"Planned future development","items":["All factories removed","New roundabout where the access road meets the main road","Shops and a medical centre near the central roundabout","New road north-west from the roundabout, with housing","New road north across the river by a bridge, to housing on the farmland","Eastern road kept: housing and a playground along it, school at its end"]}]},"mo":"The two maps show an industrial area in the town of Norbiton as it is now and as it is planned to be developed in the future.\n\nOverall, the area will be transformed from an industrial zone into a residential neighbourhood with its own services. All of the factories will be removed, and the development will extend north of the river onto what is now farmland.\n\nAt present, the site lies east of the town and south of a river, beyond which there is only farmland. It is reached by a road that runs north from the main road to a roundabout. Three factories stand around this roundabout, whereas five more line a road leading east from it.\n\nUnder the plan, the factories will have been replaced by housing, shops, a medical centre, a playground and a school. A second roundabout will have been built where the access road meets the main road. From the central roundabout, a new road will run north-west to more housing, while another will cross the river by a bridge to reach further homes on the farmland. The eastern road will be kept, with housing and a playground along it and the school at its end.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 17 · Test 1","pn":["Norbiton"]},
{"id":"d1Utm08","tp":"map","d":[],"q":"The diagram below shows the floor plan of a public library 20 years ago and how it looks now. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant.","ds":{"kind":"map","title":"Central Library: 20 years ago and today","maps":[{"n":"20 years ago","items":["Entrance at the front (south)","Front right: enquiry desk (tickets, book returns, etc.)","Front left: reading room (newspapers and magazines)","Centre: tables and chairs","Middle left: adult fiction books; middle right: adult non-fiction books","Far left: CDs, videos, computer games; far right: children's books"]},{"n":"Today","items":["Entrance: same place; centre now open","Front right: café; front left: computer room","Middle left: all reference books","Middle right: adult fiction books, information desk, self-service machines","Far left: children's fiction books, with two sofas and space for storytelling events","Far right: lecture room"]}]},"mo":"The two plans show the layout of the Central Library twenty years ago and today.\n\nOverall, the library has been reorganised to provide more space for events, technology and refreshment, and less for traditional reading. The entrance is in the same place, while the central area, which was once filled with tables and chairs, is now open.\n\nTwenty years ago, visitors entering the library found an enquiry desk on the right and a reading room for newspapers and magazines on the left. Beyond these, adult fiction was shelved on the left-hand side and adult non-fiction on the right. At the far end, there was a room for CDs, videos and computer games on the left and a children's book section on the right.\n\nSince then, the enquiry desk has been replaced by a café, and the reading room has been converted into a computer room. Adult fiction has been moved to the right-hand side, where an information desk and self-service machines have been installed, while the left-hand side now holds all reference books. At the far end, the children's section has been relocated to the left, where two sofas and a space for storytelling events have been added, and its former room has become a lecture room.","lv":"ex","src":"Cambridge IELTS 18 · Test 3","pn":["Central Library","Adult"]}
];
